      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Bruce Thornton (Sr. G, 18.7 PPG)** leads the Buckeyes as their primary scorer and playmaker, dangerous due to his quick first step, elite pull-up shooting (42% on mid-range jumpers), and ability to create off the dribble in pick-and-roll sets.[1][2][4] **Josh Ojianwuna (Sr. F, 14.2 PPG, 8.9 RPG)** dominates inside with his 6'10\", 270-lb frame, excelling in post-ups (55% FG in the paint) and offensive rebounding, making him a mismatch nightmare against smaller Big Ten fronts.[1][2][3] **Taison Chatman (R-So. G, 12.4 PPG)** provides perimeter threat with his 6'4\" length and 37% three-point shooting on high volume (6.2 attempts/game), lethal in spot-up situations and transition.[1][3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nOhio State plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **man-to-man defensive philosophy** focused on physicality and switching everything 1-4 to disrupt ball-handlers, anchored by their versatile frontcourt.[1][2] Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy emphasis on inside-out ball movement, ranking top-40 in **three-point shooting** at 36.8% on 20.1 attempts per game, while their **rebounding strength** (No. 22 nationally at 38.2 RPG) comes from second-chance opportunities via Ojianwuna and Brandon Noel.[1][4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17), Ohio State went 7-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season before stumbling in the Big Ten Tournament with a quarterfinal loss to Purdue (78-71).[4] They split their final four games, beating Michigan (82-76) and Indiana (69-65) but dropping road games at Wisconsin (74-68) and Rutgers (81-77), showing improved defensive clampdowns (holding foes under 70 twice) but turnover issues in crunch time.[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nThe Buckeyes excel in **physical half-court offense**, bullying opponents inside (No. 18 in 2-point FG% at 54.1%) and capitalizing on their **size advantage** (tallest Big Ten roster with two 7-footers in Ivan Njegovan and Christoph Tilly).[1][2][3] They thrive in matchups against **smaller, perimeter-oriented teams** like Villanova or Drake, where their rebounding and switchable defense force turnovers (14.2 forced per game) and limit transition.[1][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nOhio State is vulnerable to **elite perimeter defense** that sags off their guards, exposing their 28.1% turnover rate on drives, and struggles against **fast-paced, three-point bombing teams** (35-7 record when holding opponents under 35% from deep, but 0-9 when allowing 38%+).[4][5] Their bench depth is thin (top-7 players log 82% of minutes), leading to fatigue in extended tournaments.[2][3]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported beyond Taison Chatman's minor groin tweak, which he played through in the Big Ten Tournament.[3] Head coach Jake Diebler (in his second full season) has solid postseason experience as an assistant under Holtmann, including a 2024 Sweet 16 run.[5] Historically, Ohio State boasts strong March Madness success with 11 Final Fours (most recent 2007), 10 Elite Eights, and a #2 seed average in their last six appearances, thriving as double-digit seeds.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Bruce Thornton's three-point volume**: If Thornton heats up from deep (currently 34.2% on 5.8 attempts), Ohio State's offense jumps to elite levels (124.3 points per 100 when he makes 3+); cold nights (sub-30%) doom them to stagnant scoring.[1][2][4]",
