      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTCU's top three players are **Jayden Pierre** (PG, Sr., 6-0), **Jace Posey** (SG, Soph., 6-5), and **Xavier Edmonds** (PF, Jr., 6-8). Jayden Pierre averages 16.8 PPG, leading the team with elite playmaking (6.2 APG) and perimeter defense, making him dangerous as a quick guard who exploits switches and hits clutch threes (38% 3PT).[1][2][5] Jace Posey contributes 14.2 PPG on 45% FG, thriving as a versatile scorer with his size and athleticism to drive and shoot off screens, posing matchup problems for smaller defenders.[1][4] Xavier Edmonds posts 12.5 PPG and 8.1 RPG, dangerous inside with his 245-lb frame, rebounding prowess, and mobility to run in transition against slower bigs.[1][2][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTCU plays at a **moderate tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing a gritty, switchable defensive philosophy under Jamie Dixon that forces turnovers (18.2% opp. TO rate) via aggressive perimeter pressure and versatile bigs like Malick Diallo (C, Soph., 6-9).[1][4] Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with cuts, ball movement, and pick-and-rolls involving Pierre and Posey, relying on **above-average three-point shooting** (36.8%, 11.2 3PA/game) while ranking elite in rebounding strength (No. 25 nationally, 38.2 RPG led by Edmonds).[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb. 17–March 17, 2026), TCU went 8-3, including a **four-game winning streak** to close the regular season with wins over Texas (78-72) and Kansas State (85-79). They reached the Big 12 Tournament semifinals, upsetting No. 4 Baylor (82-76) before falling to Houston (71-68 OT).[2][4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nTCU excels in **defensive efficiency** (100.5, top-25 nationally) and rebounding, dominating the glass to fuel second-chance points (14.2/game) and transition buckets. They favor matchups against slow-paced, perimeter-heavy teams, where their switchable defense (featuring Lelevicius at 6-7 G) disrupts guards and their 109.2 adj. offensive efficiency shines via Pierre's drives.[1][2][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nTCU is vulnerable to elite athletic wings and athletic slashers who exploit their thinner backcourt depth, as seen in losses to fast-paced Iowa State (92-84) and Arizona State (88-76). Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) and occasional lapses in half-court creation against zone defenses trouble them against disciplined, physical squads.[4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy with Salatchoum (C, Sr., 6-10) backing up Diallo. Coach **Jamie Dixon** brings proven tournament experience (8 NCAA appearances at TCU/Pitt, including 2011 Elite Eight). TCU's March Madness history is modest (5-8 all-time, best 2017 Second Round), but their 22-11 record and No. 9 seed position them for a potential East Region upset run.[1][4][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Brock Harding** (G, Jr., transfer from Iowa): If his 12.1 PPG and defensive peskiness (1.8 SPG) elevate in March, providing secondary scoring beside Pierre, TCU could advance; otherwise, backcourt fatigue dooms them.[2][4][5]",
