      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nUCF's top three players are **Riley Kugel** (Senior Guard, 6-5, 210 lbs), **Jamichael Stillwell** (Senior Forward, 6-8, 245 lbs), and **Themus Fulks** (Fifth Year Guard, 6-2, 185 lbs). Kugel, a transfer from Mississippi State, averages 17.2 points per game with elite perimeter shooting (38% from three), making him dangerous as a **versatile scorer** who exploits mismatches in transition and off screens[1][3]. Stillwell, from Milwaukee, posts 15.8 points and 8.4 rebounds, thriving as a **physical bully** in the paint with his strength and mid-range pull-ups that punish smaller defenders[1][3]. Fulks leads at 12.9 points and 5.1 assists, excelling as a **quick-penetrating playmaker** whose speed and decision-making dismantle defenses in pick-and-roll sets[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUCF plays at a **mid-to-up-tempo pace** (around 70 possessions), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Johnny Dawkins that forces turnovers (18% opponent turnover rate) through active hands and help rotations anchored by towering centers like 7-2 John Bol and 7-0 Elijah Hulsewe[1][3]. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy reliance on three-point shooting (36% team rate, 22 attempts per game), while dominating rebounding (38.2 boards per game) via size mismatches[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), UCF went 7-3, including a **four-game winning streak** to close the regular season before a Big 12 Tournament quarterfinal loss to Kansas (78-72)[3]. They beat TCU and Oklahoma State in conference play but stumbled against elite tempo teams like Houston, showing resilience with comeback wins fueled by Kugel's late threes[3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nUCF excels in **interior dominance** and rebounding, outrebounding opponents by +6.2 per game thanks to Bol (7.1 rebounds) and Hulsewe's rim protection (2.3 blocks combined)[1][3]. They favor **matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams**, where their length disrupts passing lanes and Stillwell/Kugel feast on second-chance points (15.4 per game)[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter, allowing 37% opponent three-point shooting due to overhelping bigs leaving shooters open[3]. **High-tempo, athletic teams** like those with multiple switchable wings trouble them, as seen in losses to Baylor where they were outscored 52-38 in transition[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Bol cleared after a minor ankle tweak[1]. Coach **Johnny Dawkins** brings deep tournament experience (Stanford Final Four in 2008 as player, multiple NCAA appearances at UCF including a 2022 second-round run)[4]. UCF's March Madness history is limited (2-4 all-time, no Sweet 16), but as a #10 seed, their size gives upset potential in the East[3].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Riley Kugel's three-point volume**—if he hits 40%+ on 8+ attempts, UCF's spacing crumbles defenses; sub-30% exposes their halfcourt stagnation[1][3].",
