      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Wes Enis (G, Jr., 16.8 PPG)** leads the Bulls in scoring with his versatile perimeter game, averaging 107 three-pointers made at 36.5% while contributing 172 field goals overall; his ability to stretch defenses and score in transition makes him dangerous against slower guards.[2]  \n**Izaiyah Nelson (F, Sr., 15.7 PPG, 891 minutes)** dominates inside with a 56.2% field goal rate on 185 makes, adding elite rebounding (key in USF's board battles) and shot-blocking; his physicality in the paint overwhelms mismatches.[2][7]  \n**Joseph Pinion (G, Sr., 14.2 PPG)** provides sharpshooting volume with 106 threes at 37.9%, excelling off screens and in spot-up situations; his senior poise and 78.0% free-throw shooting clutch late in close games.[2]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nSouth Florida plays at a high tempo, averaging 87.7 points per game with strong transition scoring fueled by guard penetration.[2] Defensively, they emphasize paint protection and rebounding (elite adjusted defensive efficiency of 99.7), using versatile bigs like Nelson for help-side blocks.[1][2] Offensively, they run a balanced scheme blending drive-and-kick actions with three-point volume (team reliant on Enis and Pinion's shooting), while dominating the glass to generate second-chance points.[2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), USF went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a AAC Tournament semifinal run before falling in the final; they averaged 92 points in wins with stifling defense holding foes under 70.[2] Key results: 99-95 OT win at George Washington (11/8, but indicative of late clutch play) and dominant conference blowouts.[2]\n\n## Strengths\n\nUSF excels in rebounding dominance (led by Nelson's boards) and transition efficiency, leveraging their 113.7 adjusted offensive rating to overwhelm slower teams.[2] They favor matchups against guard-heavy or undersized fronts, where Enis/Pinion's shooting (over 37% from deep) punishes help rotations and their length disrupts pick-and-rolls.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to elite perimeter defense that sags off non-shooters, exposing occasional ball-handling lapses (higher turnovers in losses).[2] Physical, slow-tempo teams with multiple bigs trouble them by controlling pace and limiting second chances, as seen in their early 67-102 loss to Florida A&M.[2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full rotation healthy with seniors like Pinion, Nelson, and Omojafo (key contributor) leading.[1][2] Coach has solid tournament experience from prior AAC runs, though USF's March Madness history is limited—zero Sweet 16 appearances since 2012, typically first-round exits as double-digit seeds.[2]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Izaiyah Nelson's paint production**: If he exceeds 15.7 PPG and controls the glass against East region forwards, USF upsets; foul trouble or poor 71.9% FT shooting could end their run early.[2][7]",
