      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nNorth Dakota State's top three players are **Emil Skytta (G, 6-5, Jr.)**, **Noah Feddersen (F, 6-10, Jr.)**, and **Tay Smith (G, 6-2, Sr.)**. Skytta leads the team at **17.2 PPG**, dangerous with his versatile scoring, elite three-point shooting (42% on 6.5 attempts), and playmaking (4.1 APG), making him a mismatch nightmare for slower defenders. Feddersen averages **15.8 PPG and 9.2 RPG**, excelling as a physical rebounder and rim protector who dominates the paint with putbacks and post moves. Smith chips in **14.1 PPG**, lethal off the dribble with explosive drives, quick-release pull-ups, and transition scoring that exploits defensive lapses.[1][3][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nNDSU plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo (68.2 possessions per game)**, emphasizing gritty, physical defense rooted in man-to-man principles with heavy switching and help rotations to clog driving lanes—their **101.0 defensive efficiency** ranks top-50 nationally. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving Feddersen, perimeter ball movement, and selective three-point volume (36% on 22 attempts/game), paired with elite rebounding (**39.1 RPG**, led by Feddersen's dominance on the glass).[1][3][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), NDSU went **9-2**, including a **six-game winning streak** capped by a Summit League Tournament championship run: semifinal win over Oral Roberts (78-65) and title game victory against South Dakota State (72-68). They averaged 76.3 PPG in tournament play, holding opponents under 65 points.[1][3][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nNDSU excels in **defensive rebounding (29.4 per game)** and physicality, forcing 14.2 turnovers per contest while ranking top-30 in effective FG% defense. They thrive against teams lacking size or perimeter defenders, as Feddersen's paint presence and Skytta's shooting stretch floors—ideal vs. guards-heavy or slow-footed mid-majors.[1][3][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **explosive, switchable athletes** who can turn the corner on ball screens, exposing their average perimeter quickness (opponents shoot 35% from three). Poor free-throw shooting (**68.2%**) and occasional second-half droughts against teams with superior depth trouble them, as seen in losses to Kansas City and Omaha.[1][3][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the East Region. Coach **David Richman** (6th year, 112-62 record) has **two NCAA appearances** (2023 first-round exit, 2024 NIT), bringing solid mid-major tournament savvy. Historically, NDSU's best is a **2009 Sweet 16 run** as a 12-seed; recent bids ended early, but their 24-7 record and efficiencies signal upset potential as a #14 seed.[1][3][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Emil Skytta's three-point volume**—if he hits 40%+ from deep (as in conference tourney: 12-of-25), it unlocks NDSU's spacing and forces help, potentially flipping close games; cold nights doom their halfcourt offense.[1][3][5]",
