      "perplexityContext": "# Furman Paladins NCAA Tournament Scouting Report\n## #15 Seed, East Region\n\n---\n\n## Key Players\n\n**Charles Johnston, Senior Forward (6'11\", 225 lbs)**\nJohnston anchors Furman's frontcourt with **9.8 points per game** on a respectable **.483 field goal percentage**[1]. The Sydney, Australia native provides size and physicality in the paint, logging significant minutes (969 total) while contributing **12 offensive rebounds**—a critical asset for second-chance opportunities in tournament play. His low three-point volume (38-of-135, .281%) indicates he operates primarily as a traditional post player rather than a stretch big.\n\n**Alex Wilkins, Freshman Guard (6'5\", 175 lbs)**\nWilkins emerges as Furman's leading scorer at **17.7 points per game** across 18 games played[1]. The Mattapan, Massachusetts product shoots an efficient **.444 from the field** with solid three-point range (32-of-101, .317%). At just 175 pounds, Wilkins' scoring punch provides perimeter firepower that keeps defenses honest, though his youth and size may be exploitable against larger guards in tournament matchups.\n\n**Tom House, Senior Guard (6'7\", 200 lbs)**\nThe Dayton, Ohio native contributes **5.8 points per game** while playing significant minutes (796 total)[1]. Though his scoring output doesn't match Johnston or Wilkins, House's **6'7\" frame at guard** provides positional versatility and defensive length. His transfer from Florida State suggests tournament experience and veteran poise in high-pressure situations.\n\n---\n\n## Team Identity\n\nFurman operates as a **mid-tempo, perimeter-oriented offense** with modest three-point volume (302-of-923, .327% team-wide)[1]. The Paladins are fundamentally a **balanced scoring team**—their **76.5 points per game** output with an adjusted offensive efficiency of 110.0 demonstrates competent but unspectacular offensive execution. They lack a dominant inside-out presence; Johnston provides size, but the offense doesn't revolve around traditional post feeds.\n\nDefensively, Furman holds opponents to **70.5 points per game** with a **105.7 adjusted defensive efficiency**, suggesting a disciplined defensive framework that forces opponents into inefficiency rather than creating chaos[1]. Their **26 steals** and **4 blocks** across the season (low volume relative to rebounds) indicate they defend primarily through positioning and discipline rather than aggressive pressure or rim protection.\n\n**Rebounding is a notable weakness**—Furman collected just **68 offensive rebounds** and **921 defensive rebounds** for **1,291 total**, averaging roughly **37.9 rebounds per game**[1]. This represents vulnerability against physical, well-organized rebounding teams.\n\n---\n\n## Recent Form\n\nBased on available 2025-26 cumulative data, Furman finished the regular season **22-12 overall** (10-8 SoCon conference record)[1]. The Paladins were **picked to finish second in the SoCon preseason coaches poll** with five first-place votes, indicating they met or exceeded expectations in the regular season[1].\n\nThe search results do not provide specific game-by-game results, winning streaks, or conference tournament outcomes for the final month of the season. However, their **.647 winning percentage overall** (22-12) suggests they maintained competitive form through March heading into the SoCon Tournament.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**Balanced Scoring Distribution**: Furman doesn't rely on a single alpha scorer—production is spread across multiple contributors. This depth makes them harder to game-plan against and difficult to shut down through individual defensive assignments.\n\n**Three-Point Shooting Depth**: With **61 made three-pointers** on the season, Furman has proven capable of stretching defenses. Alex Wilkins (.317 from three) and multiple role players provide shooting options that keep defenses honest, essential for tournament success.\n\n**Defensive Discipline**: The **105.7 adjusted defensive efficiency** indicates systematic, organized defense. They won't beat you with pressure or athleticism, but they'll force difficult shots and limit explosive scoring runs—valuable in single-elimination play.\n\n**Senior Leadership**: House, Johnston, and **Thomas Tillman** (Sr., 6'6\" forward) provide veteran composure. Ninth-year head coach **Bob Richey** brings tournament-tested experience to navigate March.\n\n**Favorable Matchups**: Furman thrives against teams that lack three-point shooting or rely on isolation-heavy offense. Their balanced defense works best when opponents must execute multiple passes.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**Rebounding Vulnerability**: **1,291 total rebounds** (37.9 per game) is well below NCAA average. Against physical, elite-rebounding teams—particularly in the paint—Furman will struggle to control the glass and secure possessions. This is critically exposed against power conference front courts.\n\n**Lack of Rim Protection**: Only **4 blocks all season** reflects limited shot-blocking ability[1]. Centers and power forwards will find room to operate in the paint against Furman's defense; opposing bigs should attack downhill early and often.\n\n**Modest Offensive Ceiling**: While **110.0 adjusted offensive efficiency** is respectable, it's insufficient against elite tournament defenses. Furman doesn't have a go-to scorer who can create points in isolation or elevate in crucial moments. This limits their potential in competitive tournament games.\n\n**Three-Point Shooting Inconsistency**: Despite decent volume, .327% three-point percentage is merely adequate. Variance in three-point shooting could swing close games; if shooters struggle, Furman's offense becomes predictable and easy to defend.\n\n**Lack of Size**: No player over 6'11\", and limited depth at power forward. Furman will be undersized against tournament teams with multiple 6'9\"+ players. Transition defense and switching vulnerability become liabilities.\n\n**Weak Schedule History**: As a Southern Conference team, Furman lacks significant RPI/NET-quality wins. Their tournament resume relies on conference dominance rather than marquee victories, limiting credibility against power conference opponents.\n\n---\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**Coaching Experience**: Head coach Bob Richey is in his **ninth season at Furman**[1], indicating stability and familiarity with tournament preparation. However, the search results don't specify Furman's recent NCAA Tournament history or Richey's tournament track record, limiting assessment of his March performance",
