      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nFlorida's top three players are **Boogie Fland** (G, Sophomore, 11.6 PPG), **Xaivian Lee** (G, Senior, 11.5 PPG), and **Thomas Haugh** (F, Junior, 17.1 PPG). Fland dangers opponents with his playmaking (51 assists) and scoring versatility (138-317 FG, 6'3\" frame excels in transition).[5][1] Lee is a perimeter threat (51-181 3PT, .282%) and efficient finisher (132-319 FG), using his 6'4\" size for tough shots and 59 steals defensively.[5][2] Haugh dominates inside-out (175-380 FG, 56-169 3PT, 17.1 RPG) as a 6'9\" forward with elite rebounding (199 total).[5][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nFlorida plays at a high tempo, leveraging their 118.1 adjusted offensive efficiency through a motion-based offensive scheme emphasizing ball movement and inside-out play from bigs like Haugh and Rueben Chinyelu (11.5 RPG, .583 FG%). Defensively, they employ a switch-heavy philosophy (97.9 adj. DE) with length from 7'1\" Micah Handlogten and 7'9\" Olivier Rioux limiting second chances. They average moderate three-point volume (team .282-.331% from key shooters) but excel in rebounding strength (Chinyelu 379 total boards, Haugh 199), controlling paint and transition.[5][1]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month entering the tournament, Florida went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by an SEC Tournament championship win over presumed rivals, outrebounding foes by +12 per game. They dropped two close road games to top-25 teams but dominated conference play with 26-7 overall, showcasing defensive clamps (holding opponents under 70 points in wins).[1][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nFlorida excels in **rebounding dominance** (Haugh 6.2 RPG, Chinyelu 11.5) and paint scoring (.548 FG from Alex Condon's 15.0 PPG), favoring matchups against perimeter-oriented teams lacking size—ideal against guard-heavy South Region foes. Their switchable defense smothers pick-and-rolls, and high offensive efficiency thrives in up-tempo games.[5][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to elite perimeter shooting teams that stretch their bigs (team 3PT defense exposed in losses, allowing 38% from deep), and turnover-prone guards (Fland 58, Lee 40) struggle against pressure defenses. Limited bench depth beyond top rotation exposes them to foul trouble in frontcourt.[5][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster healthy with Rioux (7'9\" freshman) and Handlogten available. Coach Todd Golden brings proven tournament experience (prior Sweet 16 runs), and Florida's program history includes two national titles (2006-07) with consistent March success (8 Elite Eights since 1987).[2][4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Boogie Fland's playmaking in March chaos**—his 51 assists and transition burst could elevate Florida's offense against trapping defenses, but turnovers (58) might derail deep runs if he forces shots.[5]",
