      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Tavion Banks (G/F, Senior, 6'7\", 215 lbs)** averages 18.4 points per game, making him dangerous with his versatile scoring inside and out, elite rebounding for his position (8.2 rebounds/game), and ability to create off the dribble in transition.[1][3]  \n**Alvaro Folgueiras (F, Junior, 6'10\", 230 lbs)** contributes 15.7 points and 9.1 rebounds per game, excelling as a skilled international big who stretches the floor with 38% three-point shooting and dominates in the post with footwork and soft touch.[1][3]  \n**Kael Combs (G, Junior, 6'4\", 205 lbs)** leads the backcourt at 14.2 points per game, dangerous as a quick, high-volume shooter (42% from three on 7.3 attempts) who pressures defenses full-court and thrives in pick-and-roll sets.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nIowa plays at a **high tempo** (72.3 possessions per game), pushing the pace in transition to leverage their athletic wings. Defensively, they employ a **switch-heavy man-to-man philosophy** under coach Ben McCollum, prioritizing perimeter containment over rim protection. Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy emphasis on three-point shooting (39% as a team, 22.4 attempts/game) and cutting, supported by strong rebounding (38.1 boards/game, top-25 nationally) from their lengthy frontcourt.[1][3]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month (February 17–March 17, 2026), Iowa went 7-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season before stumbling in the Big Ten Tournament. They reached the quarterfinals but lost 78-72 to #3 Purdue after beating #14 Northwestern 85-67 in the second round. Key wins included road upsets at Michigan State (91-84) and Ohio State (76-71).[3]\n\n## Strengths\nIowa excels in **three-point volume and efficiency**, bombing away to overwhelm slower defenses, and **offensive rebounding** (13.2 per game), giving them second-chance points. Their **Adjusted Offensive Efficiency of 117.2** ranks top-30 nationally, favoring matchups against teams weak on the perimeter or lacking length, like guard-heavy or undersized opponents in the South Region.[1]\n\n## Weaknesses\nThey are vulnerable inside, allowing 52% two-point shooting due to thin rim protection (freshman Trevin Jirak as primary center), and struggle with **turnover creation** (18.1% opponent turnover rate, bottom-100). Physical, paint-dominant teams with strong half-court defense—like those featuring elite bigs or deliberate tempos—give them the most trouble.[1][3]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; Tavion Banks is fully cleared after a minor ankle tweak in the Big Ten quarters. Coach **Ben McCollum**, in his first year at Iowa after success at Drake, brings solid mid-major tournament experience (multiple CIT/NIT runs) but limited NCAA exposure. Iowa's historical March Madness success includes deep runs like the 1980 Final Four and 1999 Elite Eight, but recent years feature first- or second-round exits (e.g., 2025 first round).[3]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Brendan Hausen's (G, Senior) three-point shooting**—if he regains his early-season form (40% on 6.8 attempts/game), Iowa's spacing explodes; cold streaks (29% last month) have doomed them in tight games.[1]",
