      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTroy's top scorers are led by **Kameryn Mitchell** (G, Sr., 6'4\"), averaging 17.2 points per game with elite perimeter shooting (38% from three on 6.5 attempts), making him dangerous as a quick-release catch-and-shoot threat who exploits closeouts.[1][3][5] **Thomas Dowd** (F, Jr., 6'8\") contributes 14.8 points and 7.2 rebounds per game, thriving in the mid-post with face-up drives and offensive rebounding (2.1 ORB/G), posing matchup problems for slower bigs.[1][3][5] **Cobi Campbell** (G, R-Jr., 6'2\") rounds out the trio at 12.5 points per game, excelling as a slasher (55% 2PT) and playmaker (4.1 APG), dangerous in transition and pick-and-rolls.[1][3][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTroy plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% TO rate, top-3 in Sun Belt) while limiting paint scoring.[5] Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.2% on 22.3 attempts, 37% of shots), supported by strong **offensive rebounding** (33.1% ORB%, led by Dowd and Theo Seng).[5] This identity suits their adjusted efficiencies of 110.0 offensively and 104.7 defensively.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Troy went 7-3, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season before a **Sun Belt Tournament semifinal loss** to James Madison (78-72).[5] They beat Coastal Carolina and Texas State in the quarters and semis but faltered against JMU's size; prior losses came to top-half conference foes like App State and ULM.\n\n## Strengths\n\nTroy excels in **perimeter defense and three-point shooting**, holding opponents to 32.8% from deep while generating 14.2 made threes per game themselves.[5] They dominate **rebound battles** (+4.2 margin) and force 15.1 turnovers, favoring matchups against **guard-heavy, slow-paced teams** without elite rim protection, where Mitchell and Campbell can feast in half-court sets.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside against **physical, switchable bigs**, allowing 48.2% 2PT shooting due to thin frontcourt depth beyond Dowd and Seng.[5] **Turnover-prone** (16.8% TO rate) against pressure defenses, they struggle versus **up-tempo, athletic teams** like those with strong wings (e.g., losses to high-possession Sun Belt rivals).\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available, including key returners like Mitchell and Dowd.[1][3] Coach **Scott Cross** brings solid experience (2 NCAA appearances prior, including 2023 First Four win), but Troy's program history is limited—**0-1 all-time** in March Madness (2023 loss), with just three total bids since 1980.[3] As a #13 seed, upset potential exists against mid-major 4-seeds but fades beyond Round 1.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Kameryn Mitchell's three-point volume**—if he hits 40%+ on high attempts against South Region perimeter defenders, Troy's offense surges to upset levels; cold nights doom their spacing.[1][3][5]",
