      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nIdaho's top scorers are led by **Isaiah Brickner** (Senior G, 6'4\"), averaging 17.2 points per game, who earned Big Sky Player of the Week honors on Dec. 9, 2025, for his explosive scoring bursts and perimeter shooting that stretches defenses[7][2]. **Kolton Mitchell** (Redshirt Sophomore G, 6'1\"), the shifty point guard from Idaho State transfer, chips in 14.8 points and 5.2 assists per game, dangerous with his quickness in transition and ability to penetrate crowded lanes[1][7]. **Trevon Blassingame** (Redshirt Junior G, 6'4\" from FDU), rounds out the trio at 13.1 points per game, excelling as a combo guard with crafty finishing at the rim and opportunistic steals that fuel fast breaks[1][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nIdaho plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (around 68 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers and limits paint penetration, ranking top-4 in Big Sky steals[8]. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.2% on 22 attempts per game), while **rebounding** is a moderate strength led by forwards like Ewan Steele (6'9\" Sophomore F, 8.1 boards/game), focusing on second-chance opportunities[1][2][8].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb. 17–March 17, 2026), Idaho went 7-3, including a hot **five-game winning streak** to close the regular season, highlighted by a 78-72 upset over Weber State on March 1[3]. They advanced to the Big Sky Tournament semifinals but fell 69-65 to Montana in the title game, showing resilience with comebacks in three of their wins[9].\n\n## Strengths\n\nIdaho excels in **perimeter defense** and **turnover creation** (forcing 15.2 per game), making them dangerous against ball-dominant offenses[8]. They thrive in **up-tempo matchups** where their guards like Brickner and Mitchell can push in transition, and against teams weak on the glass, leveraging Rowbury (6'11\" Senior F, 7.9 rebounds/game) for putbacks[2][5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside with **poor rim protection** (allowing 52% two-point shots), they struggle against physical bigs or teams with strong post play[8]. Athletic, switchable defenses give them trouble, as their guards turn it over under pressure (14.1 per game), evident in losses to Northern Colorado and Eastern Washington[7][9].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full health for key rotation[1]. Head coach Arthur Moreira (noted for women's Coach of the Year, but men's staff includes experienced assistants like Matt Jones from Seattle) has limited NCAA experience, with Idaho's last bid in 2018[6][3]. Historically, the program has zero tournament wins, going 0-8 all-time in March Madness, with their deepest run a First Round exit as a 13-seed in 2001.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Kolton Mitchell's playmaking in chaos**—if he exceeds 6 assists without turnovers against a #2 seed's pressure, Idaho could steal a First Round upset; his Dec. 23 Player of the Week performance showed he can dictate tempo[7].",
