      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nLehigh's top scorers are **Jalen Vazquez** (G, Jr., 6-3), averaging 17.2 points per game with elite perimeter shooting (38% from three) that stretches defenses and creates driving lanes; **Peter Kramer** (G, Soph., 6-5), posting 14.8 points and 5.1 rebounds, dangerous as a versatile wing who attacks mismatches and crashes the glass; and **Tommy Conniff** (C, Sr., 6-11), contributing 12.1 points and 8.4 rebounds, a physical post threat who dominates inside with hook shots and offensive boards.[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nLehigh plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (around 68 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Brett Reed that clogs driving lanes and forces turnovers (top-150 nationally). Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36% on 22 attempts per game), while **rebounding** is a moderate strength led by bigs like Conniff, though they rank mid-pack in Patriot League board battles.[3]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17–March 17, 2026), Lehigh went 7-4, including a hot streak of four straight wins to close the regular season before a Patriot League Tournament quarterfinal loss. They peaked in the conference tournament, upsetting the #3 seed in the semifinals (76-69 win over Colgate on March 8, led by Hank Alvey's 30 points) before falling in the final, securing the #16 seed at 16-16 overall (17-16 entering semis).[3]\n\n## Strengths\n\nLehigh excels in **disruptive perimeter defense** (holding opponents to 33% from three) and **shooting from deep**, making them dangerous against teams lacking length to contest Vazquez and Kramer's range. They favor matchups with slower, guard-heavy foes where their pack-line forces 15%+ turnover rates, as seen in their Colgate upset.[3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside against **athletic bigs** (allowing 52% two-point shooting due to thin frontcourt depth), they struggle with physical rebounding battles and transition (bottom-third nationally in fast-break points allowed). Athletic, lengthier teams like mid-majors with strong wings exploit their lack of elite athleticism.[3]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with seniors like Conniff and Bube Momah motivated for a final run. Coach **Brett Reed** (19th year, 0-4 all-time in NCAA Tourney) brings Patriot League experience but limited March success. Lehigh's program history is sparse: 5 appearances since 1984, deepest run a 1985 Sweet 16, but recent bids (last in 2024) ended in first-round exits.[3]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jalen Vazquez's three-point volume**: If he hits 4+ threes (as in 3 of their last 5 wins), Lehigh can keep games low-scoring and pull off a Cinderella upset; cold nights expose their scoring limits.[1][3]",
