      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nPrairie View A&M relies on a veteran backcourt and frontcourt size for scoring punch. The top three players are:\n\n- **Tai'Reon Joseph (G, 6-3, Sr., 17.2 PPG)**: Transferred from UTSA, Joseph's quick release and ability to create off the dribble make him deadly in transition and isolation, averaging 3.1 assists while shooting 36% from three on high volume.[1][2][3]\n- **Dontae Horne (G, 6-4, Sr., 14.8 PPG)**: Ex-Georgia Southern wing who excels in the mid-range and on cuts, dangerous as a slasher with 4.2 rebounds per game and knack for drawing fouls against smaller defenders.[1][2][3]\n- **Corey Dunning (F, 6-9, Sr., 12.5 PPG)**: Graduate transfer from Xavier (LA) dominates the glass (7.8 RPG) and scores efficiently inside, threatening with face-up moves and putbacks that punish slow rotations.[1][2][3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nPrairie View A&M plays at a deliberate **tempo** (around 68 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that funnels drivers into 6-10 Ryan Bolton Jr. at the rim while relying on length from 6-8 Hassane Diallo and 6-9 Corey Dunning. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving bigs like Keeshawn Mason (6-8) and guards penetrating for kickouts, but **three-point shooting** is middling (32.1% on 18 attempts). **Rebounding** is their hallmark strength, ranking top-3 in SWAC at 38.2 boards per game thanks to size across positions.[1][2][3]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17, 2026), Prairie View went 5-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season (wins over Alcorn St 72-65, Southern 68-62, Texas Southern 75-70) before dropping two of three in the SWAC Tournament: beat Grambling 81-74 in quarters but lost to Jackson State 69-64 in semis and Alabama St 58-55 in the third-place game. This hot-then-flat finish secured the #4 seed and auto-bid despite the overall 14-17 mark.[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nPrairie View excels in **physical, half-court grinding** with superior **size and rebounding** (top-100 nationally in offensive rebound rate at 35.4%), allowing second-chance points that wear down opponents (averaging 14.2 ORB/game). Their adjusted defensive efficiency (106.2) shines against guard-heavy teams lacking interior scoring, favoring matchups vs. small-ball lineups where Dunning and Diallo feast on lobs and boards.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **up-tempo, perimeter-oriented teams** that stretch their deliberate pace and exploit poor three-point defense (allowing 35.2% from deep), as seen in losses to high-volume shooting SWAC foes like Grambling. Turnovers (14.1 per game) and slow starts plague them against athletic wings who disrupt Joseph and Horne in the halfcourt.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the South Region #16 seed game; full roster available including key seniors. Coach **Byron Smith** has limited NCAA experience (0-1 all-time, first appearance since 2018 NIT), but his 89-112 record since 2019 emphasizes gritty SWAC play. Program history in March Madness is sparse: 1-6 all-time, with zero wins since 1962 First Round upset over Texas Tech; recent SWAC auto-bids (2021, 2022) ended in blowouts as #16 seeds.[1][4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Tai'Reon Joseph's three-point volume**: If Joseph heats up from deep (currently 36% but 2.8 makes/game), it forces help rotations that spring Horne's drives and unlocks their stagnant halfcourt—potentially keeping games within 10 vs. a #1 seed; cold nights doom them to rebounding battles they can't win.[1][3]",
