      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nAlabama's top three players are **Labaron Philon** (G, 21.9 PPG), **Aden Holloway** (G, 16.8 PPG), and **Amari Allen** (F, ~11.6 PPG).[3][5][2]  \n**Labaron Philon** leads the team in scoring (21.9 PPG) and assists (4.7-5.6 APG), making him dangerous with his elite playmaking, 1.4 SPG, and ability to score at all three levels in transition.[3][5]  \n**Aden Holloway** averages 16.8 PPG with strong shooting efficiency (5.4 3PM per game estimate from totals), excelling as a perimeter threat who creates off the dribble and stretches defenses.[5][2]  \n**Amari Allen** contributes 7.1-7.3 RPG with 1.1 SPG and 0.7 BPG, dangerous as a versatile forward who dominates the glass (47 OREB) and provides switchable defense.[2][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nAlabama plays at a **high tempo** (94.6 PPG, 19th nationally), emphasizing transition scoring and a **guard-driven offensive scheme** reliant on pick-and-rolls and perimeter shooting (35.8% 3PT, 94th).[3]  \nDefensively, they employ a **switch-heavy philosophy** with length inside (5.1 BPG team), but struggle with opponent rebounding (36.4 DRPG allowed, 324th).[2][3]  \nThey rank highly in rebounding strength (38.5 RPG, 49th) led by forwards, but live by the three (high 3PA volume) in Nate Oats' motion offense.[1][3]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month leading to the SEC Tournament, Alabama went 10-3 in conference play, tying for the top of the SEC standings with teams like Arkansas, Missouri, Oklahoma, and Tennessee.[3]  \nThey showed strong form with high-scoring wins like 105-76 over South Dakota State (19/55 3PT), 103-? over Texas (17/29 3PT at 58.6%), and 110-? over UNC Asheville, but losses included a tight 76-94 defeat to Texas A&M.[5]  \nNo extended streaks noted, but they advanced deep in the SEC Tournament before the NCAA field, entering as a battle-tested 23-9 squad.[3]\n\n## Strengths\n\nAlabama excels in **offensive firepower** (adjusted 119.2 OE) with elite scoring (94.6 PPG), three-point volume (35.8%), and rebounding (38.5 RPG, 44.4 RPG team).[1][3]  \n**Matchups favoring them** include high-tempo teams they can outscore in shootouts (e.g., vs. Texas 103 points) or squads weak on the perimeter, where Philon/Holloway feast; their length (162 team BLK) disrupts slower bigs.[2][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on **defense** (adjusted 108.4 DE, 81.7 PPG allowed, 291st), particularly rebounding (36.4 allowed, 324th) and three-point defense (32.1% opponent).[3]  \n**Teams that trouble them** are physical, rebounding-heavy squads (e.g., Texas A&M 45 REB in win over ALA) or those with strong interior scoring, exposing rotation lapses and turnover-forcing pressure (6.4 SPG allowed).[2][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster availability with key returners like Philon, Holloway, Allen, and bigs Aiden Sherrell (2.3 BPG, 189 REB) and limited-minute Charles Bediako.[2][3]  \nNate Oats brings proven **tournament experience**, reaching Sweet 16 in 2024 and Final Four in 2023 with prior Alabama squads.[4]  \nHistorically, Alabama has March success under Oats (Elite Eight 2024), but as a #4 seed in Midwest, their 23-9 record positions them for a deep run against mid-major or inefficient offenses.[4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Three-point shooting variance**: Alabama's fate hinges on guards Philon (high volume) and Holloway converting from deep (team 35.8% but feast-or-famine in games like 17/29 vs. Texas); cold nights expose defensive flaws.[3][5]",
