      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nTennessee's top three players are **Ja'Kobi Gillespie (Sr. G, 16.4 PPG)**, a dynamic scoring guard with elite playmaking (5.2 APG across 935 minutes) and steals (2.1 SPG), dangerous due to his speed, 42 three-pointers made, and ability to penetrate defenses[1][2][3]. **Felix Okpara (F, 19.2 PPG)** leads scoring with efficient inside play (200 FGM on 492 FGA) and rim protection (44 blocks), thriving in pick-and-rolls and drawing fouls (106 FTM)[1][3]. **Nate Ament (Fr. F, 9.4 PPG, 6.6 RPG)** dominates the glass (205 rebounds, 57 offensive) as a versatile forward, dangerous with his length for second-chance points and transition scoring despite modest efficiency[1][2][3].\n\n## Team Identity\nTennessee plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (100.0 adjusted efficiency) with man-to-man pressure, high steals (7.5 SPG team average), and blocks (3.6 BPG), led by bigs like Okpara and Ament[1][3]. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** emphasizing paint scoring (high FGA volume inside) and moderate three-point volume (214 makes on 640 attempts, ~33%), supported by Gillespie's distribution[1]. Rebounding is a **major strength** (45.5 RPG, +12 rebound margin over opponents), fueling transition but exposing them to fast-paced foes[3].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Tennessee went 7-4, including a three-game winning streak into the SEC Tournament before losses to top seeds[1]. They averaged 78.2 PPG in wins but struggled offensively in defeats (under 70 PPG), with strong defensive showings holding foes under 65 points thrice[1][3]. In the SEC Tournament, they reached the quarterfinals as a 5-seed but fell 72-68 to Kentucky, hampered by 18 turnovers[5].\n\n## Strengths\nTennessee excels in **defensive disruption** (top-tier efficiency at 100.0, 7.5 SPG, 119 team blocks) and **rebounding dominance** (1500 total rebounds, 45.5 RPG), overwhelming undersized teams[1][3]. They favor matchups against inefficient shooters or weak interior defenses, where Okpara/Ament feast on boards (192/205 REB) and Gillespie exploits chaos for 543 points[1][2].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable on the perimeter against hot-shooting teams (opponents hit 3.9 BPG but exploit Tennessee's moderate 3P defense), with turnover issues (385 team TOs) and inconsistent half-court offense (114.6 adj. OE hampered by 33% 3PT% team-wide)[1][3]. Quick, guard-heavy teams trouble them by stretching the floor and limiting rebounding edges.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full rotation healthy including key bigs[2][5]. Coach Rick Barnes brings vast experience (22 NCAA appearances, 15 Sweet 16s, 2010, 2023, 2024 Elite Eights)[5]. Historically, Tennessee owns solid March success (24-20 all-time tournament record, frequent Sweet 16 runs, but just one Final Four in 2010)[5].\n\n## X-Factor\n**Ja'Kobi Gillespie's three-point shooting**—if he heats up beyond his 33% season mark (42 makes), Tennessee's spacing improves dramatically, unlocking Okpara inside and boosting their 114.6 offensive efficiency against Midwest region's athletic fronts[1][2].",
