      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nKentucky's top three players are **Otega Oweh** (Senior Guard, 18.2 PPG: 215/464 FG, 41/122 3PT, elite athleticism and 1.8 SPG make him dangerous in transition and as a perimeter defender), **Kam Williams** (Sophomore Guard, 18.8 PPG: 46/104 FG, 26/75 3PT at 34.7%, lethal shooter who stretches defenses), and **Malachi Moreno** (Center, 7.4 PPG with 6.4 RPG and 1.6 BPG: 89/168 FG, dominant rim protector and rebounder who controls the paint).[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nKentucky plays at a moderate tempo under Mark Pope, emphasizing a switchable defensive philosophy that relies on length and athleticism for 104.3 adjusted defensive efficiency. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with heavy three-point volume (aiming for 30+ attempts, led by shooters like Williams and Andrija Jelavić at 28.0% 3PT), averaging 114.1 adjusted offensive efficiency. Rebounding is a strength, with elite offensive boarders like Moreno (83 ORB) and Mouhamed Dioubate (67 ORB), fueling second-chance points.[1][2][4]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to the SEC Tournament, Kentucky went 4-3, snapping a three-game skid with back-to-back road wins over Vanderbilt and Florida before a quarterfinal exit. They finished the regular season 21-13 overall (likely 9-9 in SEC), losing to Tennessee in the SEC Tournament quarterfinals after rallying from 15 down but falling 78-74.[1]\n\n## Strengths\nKentucky excels in **defensive versatility** (Oweh's 60 STL, Moreno's 53 BLK) and **three-point volume** (multiple 30%+ shooters like Williams), making them deadly against slow-paced, perimeter-weak teams. They dominate rebounding battles (team leaders like Dioubate at 5.5 RPG, Moreno at 6.4 RPG), favoring matchups vs. small-ball lineups or poor defensive rebounders in the Midwest region.[1][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\nTurnover-prone guards (team-high 68 TO from top scorer) and inconsistent interior scoring expose them to elite defenses; Quaintance's limited play (4 GP, 5.0 RPG) due to ACL recovery hurts depth. Athletic, switchable teams with strong perimeter D (e.g., Big 12 physicality) trouble them by disrupting three-point rhythm and outpacing transition.[1][2][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported beyond Quaintance's ongoing ACL rehab limiting him to spot minutes; core rotation healthy entering as #7 seed. Coach Mark Pope brings solid experience (BYU Sweet 16 in 2011), but Kentucky's historical March Madness dominance (8 titles, 39 appearances) sets high bar—recent Pope era lacks deep runs. As a 21-13 squad, they project as bubble-to-second-round team in tough Midwest.[1][2]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Jayden Quaintance's health and minutes**: If the 6-10.5 sophomore (5.0 RPG, 0.8 BPG in limited action) returns to pre-injury form as a projected top-5 pick, he unlocks elite frontcourt versatility; otherwise, paint vulnerability dooms them.[2][4]",
