      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Somtochukwu \"Somto\" Cyril (C, Sophomore, 6-11, 260 lbs)** averages 14.2 points, 8.7 rebounds, and 2.1 blocks per game, making him dangerous with his physicality in the paint, elite rim protection, and ability to dominate smaller bigs on both ends.[1][2][3]  \n**Kanon Catchings (F, Sophomore, 6-9, 220 lbs)** scores 12.8 points per game with 6.4 rebounds and 1.8 assists, excelling as a versatile forward who stretches the floor with 38% three-point shooting and explosive athleticism in transition.[1][2][4]  \n**Jordan Ross (G, Junior, 6-3, 195 lbs)** leads the backcourt at 11.5 points and 4.2 assists per game, dangerous due to his quickness, playmaking vision, and 42% three-point accuracy that punishes sagging defenses.[2][3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nGeorgia plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (around 68 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Mike White that forces turnovers (18% opponent turnover rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.2% as a team, 22 attempts per game) and strong **rebounding** (38.1 boards per game, top-40 nationally) led by their frontcourt size.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17 - March 17, 2026), Georgia went 7-3, including a four-game winning streak capped by a 78-72 upset win over Tennessee in the SEC Tournament quarterfinals before a 65-59 semifinal loss to Alabama. They averaged 76.4 points per game in this stretch, holding opponents under 68 points in wins.[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nGeorgia excels in **defensive efficiency** (104.5 AdjDEF, top-25 nationally) through length and physicality, forcing 15% turnover rate and ranking top-30 in block rate (13.2%). They dominate **matchups against guard-heavy or undersized teams**, outrebounding opponents by +4.2 per game and thriving in half-court sets where Cyril and Catchings feast inside.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite perimeter shooting teams**, as their 35.8% opponent three-point defense ranks outside top-100, and they struggle with ball-screen coverage (allowing 1.12 points per possession). **High-tempo, athletic squads** like those with multiple switchable wings expose their lack of elite quickness on the perimeter.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; Cyril is fully healthy after a minor ankle tweak in February. Coach **Mike White** brings strong tournament experience, with 4 Sweet 16 appearances (2 at Florida, 2 at Georgia) and a 12-7 record as a head coach in March Madness. Historically, Georgia has limited success (2-5 since 2010, no Elite Eight since 1983), but this frontcourt gives them upset potential as an **#8 seed**.[1][2][3]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jordan Ross's three-point shooting**—if he sustains 40%+ from deep (as in their last 5 games), it opens driving lanes for Cyril and Catchings; cold nights (under 35%) force isolation offense and expose defensive lapses.[2][4]",
