      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nSaint Louis Billikens rely on a veteran core led by **Kalu Anya (F, Sr, 6-8)** averaging 17.2 points and 8.9 rebounds per game, dangerous due to his versatile inside-out scoring, elite rebounding, and ability to stretch defenses with 38% three-point shooting on 2.1 attempts[1][2][3]. **Robbie Avila (C, Sr, 6-10)** contributes 14.8 points and 9.5 rebounds, excelling as a skilled big man with soft touch around the rim (62% FG), dominant offensive rebounding (3.8 per game), and passing vision from the post that sets up cutters[1][2][3]. **Dion Brown (G, Sr, 6-3)** leads guards with 13.1 points, 4.2 assists, and 1.8 steals, a threat through his quickness in transition, on-ball pressure defense, and mid-range pull-ups (45% on jumpers)[1][2][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nSaint Louis plays at a **deliberate tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Josh Schertz, which forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and limits paint scoring (48.2 points allowed inside)[3]. Offensively, they run a **motion-based scheme** with high-low actions involving Anya and Avila, heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (37.2% on 22.1 attempts, ranking top-20 nationally), and strong **defensive rebounding** (73.1% rate) to control second-chance points[1].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Saint Louis went 9-1, including a seven-game winning streak capped by a **conference tournament championship** in the A-10 (85-72 win over VCU in final after beating Dayton 78-70 in semis). Key wins: 92-81 at Duquesne (Feb 22), 76-64 vs. Loyola Chicago (Mar 8); lone loss: 69-71 at Rhode Island (Feb 28)[1].\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in **elite defensive efficiency** (97.0 adjusted), smothering opponents with length and switching (top-15 in opponent eFG% at 48.1%), and dominate the glass (+6.2 rebound margin). **Matchups favoring them**: Slow-paced, paint-heavy teams like mid-major bigs or inefficient shooters, where Avila/Anya feast on rebounds and Schertz's pack-line disrupts rhythm—ideal vs. #8 seeds relying on isos[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **perimeter-oriented teams** that stretch their frontcourt (36% opponent 3PT in losses) and force rotations, exposing thinner wings like Quentin Jones on switches. Poor free-throw defense (opponents 76.8% FT) and occasional cold shooting nights (sub-30% 3PT in 3 losses) compound issues against high-tempo, guard-dominant offenses like Big 12 athletes[1][2].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy including freshmen Jax Kerr (6-11 F, 6.2 pts/5.1 reb) and Cam Hutson off bench[3][4]. Coach **Josh Schertz** brings strong tournament experience (3-2 NCAA record from Indiana State runs, including 2024 upset as 13-seed). Program history modest: 7 NCAA appearances since 1994, best run Sweet 16 (1994); 0-3 as double-digit seeds last decade[1][3].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Three-point shooting variance**: Saint Louis' 118.6 adjusted offensive efficiency surges (132.4 ORtg) on 40%+ nights from deep but craters to 102.1 on sub-33% games—hitting 37%+ in first-round win critical vs. likely #8 matchup[1].",
