      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nSanta Clara's top three players are **Allen Graves (F, 6-9, Fr.)**, **Elijah Mahi (F, 6-7, Sr.)**, and **Jake Ensminger (G, 6-9, Jr.)**. Graves leads with elite rebounding (221 total rebounds, 6.5 RPG) and defensive impact (67 steals, 32 blocks), making him dangerous as a versatile forward who crashes the glass and disrupts passing lanes while contributing offensively[3][1]. Mahi averages strong production with 161 rebounds (4.7 RPG), 49 steals, and 14 blocks, excelling as a senior leader who battles inside and transitions quickly, posing matchup problems with his physicality[3][1]. Ensminger provides 169 rebounds (5.5 RPG) and steady guard play across 31 starts, dangerous for his size at the position, enabling switchable defense and second-chance opportunities[3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nSanta Clara plays at a controlled tempo with strong defensive emphasis (103.4 adjusted defensive efficiency), forcing 303 steals (8.9 SPG) and limiting opponents to 36.2 RPG while holding a rebounding edge (38.8-36.2 RPG)[3][5]. Offensively, they rely on balanced efficiency (117.3 adjusted offensive efficiency, .468 FG%, .346 3PT%), mixing inside-out schemes with 445 offensive rebounds fueling second chances[1][5]. Three-point shooting (344 makes) stretches defenses, but rebounding strength (1214 total rebounds) defines their gritty, physical WCC style under Herb Sendek[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month entering the tournament, Santa Clara went 8-2, including a dominant 15-3 WCC regular-season mark (overall 25-8 adjusted to 26-8 per stats)[5][7]. They rode a five-game winning streak into the WCC Tournament, capped by a championship run (semifinal win over Gonzaga, title game victory), showcasing peak form with wins like 80-68 over Saint Louis[5]. Losses were narrow road tests against top-25 foes.\n\n## Strengths\n\nSanta Clara excels in **rebounding dominance** (1214 total, +89 margin) and **perimeter defense** (303 steals, holding foes under 103.4 efficiency), overwhelming smaller lineups[1][3]. Their physicality favors matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, as seen in WCC wins where they outrebounded opponents by double digits and converted 445 ORB into easy buckets[5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include inconsistent three-point volume (994 attempts but .346%) and occasional turnover issues (368 total), exposed against elite shooting or fast-break squads[1][5]. Teams with superior size inside (e.g., bigs neutralizing Graves/Mahi) or high-tempo pressure trouble them, as narrow losses showed opponents capitalizing on 218 steals against SCU[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available including key returners like Brenton Knapper (43 steals) and Thierry Darlan[2][4]. Coach **Herb Sendek** brings deep experience (10th season at SCU, prior NCAA runs at NC State/Arizona State with multiple Sweet 16s)[4][7]. Program history is modest (last NCAA win 2008, 2-6 since 2000), but this 26-8 squad as a #10 seed has upset potential in the Midwest[7].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Allen Graves' rebounding in crunch time**—his 221 boards and 67 steals could swing close games, but fading against athletic Big 12 fronts determines if Santa Clara advances past the first weekend[3].",
