      "perplexityContext": "# Miami (OH) RedHawks NCAA Tournament Scouting Report\n## #11 Seed, Midwest Region\n\n## Key Players\n\n**Antwone Woolfolk (F)** — The team's leading scorer and rebounder, Woolfolk averaged 10.3 points and 5.5 rebounds per game across 31 appearances.[3] He's a force on the glass with 42 offensive rebounds and provides interior presence on both ends. His 43.1% three-point shooting (11-32) shows surprising versatility for a forward.\n\n**Eian Elmer (G)** — A 6'6\" guard who led the team with 192 total rebounds and appeared in all 32 games.[5] Elmer's 6.0 rebounds per game from the guard position indicates elite positional versatility and length. He logged 1.2 steals per game, making him a perimeter defender who can disrupt passing lanes and create transition opportunities.\n\n**Luke Skaljac (G)** — The third scoring threat, Skaljac appeared in 30 games with 19 starts and provides shooting depth.[5] As a 6'2\" guard, he represents the team's ball-movement capacity and complements the other scoring options.\n\n## Team Identity\n\nMiami (OH) operates as an **elite offensive machine with stifling perimeter defense**. The RedHawks rank 6th nationally in effective field goal percentage (59.16%) while maintaining impressive shot selection discipline with a 13.17% turnover rate (70th nationally).[6] They excel in transition—their 72.8 pace is moderate but their 1.21 offensive efficiency (39th) suggests they generate clean looks in rhythm.\n\nThe three-point shot is central to their offense: they attempt 853 three-pointers and shoot 39.2% from distance, indicating a **perimeter-heavy approach** that stretches defenses horizontally.[3] Defensively, they hold opponents to just 50.84% effective field goal percentage (147th against), but their 33.03% three-point defense (124th) reveals vulnerability to outside shooting. They're aggressive rebounders, controlling 25.32% of offensive rebounds (332nd).[6]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nMiami (OH) finished the regular season with a **31-1 record and a perfect 18-0 MAC Conference mark**,[4] establishing themselves as conference champions. The lone loss represents an anomaly in an otherwise dominant campaign. As the MAC's automatic qualifier, they would have earned the conference tournament's #1 seed, though specific tournament results aren't detailed in available data. Their consistency suggests they've maintained form throughout March—this is a team peaking at tournament time with rhythm and confidence.\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Offensive Firepower**: The 1.21 offensive efficiency rating speaks to elite scoring versatility. With multiple capable scorers (Woolfolk, Elmer, Skaljac) and the adjusted efficiency of 118.5, they can score in bunches and from multiple positions.\n\n- **Perimeter Defense**: Holding opponents to 33.03% three-point shooting is a critical advantage in March, where perimeter-oriented teams struggle. The 7.4 steals per game (238 total) indicates aggressive, active hands that generate turnovers.\n\n- **Floor Spacing**: At 37.5% three-point shooting on high volume, they force opponents into difficult defensive rotations. This spacing opens driving lanes and creates paint opportunities for Woolfolk.\n\n- **Rebounding Advantage**: With 38.7 rebounds per game (265 offensive), they win the glass consistently—a crucial factor in tournament play where possessions are contested.\n\n**Favorable Matchups**: Teams that can't shoot the three-ball will struggle against Miami's perimeter-oriented defense. Similarly, slower-paced teams that want to control tempo will face a Red Hawks squad comfortable playing faster.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n- **Interior Defense**: The adjusted defensive efficiency of 103.8 and allowing 37.1 rebounds per game (compared to their 38.7) suggests vulnerability to strong post players and pick-and-roll attacks. Opponents are finding success in the paint.\n\n- **Three-Point Defense**: At 33.03%, defending the three is Miami's clear liability. Any tournament opponent with capable shooters can exploit this and force the RedHawks into foul trouble with aggressive closeouts.\n\n- **Turnover Margin Risk**: While they protect the ball (13.17% TO%), opponents force them into 15.38% turnover rates—a 2.2% disadvantage. Aggressive full-court pressure could disrupt their rhythm.\n\n- **Bench Depth**: The roster shows heavy reliance on starters. Justin Kirby (6.4 PPG in 19 games) provides limited secondary scoring, suggesting limited flexibility if foul trouble impacts the primary rotation.\n\n**Problematic Matchups**: Teams with elite interior scorers, multiple three-point shooters, and aggressive defensive pressure will pose the greatest challenges.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**Coaching Experience**: Head coach Travis Steele enters his 4th season, providing tournament experience and institutional knowledge.[4] Assistant coaches Jonathan Holmes and Khristian Smith are also in their 4th years, indicating a stable coaching staff familiar with tournament preparation.\n\n**Recent Roster Changes**: The program lost Kam Craft (transferred to Georgia Tech), Mekhi Cooper (Lindenwood), and Reece Potter (Kentucky) from last season,[4] suggesting some attrition of talented players. This roster represents continuity among remaining contributors.\n\n**Historical Context**: Miami (OH) enters as a MAC school, a mid-major conference without recent elite NCAA Tournament seeding. As an #11 seed, they face a challenging first-round matchup. Mid-major programs historically struggle in the tournament against Power Five competition, though strong offensive efficiency can carry them in single-elimination play.\n\n**Health Status**: No significant injury data is available in current reports. The team's complete participation (32 games across the roster) suggests players are healthy entering March.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Antwone Woolfolk's three-point shooting sustainability** will determine their tournament ceiling. At 11-32 (34.4%), he's efficient enough to stretch defenses, but this volume from a forward is unusually high. If he maintains this efficiency in tournament play, Miami becomes nearly impossible to defend—they lack a true center, so Woolfolk as a floor-stretching big creates spacing nightmares. Conversely, if tournament opponents shade coverage toward him and he regresses to more typical forward range, Miami's offensive versatility diminishes, and their vulnerability to interior scorers becomes",
