      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nAkron's top three players anchor their efficient offense with scoring versatility and rebounding prowess.\n\n- **Tavari Johnson (G, Sr.)**: Leads the team at **18.5 PPG** (251 FG, 75 3PM on 201 attempts), dangerous as a high-volume shooter with elite playmaking (170 assists) and low turnovers (1.4 per game), excelling in transition and spot-up threes.[1][5]\n- **Amani Lyles (F, Sr.)**: Second at **13.4 PPG** (181 FG, 45 3PM), a **double-double threat** (273 rebounds, 76 offensive) who dominates the glass and stretches the floor, making him lethal inside-out.[1][5]\n- **Shammah Scott (G, Sr.)**: Contributes **11.7 PPG** (81 3PM on 192 attempts), a sharpshooting threat with 100 assists, dangerous off screens and in pick-and-rolls for his quick release and spacing.[1][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nAkron plays at a **controlled tempo** (top-100 nationally), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (103.1 adjDEF) through active hands and help rotations. Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy **three-point volume** (elite 120.3 adjOFF driven by 35% team 3PT%), paired with **strong rebounding** (Lyles and Evan Mahaffey combine for 462 boards) to generate second-chance points.[5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Akron went **9-1**, including a **six-game winning streak** capped by a **MAC Tournament championship** (85-72 win over Toledo in the final). They averaged 82.3 PPG in conference play, holding opponents under 65 in four of five tournament wins.[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nAkron excels in **elite efficiency** (120.3 adjOFF, 103.1 adjDEF) and **three-point shooting** (team 36.2%), overwhelming slower defenses. **Rebounding dominance** (No. 25 nationally) favors them against small-ball lineups, as seen in MAC wins where they outrebounded foes by +8 per game. Matchups against mid-major or inefficient shooters play to their pack-line disruption.[5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **physical, athletic bigs** who exploit their lack of elite rim protection (team blocks rank 200th), as evidenced by a January loss to Kent State where they allowed 78 paint points. **Perimeter defense falters** against elite guards (opponents shoot 38% from three in losses), and poor free-throw defense (opponents 78% FT) hurts against foul-drawing teams.[5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**No major injuries** reported; full roster available with Lyles and Johnson at 100% post-MAC Tournament. Coach John Groce brings **extensive experience** (8 NCAA appearances, including 2013 Sweet 16 with Ohio), adept at first-round upsets. Akron's March Madness history is limited (**2 NCAA bids since 2000**, 0-2 record, last in 2010), but their 27-5 mark positions them for a classic #12 upset.[1][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Evan Mahaffey's versatility** (9.4 PPG, 189 rebounds, 118 assists, 57 steals): If the 6-6 Sr. wing locks down the opponent's star guard while crashing boards, Akron's rebounding edge becomes overwhelming; otherwise, they struggle in half-court sets.[5]",
