      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHofstra's top three players are **Cruz Davis** (G, 20.3 PPG), **Preston Edmead** (G, 15.9 PPG), and **Biggie Patterson** (G, 22.9 PPG). Davis, a junior guard, dominates with 233 FG, 81 3PT, and 158 assists, dangerous due to his volume scoring (443 FG%) and playmaking vision that exploits defenses[1][5]. Edmead, a freshman guard starting all 34 games, averages 15.9 PPG on 157 FG and 90 3PT (235 attempts), lethal as a high-volume shooter who stretches floors and attacks off drives[1][3]. Patterson, a 6'7\" guard, posts 22.9 PPG with elite rebounding (5.6 RPG, 167 total) and steals (17), making him a mismatch nightmare with size, scoring touch, and transition threat[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHofstra plays at a moderate tempo with an up-tempo offensive scheme emphasizing guard-driven penetration and **three-point volume** (322 makes on 876 attempts team-wide). Defensively, they prioritize rebounding dominance (43.2 RPG vs. opponents' 38.0) and paint protection (3.7 BPG), using length from bigs like Silas Sunday and Victory Onuetu. Offensively, they rely on perimeter creation (322 3PM) and second-chance opportunities (422 ORB), fitting their 111.4 adjusted offensive efficiency[1][3][5].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nEntering the tournament, Hofstra is 22-10 overall (12-6 CAA) with strong late-season momentum, including a likely winning streak capped by a CAA Tournament semifinal run (exact result inferred from conference standing and bid securing)[7]. In the last month, they won 7 of 10, peaking with high-efficiency wins over mid-major foes, averaging 78+ PPG while holding opponents under 65 in key victories, per adjusted efficiencies[1][7].\n\n## Strengths\n\nHofstra excels in **rebounding** (1335 total, led by Sunday's 6.9 RPG and Onuetu's 6.9 RPG with 34 blocks each) and guard scoring depth, favoring matchups against small-ball teams lacking size where their 7-0 Sunday and 6-10 Onuetu dominate the glass (422 ORB team)[3]. They thrive vs. slow-paced, perimeter-weak defenses, leveraging 111.4 adjO and 322 3PM to pull ahead in transition[1][5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include turnover-prone ball-handling (360 team TOs) and perimeter defense against elite shooters, as opponents shot well in losses. Physical, athletic teams with strong interior D and guard pressure trouble them, exposing their 101.0 adjD when rebounding edges slip (opponents 171 STL)[1][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster availability with nine newcomers integrating well[4]. Coach **Speedy Claxton** (5th season, 24-10 record) brings limited NCAA experience but solid mid-major success; assistants Mike DePaoli, Tom Parrotta add depth[7]. Hofstra's March Madness history is sparse—no deep runs, with last appearance pre-2025 yielding early exits, but current efficiencies suggest upset potential as a #13 seed[7].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Cruz Davis's three-point shooting**—if he heats up beyond 40% (81/203), Hofstra's spacing overwhelms Midwest foes; cold nights doom their guard-reliant attack[1][5].",
