      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nWright State's top scorers are **Michael Cooper** (G, 14.7 PPG), **Solomon Callaghan** (G, 9.9 PPG), and **Kellen Pickett** (F, 8.7 PPG).[1][4][5] Cooper is dangerous as a versatile guard with 407 total points, elite playmaking (115 assists), and defensive disruption (83 steals), excelling in transition.[1][3] Callaghan threatens from deep (66/171 3PT at 38.6%) and finishes at the rim (317 total points), making him a sharpshooting mismatch.[5] Pickett dominates inside-out with 46 blocks (team-high), 184 rebounds (5.4 RPG), and efficient scoring (110/195 FG at 56.4%), anchoring the paint.[4][5][6]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nWright State plays at a moderate tempo with balanced, deep rotations (up to 11 players late-season), emphasizing **defensive versatility** and rebounding dominance (1204 total rebounds, 35.4 RPG vs. opponents' 31.3).[3][5][6] Offensively, they run a motion scheme with strong three-point volume (361% team 3PT%) and inside finishing (.488 FG%), led by guards like TJ Burch (3.2 APG, 83 steals).[1][4][5][6] Rebounding is a strength (383 offensive boards), but they prioritize chaos defense with 252 steals and 148 blocks (all-time single-season record).[3][5][6]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nEntering the tournament, Wright State finished strong as **Horizon League regular-season champions** (23-11 overall, 15-5 conference) with balanced play down the stretch, expanding rotations to 9-11 deep.[4][6] They went 4-3 in their last seven games, relying on contributions from Sam Alamutu (97 points in limited minutes) and role players like Bryan Etumnu.[4][6] As the #1 seed, they won the conference tournament to secure the auto-bid, though specific streak details show no extended win/loss runs in the final month.[6]\n\n## Strengths\n\nWright State's **elite defense** (106.8 AdjDE) and rebounding edge (39.1 RPG allowed vs. 34.7 opponents) smother slower teams, with 252 steals and 148 blocks creating turnovers and second chances.[1][3][5] They excel against guard-heavy or perimeter-focused opponents, using length from Pickett (46 BLK), Holden (40 BLK), and Imariagbe (231 REB, 7.0 RPG) to control the glass and paint.[3][5][6] Matchups favoring mid-majors or inefficient shooters play to their 113.3 AdjOE and deep bench.[6]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include over-reliance on balanced scoring (no player over 15 PPG, five at 9+), exposing them to hot-shooting teams that stretch their 106.8 AdjDE from beyond the arc (opponents 33.0% 3PT).[1][5][6] Physical, elite-rebounding bigs trouble them, as seen in potential paint battles, and their moderate tempo falters against faster, athletic transition squads.[3][5] Turnovers (383 total) from nine-man rotations can snowball.[1][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy with key returners like Imariagbe and Pangonis available.[2][3] Coach Clint Sargent (2nd season) has limited NCAA experience, but assistants Dan Beré, Travis Trice, and Jaaron Simmons add tournament savvy.[6] Historically, Wright State has sparse March Madness success (last appearance pre-2025-26, no deep runs as a mid-major), but this squad's league title and defensive metrics offer upset potential as a #14 seed.[6]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**TJ Burch's** steals (83 total, 3.2 SPG) and assists (115) could spark chaos against a higher seed, forcing turnovers to fuel transition buckets and dictate tempo.[1][3][4][6]",
