      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTennessee State relies on a balanced attack led by **Jalen Pitre (F, 17.6 PPG)**, who dominates inside with 6.5 RPG, 1.2 SPG, and elite rebounding (201 total rebounds), making him dangerous through physicality and second-chance opportunities[1][5]. **Damon Howard Jr. (G, 17.3 PPG)** provides scoring punch with 81 three-pointers made on strong volume, excelling as a perimeter threat and creating off the dribble[1]. **Aaron Nkrumah (G, 11.7 PPG)** rounds out the trio as a disruptive two-way force, leading the team with 2.9 SPG and 5.6 RPG while contributing defensively across 31 starts[1][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTennessee State plays at a deliberate **tempo (pace 72.8, 28th nationally)**, emphasizing a gritty, physical defensive philosophy that holds opponents to 73.3 PPG and forces turnovers (17.72% opponent TO%)[6][8]. Offensively, they run a mid-tempo scheme focused on inside-out play, with solid three-point volume (effective FG% 50.11) but strength in **rebounding (34.93% offensive rebound rate, 51st nationally)** to generate extra possessions[8]. They prioritize defense and board control over fast breaks.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Tennessee State has surged with a **7-2 record in their final nine games**, including a five-game winning streak capped by an **OVC Tournament championship win** over the regular-season champ, securing the auto-bid despite their 20-9 overall mark[6]. This hot streak features three double-digit victories, showcasing improved defensive clampdowns (holding foes under 70 points twice).\n\n## Strengths\n\nTennessee State's biggest edge is their **rebounding dominance** (top-50 offensive rebounding), led by Pitre and Nkrumah, allowing them to control tempo and limit opponent possessions against slower, perimeter-oriented teams[1][5][8]. They excel in **gritty, low-possession games** where their defensive efficiency (102.3) shines, favoring matchups against inefficient shooters or teams weak on the glass, as seen in OVC title dominance.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerability lies in **perimeter defense against high-volume three-point teams**, as their adjusted offensive efficiency (108.0) dips when rebounding edges evaporate, and they struggle with pace-up offenses (opponents exploit their 51.24 eFG% allowed)[8]. Athletic, transition-heavy teams that stretch the floor give them trouble, exposing limited athletic depth beyond their top rebounders[6].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the #15 seed spot in the Midwest Region; full rotation healthy post-OVC Tournament[9]. First-year head coach **Nolan Smith** brings zero NCAA Tournament experience, but assistants like DerMarr Johnson add NBA pedigree[7]. Historically, Tennessee State has minimal March Madness success—zero wins since 1981, with just three appearances as a mid-major, often exiting as first-round fodder[7].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Aaron Nkrumah's defensive disruption**—if his 2.9 SPG translates to neutralizing a star guard in their First Four or 16-seed matchup, Tennessee State could steal a game; otherwise, elite offenses will expose their limits[1][5].",
