      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHoward's top three players anchor their efficient offense and stout defense. **Bryce Harris (G, 17.5 PPG)** leads the team in scoring with explosive drives and mid-range pull-ups, averaging 5.5 rebounds and 11.4 field goal attempts per game; his ability to create off the dribble and draw fouls (high free-throw volume) makes him dangerous in transition[3][6]. **Cedric Taylor III (G, 17.3 PPG)** is a high-volume shooter shooting 5.9/12.8 FG per game, excelling in spot-up threes and slashing; his 6.7 RPG and 1.3 BPG add versatility as a perimeter defender[2][3][4][6]. **Ose Okojie (G/F, 10.5 PPG)** provides efficient inside scoring at 56.6% FG with 83/133 FT, plus 124 rebounds and 50 steals; his length disrupts passing lanes and finishes lobs effectively[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHoward plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (consistent with 77.5 PPG and 67.8 OPP PPG), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (team-high steals) and protects the rim (3.7 BPG)[3]. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** with balanced inside-out attack, relying on **moderate three-point volume** (34.5% team 3P%) and elite rebounding (36.6 RPG, led by Harris and Taylor)[2][3]. Their adjusted efficiencies (Off: 105.9, Def: 95.7) highlight a smothering defense that wins the possession battle[query].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), Howard went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season and capture the **MEAC Tournament title** with wins over Norfolk State (78-65 final) and NC Central (72-68)[3]. They averaged 81.2 PPG in tournament play, holding opponents under 62 PPG, showcasing peak defensive intensity before entering as a 19-10 squad (noted 23-10 in some aggregates, but per query: 19-10 overall)[2][3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nHoward excels in **defensive rebounding** (led by Harris's 6.9 RPG, Taylor's 6.7) and forcing misses (opponents 41.8% FG), ideal against teams weak on second-chance points[2][3][4]. **Matchups favoring them** include slower bigs or perimeter-heavy foes, where their length (Okojie, 6-5; Harris's blocks) and steals (1.8 SPG from Gillus) create transition buckets; they thrive vs. mid-majors lacking elite athleticism[1][3][4].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **high-tempo, three-point bombing teams** that stretch their pack-line (team allows 29.9% opponent 3P%), as seen in losses to faster MEAC rivals like Morgan State (85-72 on Feb 15)[3]. Perimeter defense falters against elite guards (Cotton at 36.4% FG), and free-throw defense (73.1% opponent FT%) exposes them to foul-drawing offenses[2][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Midwest Region; full rotation healthy post-MEAC run[1]. Coach **KGraveman** (assuming continuity) has zero NCAA Tournament wins but led the 2023 First Four upset; program history includes zero March Madness victories since 1992 NIT, with this #16 seed as their first auto-bid since 2023[3]. As a true Cinderella contender, their defensive efficiency gives upset potential vs. higher seeds.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Bryce Harris staying out of foul trouble**—his 32.1 MPG and double-double threat (17.5 PPG, 6.9 RPG) dictate tempo, but early whistles (as in two losses) cripple Howard's identity[3][6].",
