      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Ace Valentine (G, Junior, 6'3\")**: Averages 13.8 PPG, 4.8 APG, and 4.1 RPG while leading the team in minutes (33.4 MPG). Dangerous as the primary ball-handler with elite vision (124 assists) and defensive activity (25 steals), enabling transition scoring and playmaking.[1][2][3]\n\n**Jah'Likai King (G, Junior, 6'2\")**: Leads the team at 15.5 PPG on 41.2% FG and 33.3% from three (22 makes). His scoring versatility, rebounding (2.9 RPG), and steals (1.1 SPG) make him a threat in up-tempo sets and as a pesky on-ball defender.[4][5]\n\n**Jose Roberto Tanchyn (F, Junior, 6'10\")**: Contributes 8.6 PPG and a team-high 5.7 RPG (178 total rebounds, 45 offensive). His size and efficiency (53.5% FG) pose mismatch problems inside, with 17 blocks and 18 steals adding rim protection and transition disruption.[1][2]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUMBC plays at a moderate-to-up-tempo pace, leveraging **Ace Valentine's** orchestration for transition opportunities after forcing 5.0 SPG. Defensively, they emphasize rebounding (38.2 RPG, led by forwards) and paint protection (2.6 BPG), holding opponents to 100.3 adjusted efficiency. Offensively, they run a balanced scheme with 36% three-point attempts (259 makes on 717 tries) but rely on inside scoring (53.5% from Odunowo/Tanchyn). Rebounding is a strength (1135 total), but three-point shooting is average at ~36%.[1][2][4][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), UMBC went 8-2, including a 3-game win streak capped by an **OT thriller win over Bryant (79-74 on Jan 15, but recent form aligns with 3-1 conference mark as #1 seed)**. They dominated the America East Tournament, winning the title for their automatic bid as the top seed. Key wins featured strong rebounding margins and Valentine's double-digit assists.[4][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nUMBC excels in **rebounding (38.2 RPG, + margins via Tanchyn/Odunowo)** and defensive efficiency (100.3 AdjD), smothering interior scoring while transitioning quickly. They favor matchups against slower, perimeter-heavy teams where King's scoring and Valentine's playmaking exploit turnovers (5.0 SPG). Their 111.3 AdjO thrives vs. poor defenders.[1][2][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter (36% 3P%), they struggle against elite shooting guards who punish sagging defenses. Low assist-to-turnover from bigs (e.g., Tanchyn's 28 AST/38 TO) exposes them to press defenses. Athletic, switchable teams with strong wings give them trouble by isolating King/Valentine.[1][2][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available including key transfers like Odunowo (Gr., 5.4 RPG). Head coach **Joe Leggio** (entering 3rd year) has limited NCAA experience but guided the AEC title run. Historically, UMBC's lone Dance appearance was the famous 2018 upset as a 16 over Virginia; otherwise, no deep runs (22-8 this year marks program best since).[3][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Ace Valentine's** health and assist production—if he sustains 4.8+ APG without turnovers (42 TO), UMBC's transition attack could spark a First Four or 16-seed upset; fatigue from 33+ MPG dooms them otherwise.[1][2]",
