      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nArizona's top three players are **Koa Peat (F, Fr., 6-8)** averaging 18.7 points and 9.2 rebounds, dangerous due to his polished mid-post game, versatility, and athleticism that dominates both ends; **Jaden Bradley (G, Sr., 6-3)** at 15.4 points and 4.1 assists, a threat as the steady lead guard with elite shot-making and playmaking in transition; and **Motiejus Krivas (C, Jr., 7-2)** posting 12.9 points and 8.5 rebounds, lethal with his size, rim protection, and soft touch around the basket.[1][2][3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nArizona plays at a **high tempo** (top-15 nationally), emphasizing **switchable man-to-man defense** that disrupts passing lanes and forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate). Offensively, they run a **versatile motion scheme** with heavy pick-and-roll actions, elite **rebounding** (No. 4 in offensive boards at 38.1%), and solid three-point volume (36.2% on 22.5 attempts per game), leveraging their length for second-chance points and transition buckets.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Arizona went 11-1, including a 9-game winning streak capped by a **Big 12 Tournament championship** (85-72 win over Kansas in the final). Their lone loss was a 78-75 upset at Baylor on Feb 28; they dominated Houston (92-81) and Iowa State (88-76) in conference semis, averaging 84.3 points while holding foes under 70.[1][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nArizona excels in **size and rebounding dominance** (No. 2 nationally in rebound margin at +12.4), overwhelming smaller lineups, and **defensive versatility** (94.4 AdjDEF forces 16.8% turnovers). They favor matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams like Villanova or Purdue, where their length (multiple 6-11+ bigs) and tempo create chaos.[1][2][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite perimeter shooting teams** that stretch their defense (allow 36.8% from three in losses) and stretch-fours who exploit switches. Quick, athletic guards (e.g., Duke-style backcourts) trouble them by turning their bigs, as seen in the Baylor loss where opponents shot 42% from deep.[1][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Peat and Krivas at 100%. Coach **Tommy Lloyd** boasts strong tournament experience (2022 Sweet 16, 2024 Elite Eight), going 8-4 in March Madness. Arizona's program history shines with **1997 national title**, 5 Final Fours, and consistent deep runs (Sweet 16 or better in 4 of last 6).[1][2][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Freshman Koa Peat's** ability to handle double-teams in March; if he maintains 18+ PPG efficiency against pro-style defenses, Arizona reaches the Final Four—stifled production (under 12 points) dooms them early.[2]",
