      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nPurdue's top three players are **C.J. Cox** (Guard, 14.0 PPG), **Trey Kaufman-Renn** (Forward, 12.9 PPG), and **Daniel Jacobsen** (Center, 6.0 PPG).[1][3][9]\n\n- **C.J. Cox**: A sophomore guard averaging 14.0 PPG on 44.3% FG and 36.4% from three (55 makes), he's dangerous with his volume three-point shooting (151 attempts) and playmaking (317 assists), stretching defenses and creating open looks.[1][3]\n- **Trey Kaufman-Renn**: The leading rebounder at 8.5 RPG (280 total), he scores 12.9 PPG efficiently inside while dominating the glass (97 offensive rebounds), making him a matchup nightmare for smaller frontcourts with his physicality.[3]\n- **Daniel Jacobsen**: A 7-4 sophomore center averaging 6.0 PPG on 64.5% FG and leading the team with 44 blocks (1.3 BPG, or 2.2 per ESPN per-game stats), his rim protection and 114 rebounds (3.3 RPG off bench) deter drives effectively.[1][3][9]\n\n## Team Identity\nPurdue plays at a slow **tempo** (pace of 65.9 possessions, 334th nationally), emphasizing a gritty, physical style under Matt Painter.[6]\n\n- **Defensive philosophy**: Man-to-man with strong interior presence, holding opponents to 105.7 adjusted defensive efficiency and 52.28% effective FG% allowed (232nd).[6]\n- **Offensive scheme**: High-efficiency half-court sets (123.0 adjusted offensive efficiency, #1 in some metrics at 1.31 points per possession), relying on 37.92% three-point shooting (19th nationally) and 36.67% offensive rebound rate (20th) for second-chance points.[6]\n- **Rebounding strength**: Elite on the offensive glass but solid defensively (26.19% defensive rebound rate, 21st), fueled by bigs like Kaufman-Renn and Jacobsen.[6]\n\n## Recent Form\nEntering the tournament with a 27-8 record (13-7 Big Ten), Purdue has been solid but not dominant lately, likely riding momentum from the conference tournament where they finished strong after a midseason skid.[8]\n\nLast month's play featured streakiness: a five-game win streak into the Big Ten Tournament, capped by a semifinal run before a championship loss, showcasing improved shooting (over 37% from three in key wins) amid their overall 27-8 mark.[1][8]\n\n## Strengths\nPurdue excels in **efficiency** (top-tier adjusted offensive rating of 123.0) and **rebounding**, dominating possession battles with a 36.67% offensive rebound rate and physical bigs who overwhelm undersized teams.[6]\n\nThey thrive against guard-heavy or slower-paced opponents, using Braden Smith's elite playmaking (9.6 APG) and Cox's shooting to control tempo, while their low turnover rate (11.85%, 15th) sustains leads in grind-it-out games.[6][9]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **up-tempo, athletic teams** that push the pace (Purdue ranks 338th in pace at 65.8), exposing their 52.28% effective FG% allowed and average three-point defense (34.19% opponent 3P%, 197th).[6]\n\nPerimeter speed mismatches trouble them, as smaller guards like Fletcher Loyer (13.6 MPG) struggle against pressure, and their low FTA/FGA (28.61%, 336th) hurts in foul-heavy matchups.[1][6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy with Daniel Jacobsen providing bench rim protection.[3]\n\n**Coach Matt Painter** brings deep tournament experience (21st season, multiple Sweet 16s), though Purdue's March Madness history includes recent Final Four (2024) success but early exits like 2023's first-round upset.[8]\n\nAs a #2 seed in the West (27-8 record), they match well against mid-majors but face tough roads vs. elite guards or shooters in later rounds.[8]\n\n## X-Factor\n**C.J. Cox's three-point volume**: If Cox sustains 36.4% on high attempts (151 makes), Purdue's spacing unlocks Kaufman-Renn inside; cold streaks expose their half-court limits and doom deep runs.[1][3]",
