      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Graham Ike (Forward, Graduate Student)**: Leads the team at **19.7 PPG** (572 points in 29 games), shooting 57.3% from the field and 8.2 RPG with 25 blocks; dangerous due to his elite interior scoring, rebounding dominance, and paint protection that overwhelms slower bigs.[1][3][6][7]\n\n**Braden Huff (Forward, Redshirt Junior)**: Averages **17.8 PPG** in 18 games (25.4 MPG), with 5.6 RPG, 56.7% FG, and strong offensive rebounding (49 ORB); his physicality and efficiency in the post make him a mismatch nightmare for undersized fronts.[3][7]\n\n**Tyon Grant-Foster (Guard, Graduate Student)**: Contributes **~11-12 PPG** (inferred from 371 points in 713 minutes across 33 games), 5.0 RPG, 1.1 BPG, and 25 steals; his 6'7\" length disrupts passing lanes while providing versatile scoring and shot-blocking from the perimeter.[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nGonzaga plays at a deliberate **tempo (pace of 70.4 possessions, 118th nationally)**, emphasizing half-court execution with a dominant **offensive scheme built on high-efficiency inside-out play (adjusted offensive efficiency 118.7, eFG% 56.26 at 22nd)**.[5] Defensively, they swarm with length and activity (**defensive efficiency 92.1, top-ranked at 0.90 efficiency, 18.10% opponent TO%, 25.88% opponent ORB% at 19th**), forcing turnovers and limiting second chances.[5][6] They shoot **34.0% from three (220/648, 176th)** but excel in rebounding (34.80% ORB%, 1317 total rebounds) and paint dominance (85.1 PPG).[1][5][6]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month entering the tournament, Gonzaga has been dominant, riding a **16-game winning streak** after their three regular-season losses, including a blowout 79-68 win in their most recent game.[6] They finished WCC play 16-2 and won the **WCC Tournament championship**, securing the automatic bid and #3 seed with flawless execution in conference play.[4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nGonzaga excels in **elite efficiency on both ends (1.23 off eff 21st, 0.90 def eff 3rd)**, suffocating defenses with length (281 steals, 122 blocks) and dominating the glass (395 ORB, 8.2 RPG leader in Ike).[1][5] They thrive against teams lacking size or perimeter defenders, as their **interior scoring (51.0% FG)** and rebounding bully slower bigs while wings like Grant-Foster erase pick-and-rolls—ideal vs. West region foes with average frontcourts.[1][6]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities emerge against **high-tempo, elite three-point shooting teams** that stretch their deliberate pace and exploit occasional lapses (30.77% opponent 3P%, 33.95% own 3P% at 176th), as seen in their three losses likely to faster WCC rivals.[5][6] Perimeter defense can be targeted if opponents attack gaps before help rotations, and limited FTA/FGA (31.12%) hurts in foul-heavy games.[5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with key rotation intact (33 players with 20+ MPG).[3] Coach **Mark Few** brings vast experience (27th season, consistent Sweet 16+ runs), and Gonzaga's program boasts **storied March Madness success** (multiple Elite Eights, Sweet 16s, and 2021 runner-up under Few).[4] As a #3 seed with 30-3 record, they match up well in the West but face potential Sweet 16 tests from athletic guards.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Braden Huff's health and minutes**: Limited to 18 games (17.8 PPG, 8.1 RPG), his full availability unlocks unstoppable frontcourt depth with Ike; any rust or foul trouble could expose thin big-man rotation against physical foes.[3][7]",
