      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Darius Acuff Jr. (G, Fr., 6-3, 190 lbs)**: Leading scorer at 18.7 PPG, dangerous due to his elite quickness, explosive drives, and 37% three-point shooting on 5.2 attempts per game, creating mismatches as a primary ball-handler.[1][2]\n\n**Trevon Brazile (F, Sr., 6-10, 230 lbs)**: Second-leading scorer at 15.4 PPG and 8.2 RPG, lethal with his versatile inside-out game, 42% from three, and rim protection (1.8 BPG), excelling in pick-and-pop and transition.[1][2][3]\n\n**Karter Knox (G/F, So., 6-6, 220 lbs)**: Third option at 13.9 PPG and 6.1 RPG, threatening with physicality, mid-range pull-ups, and rebounding tenacity, shooting 49% from the field while drawing fouls.[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUnder John Calipari, Arkansas plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line defensive philosophy that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with high-low actions involving bigs like Brazile and Malique Ewin, relying on moderate three-point volume (34.2% on 19.8 attempts) and dominant rebounding (39.1 RPG, top-15 nationally).[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Arkansas went 9-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a 78-72 semifinal win over Tennessee in the SEC Tournament before falling 81-76 to Alabama in the championship. They averaged 82.1 PPG in wins, holding foes under 65 in four of six road/neutral victories.[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nArkansas excels in rebounding battles (+6.2 margin) and defending the perimeter (32.1% opponent 3PT%), favoring matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams unable to match their frontcourt size (Ewin 6-11, Brazile 6-10, Nick Pringle 6-10). Their efficiency edges (120.6 AdjO, 107.4 AdjD) shine versus mid-major or inefficient offenses.[2][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to elite perimeter shooting (36% opponent 3PT allowed in losses) and heliocentric guards who exploit their occasional ball-screen coverage lapses, as seen in losses to Kentucky and Auburn. Thin guard depth beyond Acuff leads to fatigue in high-tempo games.[1][2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; Brazile fully cleared after minor ankle tweak. Calipari brings proven experience (one national title, eight Elite Eights). Arkansas historically thrives in March Madness (29-24 all-time, Sweet 16 in 2021 under previous staff), with this roster's talent projecting a Sweet 16 floor as a #4 seed.[1][7]\n\n## X-Factor\n\nDarius Acuff Jr.'s free-throw shooting (81% but 4.2 attempts/game); if he draws contact and converts in crunch time like his 28-point SEC final outburst, Arkansas advances deep—otherwise, offensive droughts doom them.[2]",
