      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Nick Boyd (G, 20.6 PPG)** leads the Badgers as their primary scorer and playmaker, averaging 31.8 MPG with a .479 FG% and .364 3PT%, making him dangerous through high-volume scoring, 143 assists (4.2 APG), and pesky defense (1.1 SPG).[1][2][3]  \n**Nolan Winter (F, 13.3 PPG)** dominates inside-out with elite rebounding (8.6 RPG, 69 ORB) and shot-blocking (1.2 BPG), shooting .569 FG% while stretching the floor at .333 from three, posing matchup problems for slower bigs.[1][2][3]  \n**John Blackwell (G, ~19.0 PPG inferred from minutes/usage)** provides backcourt versatility with 4.9 RPG and 1.2 SPG, excelling in transition and secondary creation (high assist rate); his athleticism makes him a threat in up-tempo scenarios.[3]\n\n## Team Identity\nWisconsin plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (emphasizing half-court execution over fast breaks), anchored by a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that prioritizes help rotations, low turnovers (304 total, ~8.9 per game), and rebounding dominance (35.5 RPG).[1][2][6]  \nOffensively, they run a **gap-drive-and-kick scheme** with strong **three-point volume** (.361 team 3PT%, 400 makes on 1108 attempts), complemented by physical rebounding (1207 total rebounds) and efficient finishing inside.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), Wisconsin went 7-3, including a 4-game winning streak capped by a Big Ten Tournament quarterfinal exit (lost to Purdue); they split series with top rivals like Iowa and Maryland, showing resilience in close games.[1][2][4]  \nTheir 24-10 overall record featured road wins over ranked foes, but a late skid against elite defenses highlighted turnover issues in high-stakes play.[6]\n\n## Strengths\nWisconsin excels in **rebounding battles** (top-Big Ten 35.5 RPG, led by Winter's 8.6) and **defensive efficiency** (107.2 AdjDE), suffocating opponents with physicality and 193 steals.[1][2][3]  \nThey favor **matchups against slower, perimeter-weak teams** where their 3PT shooting (.361%) and pack-line D force misses, enabling second-chance points (353 ORB).[1][6]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **explosive, athletic guards** who exploit their deliberate tempo, as evidenced by higher TOs (304 total) against press defenses; poor free-throw volume in crunch time (.786 team FT%) hurts in foul-heavy games.[1][2]  \n**High-tempo, switchable teams** like those with versatile wings give them trouble by disrupting half-court sets and limiting rebounding edges.[3][6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; key players like Boyd and Winter are fully healthy.[2][4]  \n**Coach Greg Gard** brings proven tournament experience (multiple Sweet 16s, Elite Eight in 2014), with Wisconsin's historical March Madness success including 30+ wins in recent deep runs and consistent Big Ten contention.[4]  \nAs a #5 seed (24-10, 117.2 AdjOE/107.2 AdjDE), they match well against mid-major chaos in the West region.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Nick Boyd's three-point volume**—if he sustains .364% on 165 attempts amid tournament pressure, it unlocks Wisconsin's spacing; cold shooting strands their bigs and dooms half-court offense.[1][2]",
