      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nBYU's top three players are **AJ Dybantsa** (6-9 freshman forward, 22.1 PPG), **Robert Wright III** (6-1 sophomore guard, 17.8 PPG), and **Richie Saunders** (6-6 senior guard, 15.4 PPG). Dybantsa, the projected No. 1 pick in the 2026 NBA Draft, dominates with elite scoring versatility, pull-up threes, and athleticism in transition, making him unguardable on the perimeter[1][3][4]. Wright III, a Baylor transfer, excels as a quick point guard with explosive drives and playmaking (6.2 APG), dangerous in pick-and-rolls against slower Big 12 defenses[4]. Saunders, a First Team All-Big 12 returner, thrives as a combo guard with off-ball shooting (42% 3PT) and defensive tenacity, punishing switches[3][4].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nBYU plays at a **high tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing transition scoring and **spread pick-and-bowl offense** under coach Kevin Young, with heavy reliance on **three-point volume** (38.2% on 25.6 attempts per game). Defensively, they employ a **switching man-to-man philosophy** focused on perimeter denial, but struggle in the paint; rebounding is a strength on the offensive glass (33.1% ORB%) thanks to athletic wings like Dybantsa and forwards like Mihailo Bošković[1][2][6].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), BYU went 7-3, including a five-game winning streak capped by a 78-72 upset win over No. 2 Kansas in the Big 12 quarterfinals before a 65-59 semifinal loss to Houston. They enter the NCAA Tournament hot off four straight wins, averaging 82.6 PPG in that span, with Dybantsa dropping 28+ in three of the last four[6].\n\n## Strengths\n\nBYU excels in **perimeter scoring and transition**, ranking top-20 nationally in adjusted offensive efficiency (116.1), overwhelming teams with Dybantsa's scoring and Wright's speed. They favor matchups against slow, perimeter-weak teams like mid-major 11-seeds or guards-heavy opponents, where their 3PT shooting and switching defense shines; Big 12 Tournament wins over Kansas and TCU highlight this[1][4][6].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside due to **poor rim protection** (104.2 AdjDEF, bottom-40 in block rate at 8.2%), they cede 52% 2PT efficiency to paint scorers. Athletic, rebounding-heavy teams like those with dominant bigs (e.g., twin towers or elite post players) trouble them, as seen in losses to Iowa State and Arizona where they were outrebounded by 12+ per game[2][6].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full health with Dybantsa (ankle tweak, cleared) and Saunders available. Coach **Kevin Young** (2nd year) brings NBA assistant experience but limited college postseason success (Sweet 16 exit last year); assistants like Will Voigt add international savvy. Historically, BYU has three Sweet 16s (1981, 2011, 2025) but no Elite Eight, with recent NBA talents like Egor Demin (No. 8 pick 2025)[3][4][6].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**AJ Dybantsa's three-point shooting**—if he sustains 37% volume from deep in March (vs. 34% late-season dip), BYU's offense becomes unstoppable; cold nights expose their paint weaknesses and cap Sweet 16 ceiling[3][4].",
