      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Tre Donaldson (Sr G, 6-3, 18.4 PPG)** leads the Hurricanes as a senior transfer with elite scoring from all three levels; his quick first step and mid-range pull-up make him dangerous in transition and isolation, averaging 4.2 assists per game while shooting 37% from three.[1][2][3]  \n**Malik Reneau (Sr F, 6-9, 16.7 PPG)** is the frontline scorer, using his soft touch around the rim and face-up game to exploit mismatches; his 8.1 rebounds and 52% field goal efficiency make him a double-double threat who punishes slower bigs.[1][2][3]  \n**Tru Washington (Jr G, 6-4, 14.2 PPG)** provides two-way versatility as a downhill attacker and perimeter defender; his prior 11.1 PPG at New Mexico translates to physical drives and 1.8 steals per game, thriving against weaker backcourts.[2][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nMiami plays at a **moderate tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing **pack-line defense** under new coach Jai Lucas to force turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limit paint scoring.[1][2] Offensively, they run **motion sets with high-low actions**, leveraging Reneau and Udeh Jr. for lobs while shooting **36.8% from three** on 22 attempts per game; rebounding is a **strength** at 38.2 boards per game, driven by Altuntas's international physicality.[1][2][3]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Miami went 9-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **semifinal run in the ACC Tournament** where they upset #3 NC State 78-72 before falling to #1 Duke 85-81 in the final.[3] Key wins: 92-84 over Virginia (Reneau's 22 pts, 12 reb) and 76-69 at Louisville; losses to elite guards in Pitt and Duke highlighted perimeter vulnerabilities.[2][3]\n\n## Strengths\n\nMiami excels in **defensive efficiency** (100.9 AdjDE), suffocating opponents inside with Udeh Jr.'s rim protection (2.4 BPG) and Altuntas's board dominance (9.5 RPG).[1][2] They thrive against **mid-major or slow-paced teams** without elite shooting, as their rebounding second-chance points (14.2 per game) and transition scoring (17.8 fast-break pts) overwhelm smaller lineups; favorable matchups include physical but undersized West Region foes.[1][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **perimeter-oriented teams** that stretch their defense, as they allow 36.1% opponent 3PT and struggle with ball-handlers (opponents average 12.4 assists vs. Miami).[2][3] **Turnover-prone guards** (15.8 TO%) and lack of elite size against dominant bigs give trouble to high-tempo, three-heavy squads like those with multiple 20+ PPG scorers.[1][2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy entering the dance.[1][3] First-year head coach **Jai Lucas** brings limited HC experience but strong assistant pedigree (Baylor, Texas Tech), while staff like Erik Pastrana adds FSU tournament know-how.[2][5] Miami's historical March Madness success is modest (5 Sweet 16s, no Final Fours, 20-24 all-time record), but their 25-8 mark and #7 seed position them for a potential second-weekend run in the wide-open West.[3]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Tru Washington's defensive intensity**—if he locks down opposing guards (holding them to 39% FG in recent wins), Miami's efficiency edge propels them deep; faltering here exposes their backcourt to exploitation.[2][3]",
