      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHawaii's top three players are **Isaac Johnson** (senior center), **Dre Bullock** (graduate guard), and **Harry Rouhliadeff** (senior forward). Johnson, an All-Big West First Team selection and Big West Tournament Most Outstanding Player, averages **14.1 points** on **.503 field goal percentage** with **5.8 rebounds** per game; his dangerous interior presence shone in the conference final with 22 points (10-of-18 FG, two 3s) and three blocks.[2][4] Bullock, an All-Big West Second Team pick and All-Tournament selection, delivers **13.5 points** and **5.8 rebounds** per game, providing versatile scoring and rebounding, capped by a game-sealing windmill dunk in the title game where he scored 15 points.[2][3][4] Rouhliadeff, also an All-Tournament honoree, contributes **10.8 points** and **5.0 rebounds** per contest, excelling on the boards with a championship double-double of 15 points and 12 rebounds.[2][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHawaii plays at a controlled tempo with a gritty, physical style emphasizing **defensive intensity** (94.9 adjusted efficiency) and **rebounding dominance**, as evidenced by a 43-39 edge (17-13 offensive) over UC Irvine in the Big West final.[4] Offensively (106.3 adjusted efficiency), they rely on **inside-out schemes** with paint scoring (34-24 advantage in final) and opportunistic transition, shooting efficiently in bursts like 15-of-30 in the second half of the title game.[4] They show solid **three-point shooting** capability through Johnson but prioritize paint attacks and blocks (10 team blocks in final).[4] **Rebounding strength** is a hallmark, fueling second-chance points.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Hawaii went 6-1 entering the tournament, with wins over Cal Poly (77-73 on Jan 15? wait, schedule shows late wins: 87-85 at Cal State Fullerton on Feb 28, 93-74 vs UC Riverside on Mar 5), but a final regular-season loss 75-84 to Long Beach State on Mar 7 (22-8 record).[5][6] They rode a tournament hot streak: double-digit semifinal win 78-63 over Cal State Fullerton, then 71-64 championship victory over UC Irvine, outscoring them 40-34 in the second half for the title—their first since 2016.[1][2][3][4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nHawaii excels in **defensive clampdowns** (held Fullerton 20 points below average, UCI to 64 points) and **rebounding battles**, particularly offensive glass (17 in final), creating second chances and paint dominance.[1][4] Their **physicality and shot-blocking** (10 blocks vs UCI) disrupt bigger teams.[4] Matchups favor them against inefficient shooters or teams weak on the glass, where their efficiency edges (106.3 off, 94.9 def) and tournament poise shine, as in splitting regular-season games with UCI before winning the decider.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nHawaii struggles against high-powered offenses or elite perimeter shooting, evident in road losses like 73-83 at UC San Diego (Jan 3) and 75-84 home to Long Beach State (Mar 7).[5][6] Limited depth shows in bench reliance (Isaiah Kerr's 10 points in final), and they can falter in close games against faster tempos, as in narrow non-conference defeats (59-60 at Oregon, 76-83 vs Arizona State).[5][6] Athletic, guard-heavy teams that control tempo give them trouble by exposing occasional shooting slumps.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo reported injuries heading into the #13 vs #4 Arkansas first-round matchup in Portland on March 19.[2] Head coach **Eran Ganot** brings strong tournament experience: 197-128 overall (194-126 at Hawaii, 11th season), with Big West Championship record of 8-10 (recently 2-0 as lower/top-two seed).[1] Program history includes limited March Madness success—first appearance since 2016 (when they won Big West under Ganot), with prior deep runs rare; this is just their second conference title ever.[3][4][6]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Rebounding and second-half execution**—Hawaii's 17 offensive boards and 40-34 second-half surge vs UCI could swing their upset bid against physical Arkansas, but failing to replicate it against SEC size determines if they advance.[4]",
