      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nQueens NC's top scorers are **Yoav Berman** (G, 6-6 SO), averaging 18.2 points per game with elite shooting from deep (42% on 7 attempts), making him dangerous as a versatile wing scorer who exploits mismatches in transition.[1][5][6] **Nasir Mann** (G, 6-5 SR) leads with 17.8 PPG, 5.2 rebounds, and 5.1 assists, thriving as a crafty playmaker who attacks the rim and facilitates for shooters.[1][5] **Chris Ashby** (G, 6-2 SR) chips in 15.4 PPG, excelling in clutch moments with pull-up jumpers and defensive steals (1.8 SPG), posing matchup problems for slower guards.[1][5][6]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nQueens plays at a moderate tempo (68 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line defensive philosophy under coach Grant Leonard that forces turnovers (18% opponent TO rate) while ranking top-3 in ASUN steals.[1][6] Offensively, they run a motion scheme heavy on three-point shooting (38% on 22 attempts, 116.7 AdjO), with strong rebounding from bigs like Gus Larson (6-10 SR) and Maban Jabriel (6-9 SO) grabbing 35% of misses.[5][6] Perimeter-oriented identity favors spacing and cuts over post play.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Queens went 8-3, including a five-game winning streak capped by an ASUN Tournament semifinal win over Florida Gulf Coast (82-75, Berman 25 pts).[5] They lost the conference title game to Stetson (78-71) but enter the NCAA Tournament hot, averaging 84 PPG and holding foes under 70 in wins.[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nQueens excels in **three-point volume and accuracy** (top-20 nationally at 38%), overwhelming teams lacking perimeter D, and rebounding battles where Larson/Jabriel dominate the glass (35.2 RPG).[6] They favor matchups against slow, iso-heavy teams, as their pack-line disrupts rhythm (114.4 AdjD) and generates 15+ steals per game.[1][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside against physical bigs (opponents score 52% in paint), with thin frontcourt depth allowing second-chance points (28% ORB allowed).[6] Athletic, up-tempo teams trouble them, as seen in losses to Wake Forest (111-73) and Arkansas (108-80), exposing poor transition D (22 fast-break PPG allowed).[5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available, including key returners Berman and Mann.[1][2] Coach Grant Leonard has zero NCAA Tournament experience (3rd year at Queens), but strong ASUN success (20-13 overall).[1] Program has no prior March Madness history as D1 (transitioned 2023), marking first appearance as #15 seed.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Yoav Berman's three-point shooting**—if he hits 40%+ from deep (as in 6/12 vs UNC Greensboro), Queens can upset; cold nights (under 30%) doom their spacing-dependent offense.[1][5]",
