      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Malachi Davis (Sr G, 18.2 PPG)**: First-Team All-NEC returner, Davis is dangerous as a versatile scorer who attacks off the dribble, hits 37% of his threes, and averages 4.1 assists, creating mismatches with his quickness and vision.[1][3]\n\n**Jamal Fuller (Gr G/F, 15.9 PPG)**: Second-Team All-NEC selection, Fuller's physicality at 6-5 makes him a threat in transition and on the boards (7.2 RPG), excelling in mid-range pull-ups and drawing fouls at 5.3 FTA per game.[1][3]\n\n**Shadrak Lasu (So F, 12.4 PPG)**: NEC All-Rookie from last year, Lasu's 6-8 frame and athleticism shine in the paint, where he grabs 6.8 rebounds and blocks 1.2 shots per game, punishing smaller defenders on lobs and putbacks.[1][2]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nLIU Brooklyn plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing gritty man-to-man defense that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a motion scheme heavy on ball movement and cutting, relying on **inside-out play** from bigs like Isaiah Miranda (7-1 C) to set up perimeter shots (35.8% 3PT, 22.5 attempts/game). Rebounding is a strength on the defensive glass (73.1% rate), fueling second-chance points.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), LIU went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season. They dominated the NEC Tournament, beating Merrimack in the semis (78-62) and Central Connecticut in the final (71-65) to claim the auto-bid, with Davis dropping 25 in the championship.[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nLIU excels in **defensive rebounding** (No. 1 in NEC) and physicality, wearing down opponents in half-court sets—their 102.4 defensive efficiency ranks top-100 nationally. They favor matchups against fast-paced teams that turn it over, as seen in wins over high-major transfers' former foes, leveraging length from Miranda and Xinyi Li (6-9 F).[2][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to elite perimeter shooting (opponents hit 36.2% from three vs. LIU) and teams with superior athleticism at guard, as their 106.7 adjusted offensive efficiency dips against switchable defenses. Squads like Big 12 athletes expose their lack of elite quickness, leading to 12 losses by double-digits.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available, including key returners. Coach Rod Strickland brings NBA pedigree (17-year pro career) but limited NCAA Tournament experience (0-1 as DePaul HC in 2021). LIU historically has zero NCAA wins (0-5 all-time appearances, last in 2011 as NEC champ).[1][4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Isaiah Miranda's rim protection**: The 7-1 transfer's ability to alter shots (2.1 BPG) and anchor the paint could extend possessions against West Region foes; if he stays out of foul trouble, LIU's defense climbs another notch.[1][2]",
