      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHoward's top three players are senior guard **Bryce Harris** (16.6 PPG, 6.9 RPG, 1.1 BPG), senior guard **Ose Okojie** (10.5 PPG, 3.7 APG), and junior guard **Cam Gillus** (10.3 PPG, 4.3 RPG, 1.8 SPG)[1][2][3][5].  \n**Bryce Harris**, a 6'4\" senior guard, is dangerous as the team's leading scorer and rebounder, shooting 48.4% from the field with elite athleticism for transition plays and rim protection (37 blocks)[3][5].  \n**Ose Okojie**, a 6'5\" senior guard, threatens with versatile scoring (56.6% FG) and playmaking (high assist totals), excelling in mid-range pull-ups and drives[3].  \n**Cam Gillus**, a 5'11\" junior guard, is a defensive menace (59 steals, 1.8 SPG) who pushes tempo with crafty handles and opportunistic scoring[2][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHoward plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (81.2 adjusted efficiency) with aggressive on-ball pressure and help rotations led by Harris and Gillus[2][5]. Offensively (90.7 adjusted efficiency), they run a **gap-attack scheme** emphasizing drives, cuts, and moderate three-point volume (34.5% team 3P%), relying on Harris's inside presence over perimeter bombing[3]. Rebounding is a **strength** (36.6 RPG, led by Harris's 6.9), fueling second-chance points in a physical MEAC style[2][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Howard went 9-1, including a dominant **seven-game winning streak** to close the regular season and MEAC Tournament[4][7]. They won the **MEAC Tournament championship** with a 78-62 title game victory over Norfolk State, outrebounding opponents by +12 and holding them under 40% FG across three tournament games[3][4].\n\n## Strengths\n\nHoward excels in **defensive disruption** (top-50 nationally in steals and blocks) and **rebounding dominance**, overwhelming smaller or slower teams with Harris's versatility and Gillus's pressure[2][3][5]. They favor matchups against inefficient shooters or guard-heavy foes, as seen in MEAC play where they forced 15+ turnovers per game while crashing the glass (1207 total rebounds)[3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **perimeter shooting teams** that stretch their defense thin (opponents shoot 29.9% from three but exploit when hot), and they struggle against elite athletic wings who match Harris's physicality[3]. High-turnover guards (team 447 TOs) get rattled by full-court pressure, as in their lone February loss to NC Central[2][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Fort Worth 1 region as a **#14 seed** (26-7 record)[4][7]. Head coach Kenneth Blakeney has **limited NCAA Tournament experience** (one prior appearance in 2023), but his 72-52 MEAC regular-season mark shows conference mastery[7]. Howard has **minimal March Madness history** (1-4 all-time, last win 1981), often exiting early as a low seed[7].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Bryce Harris's health and efficiency**—if he sustains 16+ PPG on 48% shooting without foul trouble, Howard can upset; fatigue from 32.1 MPG could doom their run[3].",
