      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Austin Nunez (Guard, Redshirt Junior, 6'2\")** averages 14.2 points per game, leading the team in scoring with his quickness and ability to create off the dribble, making him dangerous in transition and as a spot-up shooter from deep.[1][2][5]  \n**Vasean Allette (Point Guard, Junior, 6'2\")** contributes 11.8 points per game, excelling as the primary ball-handler with sharp playmaking vision and sneaky athleticism that allows him to penetrate defenses and finish at the rim.[1][4][5]  \n**Baboucarr Njie (Forward/Guard, Sophomore, 6'6\")** posts 10.5 points per game, dangerous due to his versatility in stretching the floor with threes and crashing the glass, often erupting for 20+ in key spots like his 25-point outing vs. Temple.[1][2][6]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUTSA plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing half-court execution over fast breaks, anchored by a pack-line defensive philosophy that clogs driving lanes with their lengthy wings and twin towers in **Mo Njie (6'11\" Center)** and **Stanley Borden (7'0\" Center)**.[1][2][5] Offensively, they run a motion scheme heavy on ball movement and cutting, relying moderately on three-point shooting (around 32% as a team) but excelling in rebounding strength via second-chance opportunities from their bigs.[6]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (mid-February to mid-March 2026), UTSA has gone 7-4, surging with a four-game winning streak into the AAC Tournament before dropping a quarterfinal heartbreaker to UAB 73-83 on March 13.[6] They finished conference play 8-10 overall but peaked late, including gritty home wins over Temple (64-70 loss avenged in rematch simulation) and strong showings from guards in low-scoring grinds.[6]\n\n## Strengths\n\nUTSA thrives in low-possession, physical games where their **defensive efficiency (83.5)** shines, forcing turnovers and limiting paint scores with Njie and Borden's rim protection.[user-provided] They favor matchups against inefficient shooters or teams lacking size, as their rebounding edge (top-100 nationally) leads to extra possessions against smaller lineups like high-tempo guards without interior help.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter against elite shooting guards who exploit their slower bigs in pick-and-rolls, with **adjusted offensive efficiency (84.8)** hampered by poor half-court creation beyond Nunez.[user-provided] Teams with athletic wings and switchable forwards—like those in the Fort Worth region—give them trouble by disrupting their motion offense and outrunning them in transition.[6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full roster available including key transfers like **Mo Njie (ex-SMU)**.[1][2] Second-year head coach Austin Claunch brings limited NCAA experience (0-0 in Dance), but his AAC rebuild shows promise.[6] UTSA's program history in March Madness is barren—no wins since joining D1 in 1986, with just two appearances as heavy underdogs.[6]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Austin Nunez's three-point volume**—if he heats up beyond his 34% clip (16 high vs. Charlotte), it unlocks spacing for Allette drives and Njie post-ups, potentially stealing a First Round upset; cold nights doom their stagnant offense.[1][6]",
