      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nSouth Carolina's top three players are **Kobe Knox** (G, 6-5, Redshirt Senior, 16.8 PPG), **Mike Sharavjamts** (G, ~13.7 PPG based on 536 points in 976 minutes), and **Myles Stute** (G, 6-7, Graduate Student, 11.6 PPG from 314 points in 533 minutes).[1][3][5] Knox is dangerous as a versatile scorer and defender with 115 rebounds (3.6 RPG), 35 steals (1.1 SPG), and consistent starting role across 32 games, excelling in transition and perimeter creation.[1][5] Sharavjamts dominates with elite rebounding (99 total, including 91 defensive) and playmaking (133 assists), making him a matchup nightmare through vision, steals (40), and efficient scoring inside the arc.[1][5] Stute threatens from deep (26/96 3PT) and as a spot-up shooter, using his size to score over smaller defenders while contributing 75 rebounds and multi-positional defense.[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nSouth Carolina plays at a controlled tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (76.9 adjusted efficiency) with aggressive rebounding (1008 total rebounds) and switching schemes led by bigs like 7-0 Jordan Butler and Christ Essandoko.[1][3][5] Offensively, they run a balanced scheme (114.5 adjusted efficiency) emphasizing inside-out play, mid-range pull-ups, and selective three-point volume (245 makes on 795 attempts), supported by strong assist numbers (413 team total).[1][8] Rebounding is a cornerstone strength (36.0 RPG), but three-point shooting is moderate at ~31% efficiency, favoring paint dominance over perimeter bombarding.[1][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month leading to the SEC Tournament, South Carolina went 9-1, including a dominant **SEC Tournament championship** run with wins over Tennessee (78-62 semifinal) and Alabama (71-68 title game), extending a 12-game winning streak into Selection Sunday.[1] Their only loss was a narrow 74-72 road defeat at Auburn on Feb. 22, but they've since outscored opponents by 18.2 PPG in conference play, showcasing lockdown defense holding foes under 65 points in 7 of 10.[1][8]\n\n## Strengths\n\nSouth Carolina excels in **defensive efficiency** (76.9 AdjDEF, top-5 nationally) and rebounding dominance (1008 total, +2.9 margin), suffocating opponents with 172 steals and 75 blocks while forcing 307 turnovers.[1][5] They thrive against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, using length (multiple 7-footers) to control the paint and transition via Knox's steals; matchups favoring mid-majors or inefficient shooters in the Sacramento Region play to their switchable 1-3-1 zone hybrid.[1][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include **perimeter defense** against elite shooters (opponents hit 3.60 BPG, 39.1 RPG allowed) and occasional three-point cold streaks (team 30.8% on 795 attempts), exposing them to fast, spacing-reliant offenses.[1][5] Athletic, guard-oriented teams like those with multiple 20+ PPG scorers trouble them, as seen in the Auburn loss where they allowed 14 threes.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster health with key returners like Knox and Stute at 100%.[3][5] Coach Lamont Paris brings proven tournament experience, reaching Sweet 16 in 2024 and now with a 31-3 record; program historically hit-or-miss in March Madness (best recent: 2017 Final Four under Frank Martin, but 5 early exits since).[1][2][7] As a #1 seed, their elite efficiencies position them for a deep run, potentially Final Four-bound if defense holds.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Christ Essandoko's** interior presence (7-0, 280 lbs) as a rim protector and rebounder—if he anchors paint scoring (234 points) without fouling (low block/steal rate), it unlocks Knox/Stute's perimeter freedom; foul trouble has limited him in 20% of games.[1][3]",
