      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nIowa's top three players anchor a balanced attack led by transfers from Drake University under new coach Ben McCollum.\n\n- **Tavion Banks (G/F, Senior, 6'7\")**: Averages 18.2 PPG, dangerous with versatile scoring inside and out, elite rebounding for his size (7.1 RPG), and playmaking (4.2 APG) that exploits mismatches[1][4][5].\n- **Cam Manyawu (F, Junior, 6'9\")**: Posts 15.4 PPG and 8.3 RPG, lethal in the paint with physical post moves and high-efficiency finishing (62% FG near rim), dominating smaller Big Ten forwards[1][5].\n- **Kael Combs (G, Junior, 6'4\")**: Leads at 16.7 PPG on 42% 3PT, a sharpshooting threat off screens who stretches defenses and thrives in transition (3.8 APG)[1][3][5].\n\n## Team Identity\nIowa plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line **defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% opp. TO rate) and protects the rim. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with heavy ball movement (62 assists per 100 possessions), elite **three-point shooting** (38.4% on 22.5 attempts/game), and strong **rebounding** (36.8 RPG, top-20 nationally) via length across positions[1][4].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Iowa went 8-1, including a six-game winning streak capped by a Big Ten Tournament semifinal run (lost to Purdue 78-72 in semis after beating Indiana and Nebraska). They averaged 82.3 PPG while holding foes to 65.1 PPG, with Banks and Combs combining for 62% of scoring in wins[1][4].\n\n## Strengths\nIowa excels in **defensive efficiency** (88.1 AdjDE, elite rim protection at 48% opp. 2PT) and **offensive balance** (103.8 AdjOE), overwhelming slower teams with size (Manyawu/Folgueiras frontcourt duo) and perimeter shooting. They favor matchups against guard-heavy or poor-rebounding squads, outrebounding opponents by +5.2 in wins[1][4].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **pace-and-space teams** that push tempo (Iowa -4.1 net rating in losses above 72 possessions) and elite perimeter defenders disrupting Combs/Banks (35% 3PT in defeats). Frontcourt depth thins if fouled, allowing second-chance points (opp. 14.2 ORPG)[1][5].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with Banks cleared from minor ankle tweak. Coach **Ben McCollum** brings D1 experience from Drake (multiple NCAA bids) but limited March success (0-2 as head coach). Iowa's historical March Madness record includes five Sweet 16s since 1980 but no Final Four since 1980, with recent #2 seeds flaming out early (e.g., 2022 Elite Eight)[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n**Brendan Hausen's bench shooting** (14.1 PPG off bench, 41% 3PT)—if he heats up in Sacramento's wide-open region, Iowa's offense becomes unstoppable; cold streaks have doomed them in tight games[1][5].",
