      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTCU's top scorers are **David Punch (F, 18.2 PPG)**, a versatile forward who dominates inside with explosive athleticism and rebounding prowess, making him dangerous in transition and on putbacks; **Xavier Edmonds (F, 16.5 PPG)**, a physical junior transfer from Salt Lake CC who excels in the mid-range and post-ups with his 6-8 frame and strength, posing matchup problems for smaller defenders; and **Jayden Pierre (G, 15.1 PPG)**, the senior point guard from Providence who orchestrates the offense with elite vision (6.2 APG) and pull-up jumpers, lethal in pick-and-roll situations.[1][2][4][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTCU plays at a **deliberate tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.4% opponent turnover rate) and suffocates drives with their length. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** focused on paint touches and cuts, ranking elite in two-point field goal percentage (58.2%), while being opportunistic from three (36.1% on 22.4 attempts per game). Rebounding is a **strength** on the defensive glass (76.2% defensive rebound rate), led by their bigs.[1][2][4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), TCU went 9-1, including a dominant **7-game winning streak** to close the regular season and a **Big 12 Tournament championship** (beat Kansas in semis 82-71, Houston in final 78-65). Their lone loss was a narrow road defeat at Baylor (74-72).[2][4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nTCU excels in **elite defense** (77.9 AdjDEF, No. 2 nationally), smothering opponents with length and activity to limit second-chance points. They thrive against **mid-major or guard-heavy teams** without size, using forwards like Punch and Edmonds to bully the glass and force 3-and-D lineups into uncomfortable matchups in the Sacramento Region.[1][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **high-powered perimeter shooting teams** that stretch their pack-line (opponents shoot 35.8% from three in losses), and they struggle with **pace-and-space offenses** due to limited elite rim protection (blocks per game: 4.2). Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) has hurt in close games.[4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the dance. Head coach has **strong March Madness experience** (multiple Sweet 16s in prior stops), and TCU's program history includes a 2018 run to the Elite Eight but recent early exits (second round max since). As a **#3 seed**, their 29-5 record and top efficiencies position them for a deep run, potentially to the Sweet 16.[1][2][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jayden Pierre's playmaking in crunch time**—if he exceeds 7 APG and limits turnovers (<2.5), TCU's half-court execution elevates; otherwise, stagnant offense dooms them against region foes.[2][4][5]",
