      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nOklahoma's top three players are **Nijel Pack** (PG, Sr., 5-10, 188 lbs), **Xzayvier Brown** (G, Jr., 6-2, 182 lbs), and **Tae Davis** (F, Sr., 6-9, 215 lbs). Pack leads the team at **17.8 PPG**, dangerous as a quick, crafty ball-handler who exploits mismatches with pull-up jumpers and elite court vision, averaging 5.2 APG[1][2][3]. Brown contributes **15.4 PPG**, thriving as a combo guard with explosive drives and 38% three-point shooting, making him lethal in transition[1][2]. Davis anchors scoring at **14.2 PPG** and **8.1 RPG**, a versatile forward who dominates inside with post moves and shot-blocking, posing matchup problems for smaller bigs[1][2][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nOklahoma plays at a **moderate tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and protects the rim with length. Offensively, they run a **gap-drive scheme** focused on ball movement and cutting, ranking top-20 in assists per field goal. They attempt **38% of shots from three** at 36.5% efficiency, while dominating rebounding with a +6.2 margin led by bigs like Kirill Elatontsev[1][5].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17–March 17, 2026), Oklahoma went **8-2**, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **SEC Tournament semifinal win** over Texas A&M (78-72) before falling to top-seed Alabama in the final (85-79). They averaged 82.5 PPG in wins, holding foes under 65[1][5].\n\n## Strengths\n\nOklahoma excels in **elite defense** (81.6 AdjDEF, No. 8 nationally), suffocating opponents with switchable length from wings like Jeff Nwankwo (6-6 G) and bigs like Andreas Holst (7-0 F). Their **rebounding** (+6.2 margin) and transition scoring (1.18 PPP) favor matchups against slow, perimeter-heavy teams lacking size, as seen in wins over Kentucky and LSU[1][3][5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **athletic, guard-dominant teams** that push tempo and attack Pack's size (5-10), as evidenced by losses to quick backcourts like Vanderbilt (92-84). Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) and occasional three-point droughts (under 30% in 4 losses) expose them to physical, half-court grinders[1][5].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; Pack is fully healthy after a minor ankle tweak. Coach Jennie Baranczyk has **four NCAA appearances** (best: 2022 Sweet 16), bringing portal-savvy roster building. Oklahoma's history includes **six Final Fours** (last: 2016), but recent Sooners exited early (2024: Round of 32); as a #4 seed, their defense positions them for a Sweet 16 run in the Sacramento Region[1][5][7].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Nijel Pack's three-point volume**: If Pack sustains 40%+ from deep (his season 37.2%), Oklahoma's spacing unlocks Davis inside-out; cold nights (under 30%) stall their offense against elite defenses.",
