      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nMichigan State's top three players are **Jaxon Kohler** (Sr. F, 6'10\", 12.7 PPG, 9.1 RPG), **Kur Teng** (So. G, 6'5\", 7.7 PPG, 38.1% 3PT), and **Carson Cooper** (Sr. C, 6'11\", 7.0 RPG, 0.9 BPG). Kohler dominates inside with elite rebounding (290 total rebounds, 97 offensive) and efficient scoring (.510 FG), making him dangerous in the paint against smaller fronts[1][3][4]. Teng stretches defenses as a sharpshooter (53 made 3s), thriving in transition and spot-up situations[1][3]. Cooper anchors the rim with 30 blocks and 225 rebounds, punishing opponents on the glass while protecting against drives[1][4].\n\n## Team Identity\nMichigan State plays at a controlled tempo (18.5 APG, #6 nationally), emphasizing **elite defense** (88.4 AdjDE) with physical man-to-man pressure and help rotations that limit opponents to 31.2 RPG[7]. Offensively, they run a motion scheme (111.3 AdjOE) focused on inside-out play, rebounding dominance (39.8 RPG, #17), and moderate three-point volume (35.9% on 682 attempts), leveraging size for second-chance points (407 offensive rebounds)[1][3][7].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month, Michigan State went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a Big Ten Tournament semifinal run (lost final to Purdue). They averaged 82 PPG in wins, holding foes under 65 in four straight before the title game, showcasing defensive clampdowns in high-stakes matchups[1].\n\n## Strengths\nThe Spartans excel in **rebounding** (1275 total, +278 margin) and defense, suffocating teams with length and physicality (164 steals, 131 blocks). They favor matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, where Kohler's boards and Cooper's rim protection force turnovers (opponents 6.5 APG allowed) and easy buckets[1][3][4][7].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to elite perimeter shooting and speed, as their 35.9% opponent 3PT defense falters against hot shooters; teams with strong pick-and-roll guards exploit turnovers (368 team TOs). Quick, athletic squads trouble their deliberate pace[1][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering as #5 seed. Coach **Tom Izzo** (31st season) brings unmatched March Madness experience (8 Final Fours, 2019 title). Program history includes consistent deep runs (6 Elite Eights since 2000), thriving in bracket play with 22-8 record[5].\n\n## X-Factor\n**Jaxon Kohler's rebounding**—if he sustains 9+ RPG against athletic bigs, MSU controls tempo and paints; foul trouble or poor second-chance efficiency dooms them.",
