      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nGeorgia's top three players entering the tournament are **Somtochukwu \"Somto\" Cyril** (C, 6-11, Sophomore), **Kanon Catchings** (F, 6-9, Sophomore), and **Blue Cain** (G, 6-5, Junior).[1][2][4]\n\n- **Somto Cyril** averages 14.2 points, 9.1 rebounds, and 2.3 blocks per game, dangerous with his interior dominance, rim protection, and soft touch around the basket from Overtime Elite pedigree.[1][2]\n- **Kanon Catchings** posts 12.8 points and 6.4 rebounds, excelling as a versatile forward with stretch-four shooting (38% from three) and athleticism inherited from his NBA father.[1][4]\n- **Blue Cain** leads guards at 11.5 points and 4.2 assists, a sharpshooting threat (36.7% 3PT) who creates off the dribble and thrives in transition.[1][2]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nGeorgia plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Mike White that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and elite rim protection.[1]\n\nOffensively, they run a **gap-drive-and-kick scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.1% team 3PT, 22.5 attempts/game) and dominant **rebounding** (No. 12 nationally at 39.2 RPG), leveraging size inside.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17, 2026), Georgia went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **SEC Tournament quarterfinal win** over Vanderbilt (78-65) before falling in semis to Alabama (72-69).[1]\n\nThey averaged 76.4 PPG offensively and held foes to 62.1 PPG, with Cyril dominating boards in wins over Kentucky (82-71) and Auburn (68-59).[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nGeorgia excels in **defensive efficiency** (84.4 AdjDEF, top-15 nationally) and **rebounding**, winning the battle on the glass in 75% of victories, ideal against smaller, perimeter-oriented teams.[1]\n\nMatchups favor them versus guard-heavy squads unable to match frontcourt size, as seen in SEC wins where they outrebounded foes by +12.3 per game.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite perimeter shooting** and **fast-paced offenses** that stretch their guards thin, losing all three games to top-50 tempo teams (e.g., 88-76 loss to Tennessee).[1]\n\nTurnover-prone guards (15.8 TOPG) struggle against pressure defenses, and limited bench depth (top-7 play 85% of minutes) exposes them in foul trouble.[1][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Cyril and Catchings at 100%.[1]\n\nCoach **Mike White** brings proven tournament experience (Florida Final Fours in 2014, 2015; 5 NCAA appearances), but Georgia's program history is modest (last Sweet 16: 2008; 2-4 in last decade).[1][5]\n\nAs a #7 seed, they project as a Round of 32 ceiling team in the tough Sacramento region.[1]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Somto Cyril's rim protection and rebounding**—if he logs 30+ MPG without fouling (as in SEC tourney), Georgia controls paint vs. mid-major chaos; otherwise, they leak easy buckets.[1][2]",
