      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nClemson's top three players are **Nick Davidson (F/C, Senior)** averaging 7.5 PPG with 112 FG made and 29 3PM, dangerous as an efficient inside-out scorer (.519 FG%, .354 3P%) and rebounder (3.8 RPG, 128 total rebounds).[1][3] **Carter Welling (F, Junior)** leads at 10.3 PPG (186 points) with elite rebounding (5.4 RPG, 174 total) and defense (27 steals, 24 blocks), excelling in transition and paint protection.[3][4] **RJ Godfrey (F, Senior)** contributes 11.9 PPG (406 points) as a versatile forward with strong rebounding (5.3 RPG, 180 total) and finishing (59 ORB), thriving on second-chance opportunities.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nClemson plays at a controlled tempo, emphasizing **defensive efficiency** (87.2 AdjDEF) with pack-line principles under Brad Brownell, forcing 6.3 SPG and limiting opponents to 66.2 PPG allowed.[4][7] Offensively (100.6 AdjO), they run a balanced scheme mixing inside scoring (37.1 RPG) and perimeter shooting (279 3PM at moderate volume), ranking strong in rebounding margins (+1.9) but average in three-point volume (818 attempts).[1][7]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February-March 2026), Clemson went 7-3, including a four-game win streak capped by a **semifinal loss in the ACC Tournament** to a conference rival, securing their 21-11 record (noting slight variance to 24-10 overall in some logs).[3][5] They split road/neutral games but dominated home rebounding battles, outrebounding foes by +8 per game in wins.[4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nClemson excels in **rebounding dominance** (1166 total rebounds, +64 margin over opponents) and defensive disruption (215 steals, 96 blocks), suffocating teams inside.[1][4] They favor **matchups against inefficient shooters or weak rebounders**, where Nick Davidson and Carter Welling's paint control (combined 5.4 ORB/G) creates easy buckets, as seen in ACC wins over bottom-half teams.[3][7]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter against high-volume three-point teams (opponents shot well beyond their 3P%), with turnover issues (319 total TOs) and modest scoring (74.4 PPG).[1][7] **Elite guards or fast-paced offenses** trouble them, exposing limited backcourt depth beyond Jestin Porter (1.2 SPG but 1.8 RPG).[4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Sacramento Region as a **#8 seed**; full rotation available.[2] Coach **Brad Brownell** (16th season) brings proven tournament experience, with Clemson reaching the Elite Eight in 2021-22 and Sweet 16 in 2018.[5] Historically, the program has three Elite Eight appearances (1980, 2010, 2021-22) but no Final Fours, often exiting early as midseeds.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Carter Welling's defensive versatility**—if his 0.8 BPG and rebounding translate against Sacramento's athletic forwards, Clemson advances; foul trouble could derail their paint edge.[3][4]",
