      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nUSC's top three players are **Terrance Williams II** (F, Sr., 6'7\", 14.8 PPG), **Chad Baker-Mazara** (G, Sr., 6'7\", 13.2 PPG), and **Jacob Cofie** (F, So., 6'10\", 11.5 PPG). Williams II is dangerous as a versatile forward who excels in transition and rebounding (7.2 RPG), using his strength to score inside and stretch defenses with 38% three-point shooting. Baker-Mazara threatens with explosive scoring off the dribble and elite perimeter defense, averaging 4.1 assists while disrupting passing lanes (1.8 SPG). Cofie dominates the paint as a mobile big, finishing lobs efficiently (62% FG) and protecting the rim (1.6 BPG).[4][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUSC plays at a **mid-tempo pace** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under Eric Musselman that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **motion-based scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (36.8% on 22.4 attempts per game) and strong **rebounding** (38.1 RPG, top-40 nationally), transitioning quickly off misses via wings like Williams II.[4][5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17–March 17, 2026), USC went 5-4, including a three-game winning streak capped by a 78-72 upset over Michigan in the Big Ten Tournament first round before a 65-59 quarterfinal loss to Purdue. They averaged 75.3 PPG in wins but struggled with 68.1 PPG in losses, shooting 42% from the field overall amid road fatigue.[4][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nUSC excels in **defensive efficiency** (86.3 AdjDE, elite top-25 mark), suffocating opponents with length and switchability, holding foes to 39% FG. They thrive against **mid-major or slower-paced teams** without elite rim protection, dominating rebounds (+4.2 margin) and forcing 15+ turnovers, as seen in their streak-clinching win over Rutgers (82-67).[4][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the **offensive end** (96.0 AdjOE, bottom-half Big Ten), USC struggles with half-court creation against **zone defenses or athletic perimeter teams**, turning it over 16.8% and shooting 32% from three in losses. Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) and frontcourt depth issues expose them to physical, rebounding squads like Indiana.[4][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available, though Gabe Dynes (C, 7'5\") is questionable with a minor ankle tweak. Coach **Eric Musselman** brings proven tournament experience (8 NCAA appearances, 5 Sweet 16s at Arkansas/Nevada). USC's March Madness history includes 26 appearances but modest success (6-9 since 2010, best: Elite Eight in 2021); as a #9 seed, they face upset potential in Sacramento Region.[4][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Three-point shooting variance**: USC's fate hinges on knocking down 35%+ from deep (vs. 29% season average in tournament spots); hot nights from Baker-Mazara and Williams II could propel a run, but bricks expose their offensive flaws against balanced #8 seeds.[4][5]",
