      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Ugonna Onyenso (C, 7'0\" Senior)** averages 12.4 points and 8.7 rebounds per game, making him dangerous with his rim protection (2.1 blocks/game) and efficient finishing around the basket in Virginia's pack-line defense.[1][2][4]  \n**Malik Thomas (G, 6'5\" Graduate Student)** leads the team at 14.2 points per game with strong perimeter shooting (38% from three), excelling in spot-up situations and transition pushes.[1][4][5]  \n**Devin Tillis (F, 6'7\" Graduate Student)** contributes 11.8 points and 6.2 rebounds, dangerous as a versatile scorer who attacks mismatches and crashes the glass effectively.[1][2][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nVirginia plays at a slow tempo (64.2 possessions per game), emphasizing Tony Bennett's pack-line defensive philosophy adapted under new coach Griff Aldrich, focusing on ball denial and help rotations to force turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate).[4] Offensively, they run a deliberate motion scheme with high-low actions involving bigs like Onyenso, moderate three-point volume (32.1% attempts, 35.8% accuracy), and elite rebounding strength (No. 12 nationally in defensive rebounding percentage at 78.4%).[1][4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 15-March 15, 2026), Virginia went 7-3, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season but dropping two of three in the ACC Tournament, where they lost in the quarterfinals to Duke 68-62 after beating NC State in the second round.[4] Key wins featured dominant defense, holding opponents under 65 points in four of the seven victories.\n\n## Strengths\n\nVirginia's elite **defensive efficiency (88.2)** suffocates opponents through length and discipline, winning 78% of games when holding foes below 90 points.[1][4] They excel against guard-heavy teams lacking size, as Onyenso and Tillis dominate the paint (opponents shoot 41.2% inside), and matchups favoring slower, half-court offenses play into their controlled pace.[2][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerabilities include **adjusted offensive efficiency (103.2)**, struggling against athletic, switching defenses that disrupt their motion (28% effective FG% in losses to top-50 teams).[1][4] Quick-transition teams with strong perimeter defense and three-point volume give them trouble, exposing poor athleticism and turnover issues (15.4% rate).[2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; Onyenso fully healthy after minor ankle tweak in February.[4][5] Coach Griff Aldrich (1st season) lacks deep tournament experience but inherits Bennett's system; assistants Matt Henry and Bryce Crawford add March prep knowledge.[4] Virginia's historical success includes the 2019 national title and 7 Elite Eight appearances, but recent early exits (2023, 2024) temper expectations as a #10 seed.[4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Malik Thomas's three-point shooting**—if he heats up (over 40% from deep in wins), it stretches defenses and opens driving lanes; cold streaks (under 30%) force reliance on stagnant inside scoring, dooming upsets in the Sacramento Region.[1][5]",
