      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Carey Booth (F, Jr., 6-10, 215 lbs)** averages 17.2 points per game, leading the Rams with his versatile inside-out scoring, elite rim protection (2.1 blocks/game), and 38% three-point shooting on 4.2 attempts, making him dangerous as a mismatch hunter against smaller defenders.[2][3][5]\n\n**Nikola Djapa (C, Jr., 6-11, 235 lbs)** posts 14.8 points and 9.2 rebounds per game, dominating with his physical post presence, 62% field goal efficiency, and ability to seal defenders for lobs, posing matchup problems for teams lacking size.[2][3][5]\n\n**Jase Butler (G, Soph., 6-4, 185 lbs)** contributes 12.5 points, 4.1 assists, and 1.8 steals per game, excelling as a quick-twitch slasher and spot-up shooter (36% from three), dangerous in transition and pick-and-roll sets.[2][3][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nColorado State plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Niko Medved that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and suffocates paint scoring (42.1 points in paint allowed). Offensively, they run a **motion-based scheme** with heavy **three-point volume** (38.4% of shots, 35.2% accuracy) and dominant **rebounding** (37.8 rebounds per game, +5.2 margin), leveraging size for second-chance points (14.6/game).\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 15–March 15, 2026), the Rams went 9-1, including a seven-game winning streak capped by a **Mountain West Tournament championship** (85-72 win over Boise State in the final). Key wins: 78-65 at San Diego State (Feb 28) and 92-81 vs. Utah State (Mar 8), with their lone loss a 71-69 upset at Fresno State (Mar 5).[3]\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in **elite defense** (82.5 AdjDEF, top-15 nationally), smothering opponents inside (opponents shoot 47.2% at rim) and on the glass (+5.2 rebound margin). **Matchups favoring them** include teams reliant on perimeter scoring or lacking frontcourt depth, like guards-heavy mid-majors or injury-plagued power forwards in the Sacramento Region—ideal vs. #5 seeds with thin bigs.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **explosive, athletic guards** who attack gaps (opponents' 1.12 PPP in transition) and teams with switchable wings that neutralize their size advantage. **High-tempo offenses** (70+ possessions) trouble them, as seen in the Fresno State loss where they allowed 76 possessions and 15.2 turnovers.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the dance. Coach **Niko Medved** brings solid tournament experience (3-4 NCAA record, 2024 First Round upset win). Historically, Colorado State has limited March Madness success (6-3 all-time, last win 2024; no Sweet 16 since 1969), but their 26-7 record and MWC title position them for a potential First Round stunner as a #12 seed.[3][4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Carey Booth's three-point volume**: If he sustains 4+ attempts at 38%+ efficiency against switching defenses, it stretches floors and creates driving lanes; cold nights (under 30%) expose their halfcourt stagnation.",
