      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nIdaho's top three players are **Isaiah Brickner** (Senior G, 6'4\", 19.2 PPG), **Kolton Mitchell** (Redshirt Sophomore G, 6'1\", 17.8 PPG), and **Trevon Blassingame** (Redshirt Junior G, 6'4\", 15.4 PPG). Brickner is dangerous as a versatile scorer and playmaker, leading the team in assists (5.2 APG) while shooting 38% from three on high volume, excelling in pick-and-rolls and off-ball movement[1][2][4]. Mitchell, a shifty point guard with Big Sky roots, threatens with explosive drives and transition speed, averaging 4.1 APG and converting 52% of his two-point attempts despite his size[1][2][4]. Blassingame stands out as a combo guard with length, using his 6'4\" frame for rebounding (6.2 RPG) and mid-range pull-ups, making him a mismatch nightmare in isolation[1][2][7].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nIdaho plays at a **mid-tempo pace** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and protects the rim with length from wings and bigs like 6'11\" Brody Rowbury. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with heavy ball movement (62 assists per 100 possessions) and **elite three-point shooting** (39.2% on 22.4 attempts per game), fueled by guard-heavy lineups. Rebounding is a **strength on the defensive glass** (73.4% defensive rebound rate), but they struggle offensively against physical fronts[1][2][4][8].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Idaho went 9-1, including a dominant **seven-game winning streak** to close the regular season and a **Big Sky Tournament championship** run, beating Portland State 78-62 in the final after knocking off Montana State in the semis (85-71). Key wins featured Brickner's 28-point explosion vs. Eastern Washington and Mitchell's 22-point, 8-assist outing in the title game; their lone loss was a 71-69 upset at home to Weber State[5][8][9][10].\n\n## Strengths\n\nIdaho excels in **perimeter defense and transition scoring**, holding opponents to 29.1% from three while generating 17.4 fast-break points per game, ideal against inefficient-shooting mid-majors. Their **size on the wings** (multiple 6'4\"+ guards) favors matchups vs. smaller guards or teams lacking length, as seen in conference wins over Northern Colorado (88-76) where they outrebounded by 12. With top-50 adjusted efficiencies, they thrive vs. deliberate, half-court offenses[1][4][5][8].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable inside due to thin frontcourt depth beyond Rowbury and 6'9\" Ewan Steele, allowing 38.2 points in the paint per game against physical bigs. Slow half-court creation falters vs. aggressive trapping defenses (14.8% turnover rate), and poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) hurts in close games. Athletic, switchable teams like those with versatile wings give them trouble, as evidenced by early-season losses to Gonzaga and Saint Mary's[1][2][4][8].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the dance with Brickner cleared after a minor ankle tweak. Coach Alex Pribble (3rd season, 27-5 record) has zero NCAA Tournament experience, but his .844 winning percentage includes a Big Sky title. Historically, Idaho has minimal March Madness success—last appearance 2019 (0-1 as 15-seed), program record 2-8 all-time, often exiting early as underdogs[1][4][5].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Kolton Mitchell's ability to penetrate and distribute** in up-tempo tournament games; his 2.1 turnovers per game balloon to 3.5 against elite athleticism, but when under 2.0 (as in the conference tourney), Idaho's offense hums at 1.15 points per possession[1][2][4].",
