      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Emanuel Prospere II (G/F, 6'5\" Graduate Student)** averages 16.8 points per game, dangerous due to his versatile scoring as a transfer from Missouri-St. Louis, excelling in transition and mid-range pull-ups with improved 3-point shooting at 37%.[1][2][5]  \n**Leo Beath (F, 6'8\" Redshirt Junior)** leads with 17.2 points and 6.6 rebounds per game (398 points on 141/323 FG), a mismatch nightmare inside with soft touch (49/139 3PT) and rebounding prowess from Lynn University transfer.[1][2][7]  \n**Bol Dengdit (F, 6'11\" Redshirt Junior)** posts 15.0 points and 7.1 rebounds (354 points on 131/267 FG), lethal with length blocking shots (17 BLK) and finishing through contact from Portland background.[1][2][7]\n\n## Team Identity\nUC San Diego runs a deliberate **half-court offense** emphasizing spacing and 3-point volume (top Big West in attempts), paired with an elite **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers and limits paint access, reflected in their 84.5 adjusted defensive efficiency. They play at a controlled tempo (68 possessions/game), rank mid-tier in rebounding thanks to bigs like Dengdit and 7'0\" Dimitrije Vukicevic, but thrive on perimeter shooting (35.8% from deep).[7]\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17, 2026), the Tritons went 8-2, including a six-game win streak capped by a **Big West Tournament championship** (85-72 win over UC Irvine in final after semis vs. Long Beach State). Key wins: 78-65 at Hawai'i, 92-88 OT vs. UC Davis; losses to top non-con foes.[7]\n\n## Strengths\nThey excel in **defensive efficiency** (84.5 AdjD, No. 1 Big West), suffocating opponents with length (Dengdit/Vukicevic rim protection) and guards forcing 15% TO rate. **Matchups favoring them**: Slow, half-court teams without elite athleticism (e.g., vs. #3 Sacramento region higher seeds reliant on isos), where their spacing and Beath/Prospere cutting wear down defenses.[7]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **athletic, transition-heavy teams** that exploit their average rebounding (35.2 RPG, weak on offensive glass) and push tempo, leading to fatigue. Perimeter defense wavers against 40%+ 3PT shooters, and foul trouble plagues bigs (Dengdit 58 TOs).[7]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo reported injuries entering the #14 seed vs. Sacramento 4 region. Coach **Clint Allard** has zero NCAA Tournament experience (4th year, prior CIT runs), and UCSD has **no historical March Madness success** (first appearance ever as D1 since 2000, 0-0 record).[1] Upset potential limited by inexperience, but 23-8 record and 95.2 AdjO suggest First Round fight.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Tyson Dunn's (G, 6'3\" Gr. Student) playmaking** (13.7 PPG, 51 AST in limited minutes)—if he disrupts as secondary creator (low TO at 33), it unlocks Beath/Dengdit; cold nights expose backcourt depth.[2][7]",
