      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nFairleigh Dickinson's top scorers are **Josiah Francis** (G, 6'7\" FR), averaging 18.2 points per game with elite perimeter shooting (42% from three) that stretches defenses thin; **Chidube Ekwommadu** (G, 6'4\" FR), posting 16.8 PPG on explosive drives and transition plays, dangerous in transition with quick first steps; and **Bismark Nsiah** (F, 6'8\" SR), contributing 14.5 PPG and 9.2 rebounds, lethal inside via physical post-ups and offensive rebounding putbacks.[1][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nFDU plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68 possessions per game), anchored by a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under coach Jack Castleberry that clogs driving lanes and forces turnovers (18% opponent turnover rate). Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** emphasizing **high-volume three-point shooting** (38% on 25 attempts per game) and **dominant rebounding** (39.2 boards per game, top in NEC), fueling second-chance points.[5]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17, 2026), FDU went 12-1, including a **10-game winning streak** capped by a NEC Tournament semifinal win over Central Connecticut (82-71) before falling in the championship to LIU (78-75). They averaged 85.3 PPG in this stretch, holding foes to 62.1.[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nFDU excels in **elite defense** (79.3 AdjDEF, No. 5 nationally) and **rebounding**, overwhelming smaller teams on the glass while their three-point barrage punishes sagging bigs. They favor matchups against **up-tempo, perimeter-heavy teams** lacking size, as seen in their 28-point rout of Stonehill.[5]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **physical, half-court grinders** with strong interior scoring, as their thin frontcourt (average height 6'5\") struggles against dominant post players—evident in early losses to Army and Fordham. Poor free-throw shooting (68%) and occasional three-point droughts (sub-30% in 4 losses) expose them.[1][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available for #15 seed vs. Sacramento Region opener. Coach **Jack Castleberry** has solid tournament experience, guiding FDU to the 2023 First Four upset as an 16-seed. Historically, FDU's March Madness success is limited (2-5 all-time), but their 2023 Cinderella run signals upset potential against sloppy high seeds.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Josiah Francis's three-point volume**: If the freshman hits 10+ attempts at 40%+ efficiency, FDU's spacing forces help rotations, unlocking drives and rebounding; cold nights doom their offense in a potential First Round battle.[1][5]",
