      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nSouthern University's top scorers are **Jayce Depron** (G, 6-3, So.), averaging 14.2 points per game with his quick first step and 37% three-point shooting on 4.8 attempts, making him dangerous as a pull-up threat off the dribble[1][2][3]. **DaMariee Jones** (F, 6-5, Sr.) leads with 12.8 PPG, excelling in transition and mid-range pull-ups (45% FG), using his length to finish over defenders[1][2]. **Brandon Hardy** (G, 6-3, Jr.) chips in 11.5 PPG, dangerous via off-ball movement and spot-up threes (35% from deep), creating spacing for drives[1][2].\n\n## Team Identity\nSouthern plays at a deliberate **mid-tempo** (68 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line **defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving forwards like Jones and Dixon Jr., relying on **moderate three-point volume** (32% of shots, 34% accuracy) but dominating the glass with a +4.2 rebounding margin led by bigs Abdelgowad and Bell[1][2][3].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17-Mar 17), Southern went 6-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season but dropping two of three in the SWAC Tournament, losing the championship 72-68 to Texas Southern after beating Grambling in semis (81-75)[1][6]. They shot 44% from the field in wins but struggled with 15 turnovers per game in losses.\n\n## Strengths\nSouthern excels in **defensive efficiency** (83.5 AdjDEF), clamping guards with perimeter pressure from Depron and Hardy while forwards like **Terrance Dixon Jr.** (6-8 Sr.) protect the rim (4.8 blocks/game team total). They favor **matchups against slow, perimeter-heavy teams**, outrebounding opponents by 5 per game and winning the glass against smaller lineups, as seen in their 78-62 SWAC quarterfinal rout of Alcorn State[1][2].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **explosive, athletic guards** who attack gaps (opponents score 1.12 PPP on drives), with poor free-throw defense (opponents 76% FT) and turnover creation dropping against ball-dominant offenses. **Fast-paced, three-point bombing teams** trouble them, as in a 92-81 loss to Prairie View where they allowed 14 threes[1][3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with Puoch Dobuol back from minor ankle tweak[2]. Coach Michael Grant has **zero NCAA Tournament wins** in three appearances (last in 2013), but reaches March Madness for the first time since 2020. Historically, Southern is **0-5 all-time in the NCAA Tournament**, with no upsets as a #16 seed[6].\n\n## X-Factor\n**Jayce Depron's three-point volume**—if he hits 4+ threes (as in 4/8 vs. Grambling), Southern keeps games low-scoring and grinds out an upset; cold nights (under 30%) expose their offense[1][2].",
