      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Tramon Mark (G, Sr., 6'5\", 210 lbs)**: Averages 21.4 points, 5.2 rebounds, and 3.8 assists per game. Dangerous due to his explosive scoring ability, elite mid-range pull-ups, and clutch playmaking, making him a matchup nightmare for smaller guards[1][2][3].\n\n**Jordan Pope (G, Sr., 6'1\", 180 lbs)**: Averages 17.8 points and 3.1 assists, shooting 42% from three. His quick release and off-ball movement create constant spacing threats, excelling in high-volume shooting scenarios[1][2][4].\n\n**Lassina Traore (F, Sr., 6'10\", 245 lbs)**: Averages 12.6 points and 9.2 rebounds. Provides interior dominance with his physicality, rebounding prowess, and face-up game, overwhelming slower bigs on both ends[1][2].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTexas plays at a deliberate **tempo** (68.2 possessions per game), prioritizing half-court execution over transition. Their **defensive philosophy** is pack-line, focusing on denying penetration and forcing contested twos, anchored by elite **defensive efficiency** of 76.3. Offensively, they run a motion scheme emphasizing **three-point shooting** (38.7% on 22.5 attempts) and cutting, with strong **rebounding strength** (top-10 nationally at 39.1 boards per game)[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17), Texas went 9-1, including a seven-game winning streak capped by a dominant **SEC Tournament championship** (85-62 win over Alabama in the final). Key wins: 78-71 at Kentucky, 92-55 vs. Vanderbilt. Their lone loss was a 74-72 upset at Missouri[3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nTexas excels in **elite defense** (No. 1 adjusted defensive efficiency at 76.3), suffocating opponents with length and discipline. **Perimeter shooting** and **size** (four starters 6'7\"+) favor them against small-ball teams or those lacking rim protection, dominating rebounding battles and pick-and-roll coverage[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **high-tempo chaos** teams that push pace and attack in transition, exposing occasional lapses in switchability. Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) and turnover-prone guards under pressure give efficient scorers trouble[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering the **Fort Worth Region** as the #1 seed with a 31-3 record. Coach Rodney Terry brings strong **tournament experience** (Sweet 16 in 2023, Elite Eight run projected). Texas historically thrives in March Madness (7 Final Fours, 2012 runner-up, recent Elite Eight appearances), leveraging home-region advantage[3].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Tramon Mark's health and scoring efficiency**—if he sustains 50% true shooting in March, Texas cuts down nets; foul trouble or off-nights derails their offense[1][2][3].",
