      "perplexityContext": "# Michigan Wolverines 2025-26 NCAA Tournament Scouting Report\n## #2 Seed, Fort Worth Region\n\n## Key Players\n\n**Yaxel Lendeborg (F, #23)** — The Wolverines' primary offensive engine, Lendeborg averaged **14.6 points per game** on .509 field goal percentage and .353 from three[2]. Over 1,028 minutes played across 33 starts, he contributed **7.0 assists and 1.2 steals per game**, making him a two-way threat[2]. His versatility in initiating offense and defending multiple positions makes him essential to Michigan's tournament hopes.\n\n**Morez Johnson Jr. (F, #21)** — A dominant interior presence, Johnson Jr. posted **13.1 points per game** on an impressive .628 shooting percentage, the team's most efficient scorer[2]. His rebounding prowess stands out with **7.2 rebounds per game** and he added **0.70 steals per game**, playing all 34 games and 34 starts[2]. Johnson Jr. is the backbone of Michigan's frontcourt defense and pick-and-roll finishing.\n\n**Aday Mara (C, #15)** — The 7'3\" Spanish transfer from UCLA recorded **11.6 points per game** on .674 shooting, along with **6.9 rebounds per game and a team-leading 2.6 blocks per game** across 33 starts[2]. Mara's defensive anchoring—particularly his shot-blocking presence—fundamentally shapes how opponents attack the paint.\n\n## Team Identity\n\nMichigan operates with a **measured, possession-conscious approach** centered on interior dominance and perimeter spacing. Their **adjusted offensive efficiency of 108.0** reflects a methodical system that prioritizes high-percentage looks, evidenced by Johnson Jr. and Mara's elite shooting percentages[2]. Defensively, the **81.3 adjusted defensive efficiency** is built around Mara's rim protection (45 blocks) and Johnson Jr.'s versatile perimeter-to-post defense[2].\n\nThe Wolverines attempt **150 three-pointers per game** but only make **53 per game** (.353), indicating they're not a perimeter-first team—instead, three-point shooting serves as a complementary weapon rather than their identity[2]. Rebounding is a significant strength: Michigan grabbed **237 offensive rebounds** and **985 defensive rebounds** for **1,359 total rebounds** compared to opponents' **1,199**, demonstrating consistent second-chance opportunities[3].\n\nTheir **628 total turnovers against 407 opponent turnovers** suggests a patient offensive approach with occasional ball-handling vulnerabilities[3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nThe search results provided do not include specific game logs, recent winning/losing streaks, or Conference Tournament results for March 2026. However, their **25-6 record entering the tournament** indicates sustained success throughout the 2025-26 season. Without detailed recent form data, it's advisable to note that Michigan's consistency across 34 regular season games—with key players like Lendeborg, Johnson Jr., and Mara each playing nearly every game—suggests they've maintained form rather than experienced significant late-season volatility.\n\n## Strengths\n\n**Interior Dominance** — The combination of Mara's elite rim protection (2.6 blocks per game) and Johnson Jr.'s .628 shooting efficiency creates a two-pronged advantage. Teams cannot afford to collapse on the ball handler, nor can they leave either player unguarded in the post. This forces opponents into difficult perimeter-dependent offenses.\n\n**Efficiency Standards** — With an **adjusted offensive efficiency of 108.0**, Michigan rarely beats itself through poor shot selection. Their defense at **81.3 efficiency** is elite—they don't allow second chances and force difficult looks. This combination is tournament-proof against most opponent styles.\n\n**Rebounding Control** — A **+160 rebounding margin** (237 offensive vs. 377 allowed in opponent scoring opportunities) gives Michigan consistent extra possessions and limits three-point volume for opponents[3].\n\n**Balanced Scoring** — While led by Lendeborg's 14.6 PPG, the team doesn't rely on a single scorer; Johnson Jr. (13.1), Mara (11.6), and bench contributors like Nimari Burnett (8.3 PPG) provide secondary scoring outlets[2].\n\n**Favorable Matchups** — Teams without reliable centers or those dependent on perimeter scoring will struggle; Michigan's interior defense and rebounding make run-and-gun offenses extremely difficult to execute.\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**Three-Point Shooting Inconsistency** — At **.353 from three** (53 makes on 150 attempts), Michigan's perimeter game is a liability[2]. Against tournament teams with disciplined perimeter defenses, the Wolverines may lack offensive firepower if Johnson Jr. and Mara are properly defended. Lendeborg's .353 three-point percentage is team-high among frequent shooters, but this remains Michigan's primary offensive ceiling.\n\n**Turnover Vulnerability** — With **407 turnovers in 34 games**, Michigan commits roughly **12.0 turnovers per game[3]**. Against full-court pressure or aggressive ball-hawking defenses, this rate could spike significantly in tournament-intensity environments.\n\n**Lack of Elite Ball-Handling Depth** — While Lendeborg distributes reasonably (7.0 APG), the guard depth behind him is limited; L.J. Cason (8.4 PPG off bench) and Burnett (8.3 PPG, 2.4 RPG) are capable but not dynamic creators. A team with elite perimeter defenders can limit Michigan's playmaking options.\n\n**Pacing Vulnerability** — Their methodical, possession-conscious identity may struggle against teams that thrive in transition or in high-tempo environments. If forced into a fast-paced game, Michigan's rebounding advantage diminishes.\n\n**Vulnerable Matchups** — Teams with elite interior scorers who can match Johnson Jr. and Mara's efficiency, combined with perimeter shooting to space the floor, create ideal tournament opposition.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nThe search results do not provide explicit information regarding current roster injuries, coaching tournament experience for the 2025-26 staff, or Michigan basketball's historical NCAA Tournament performance. However, the **34 games played by Lendeborg, Johnson Jr., and Mara** suggests a fully healthy roster entering March[2]. ",
