      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Labaron Philon (G)** averages 21.9 points per game, leading the team in scoring, assists (5.6), and steals (1.4); his explosive quickness, playmaking vision, and ability to create off the dribble make him dangerous in transition and isolation[3].  \n**Amari Allen (F)** posts 7.1 rebounds per game (207 total) with 1.1 steals and 0.7 blocks; his versatility as a 6'5\" wing who crashes the glass, defends multiple positions, and scores efficiently inside makes him a matchup nightmare[2].  \n**Aiden Sherrell (F)** dominates with 2.9 blocks per game (72 total) and 6.1 rebounds (189 total); his elite rim protection and length disrupt drives while enabling fast breaks[2][3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nAlabama plays at a high tempo, averaging 94.6 points per game (19th nationally), emphasizing transition scoring and volume three-point shooting (7.4 makes per game at 35.8%, 94th)[3][5]. Defensively, they prioritize rim protection (5.1 blocks per game) and rebounding edges (38.5 rebounds per game, 49th), but allow 81.7 points (291st) due to middling perimeter defense (opponents 32.1% from three)[3]. Offensively, they run a motion scheme reliant on guard penetration and kick-outs, with strong free-throw shooting (76.1%, 54th)[3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month leading into the SEC Tournament, Alabama went 7-3, including a win over Alabama A&M (89-88 on 2/28) and a narrow loss (88-89), showing resilience in close games[5]. They entered the conference tournament on a three-game win streak but fell in the quarterfinals to a top SEC foe, finishing SEC play 10-8 overall[3]. Their 23-10 record reflects steady late-season play without extended streaks[1].\n\n## Strengths\n\nAlabama excels in offensive firepower (adjusted 98.8 efficiency) and rebounding dominance (44.4 per game vs. opponents' 43.8), winning the glass to fuel second-chance points and transitions[2][3]. They thrive against slower, perimeter-weak teams, using Philon's scoring and Sherrell's blocks to control paint battles and exploit mismatches on wings like Allen[1][2].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on defense (adjusted 86.7 efficiency but 81.7 points allowed, 291st), they struggle against efficient three-point shooters (opponents 32.1%) and teams that attack the rim without fouling (opponents 68.5% FT)[3]. Athletic, guard-heavy squads that push tempo and probe the perimeter give them trouble by exposing backcourt weaknesses[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported among key players entering the Fort Worth Region[4]. Coach Nate Oats brings proven March Madness experience, with deep runs including Elite Eight appearances in recent years. Alabama's program boasts strong historical success, reaching Sweet 16s and Final Fours under Oats, thriving as mid-seeds with 23-10 records like this[4].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Labaron Philon's three-point volume**: If Philon heats up from deep (team 35.8% but relies on his creation), Alabama's spacing unlocks their offense for upsets; cold nights expose defensive flaws and stall runs[3].",
