      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Quadir Copeland (G, 13.9 PPG)** leads the team in scoring with 458 points across 33 games, shooting 49.7% from the field and 39.7% from three (23 makes); his efficiency and ability to score at all levels, paired with 219 assists (6.6 APG), make him dangerous as a playmaking guard who exploits defenses in transition.[1][3][4]  \n**Ven-Allen Lubin (F, 13.9 PPG)** dominates inside with a team-high 236 rebounds (7.2 RPG) and 30 blocks, shooting 67.6% on 2-pointers; his rebounding prowess and paint scoring (458 points) threaten any frontcourt, especially on the glass where he grabs 96 offensive boards.[1][3][4]  \n**Darrion Williams (F, 13.8 PPG)** provides versatile wing scoring (440 points on 41.3% FG, 40.1% 3PT with 75 makes) and elite defense (35 steals, 1.1 SPG); his length and perimeter shooting stretch defenses, making him lethal in pick-and-rolls and spot-up situations.[1][3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nNC State plays at a deliberate tempo, ranking mid-pack in possessions, with a **defensive philosophy** anchored by physicality and rebounding (elite defensive efficiency of 88.1), forcing opponents into inefficient shots via blocks (team-high from Lubin) and steals (57 from Copeland).[1][3] Offensively, they run a **balanced scheme** emphasizing inside-out play, leveraging Lubin's dominance (67.6% 2PT) and Copeland/Williams' three-point volume (40%+ shooters), attempting high 3PA volumes while crashing the offensive glass (354 team OREB).[1][3] **Rebounding strength** is their hallmark (35.5 RPG allowed to opponents but superior own boards), but three-point shooting (35.6% team) is volume-reliant rather than elite.[3]\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), NC State went 6-4 in their final 10 games, including a three-game winning streak snapped by a loss to #20 Auburn (83-73); they finished ACC play 10-8 overall (20-13 adjusted record).[1][5][7] In the **ACC Tournament**, they exited early in the second round after a quarterfinal upset bid, hampered by turnovers (team 422 TOs season-long) against faster guards.[5][7]\n\n## Strengths\nNC State's **elite defense (88.1 efficiency)** smothers opponents through rebounding dominance (Lubin/Williams combine for 11.8 RPG) and active hands (team 170 steals), excelling against slow-paced, paint-heavy teams.[1][3] **Matchups favoring them** include undersized fronts or inefficient shooters, where their physicality (high OREB%) and Copeland's playmaking shine, as seen in home wins over physical ACC foes.[1][5]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable on the perimeter against elite guard play, yielding 35.6% opponent 3PT on 870 attempts, with Copeland's 93 TOs exposing ball-handling issues.[1][3] **Teams that trouble them** are high-tempo, three-point bombing squads (e.g., Auburn loss) that exploit their 41.3% team FG and occasional rebounding lapses away from home.[1][5][7]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported entering the Fort Worth Region; full roster availability with transfers like Lubin (North Carolina) and Williams bolstering depth.[2][5] Coach **Will Wade** (1st season) brings proven tournament experience, reaching Sweet 16 at LSU despite off-court issues; assistants Brandon Chambers and Vernon Hamilton add ACC pedigree.[5] Historically, NC State boasts **strong March Madness success**, including the 1983 championship and multiple Final Four runs (1974, 1983), thriving as midseeds with 20-10 (or 20-13) profiles.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n**Quadir Copeland's turnover control**—his 219 assists vs. 93 TOs could swing close games; if he limits mistakes under tournament pressure, NC State's efficiency edge propels them to the Sweet 16, but miscues against athletic guards doom an early exit.[1][3]",
