      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Jackson Shelstad (JR G, 6'1\", 17.2 PPG)** leads the Ducks in scoring with his quickness and elite shooting touch, making him dangerous as a pull-up jumper threat and transition scorer who exploits mismatches.[1][3]  \n**Nate Bittle (SR C, 7'0\", 12.8 PPG)** dominates inside with rebounding prowess (8.5 RPG) and soft touch around the rim, dangerous in pick-and-rolls and as a rim protector (1.8 BPG).[1][2]  \n**Kwame Evans Jr. (JR F, 6'10\", 11.4 PPG)** is a versatile wing with athleticism, excelling in transition dunks and perimeter defense, dangerous for his ability to switch across positions and hit spot-up threes (35% 3PT).[1][3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nOregon plays at a **mid-tempo pace** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and elite rim protection led by their bigs. Offensively, they run a **motion offense** with heavy ball movement, relying on **above-average three-point shooting** (36.2%, 11.2 3PA/game) and strong **offensive rebounding** (29.8% rate) to generate second-chance points.[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17 - Mar 17, 2026), Oregon went 7-4, including a three-game winning streak to close the regular season before a **Big Ten Tournament second-round exit** (lost 78-72 to #4 Purdue after beating #13 Penn State 85-76 in the first round). They split their final four Big Ten games, showing resilience with comeback wins over Michigan (82-79) and Illinois (71-68).[1][4]\n\n## Strengths\n\nOregon excels in **defensive efficiency** (86.2 AdjDEF), suffocating opponents inside with length (top-20 in block rate) and forcing misses on 54.2% of two-point attempts. They thrive in **matchups against slower, perimeter-heavy teams** like mid-major shooters or guard-dominant squads, where their size (multiple 6'8\"+ players) and transition attack (1.18 PPP in fast breaks) overwhelm.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nThe Ducks are vulnerable to **elite perimeter scorers** who can attack their smaller backcourt (Shelstad at 6'1\"), as evidenced by losses to Indiana (92-81) and Ohio State (88-76) where opponents shot 42% from three. **Turnover-prone guards** (15.8% TO rate) and poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) trouble them against physical, pressing teams that control tempo.[1][3]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**No major injuries** reported, though Ege Demir (JR C) is questionable with a shoulder issue but played limited minutes in the Big Ten Tournament.[2] Coach Dana Altman brings **extensive tournament experience** (21 NCAA appearances, 25 wins including 2017 Final Four run). Oregon's historical March Madness success includes consistent bids (18 straight before 2025 miss) but only one Elite Eight since 2002, often flaming out as mid-seeds due to offensive droughts.[4]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jackson Shelstad's three-point volume**—if he sustains 38% on 6.2 attempts/game in March, Oregon's spacing unlocks Bittle/Evans inside; cold nights (under 30%) expose their halfcourt stagnation and doom upset chances.",
