      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nVirginia Tech's top three players are **Jailen Bedford** (senior guard, 13.5 PPG), **Tyler Johnson** (sophomore guard, 18.9 PPG), and **Tobi Lawal** (senior forward, 8.5 RPG, 12.4 PPG).[1][3][4]  \n**Bedford** leads in minutes (31.0 MPG) with 418 total points on 53.9% FG, dangerous as a high-volume scorer and rebounder (7.0 RPG) who crashes the glass effectively.[1][4]  \n**Johnson** averages 322 points with 49/134 from three (36.6%), excelling as a versatile wing shooter who creates off the dribble (140 assists).[1][3]  \n**Lawal** dominates rebounding (195 total, 8.5 RPG including 52 offensive) and defense (26 blocks, 143 defensive boards), threatening with putbacks and rim protection.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nVirginia Tech plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (81.4 AdjDEF) with pack-line principles under Mike Young, forcing opponents to 96.8 AdjOFF while limiting transition.[1][3] Offensively, they rely on **balanced schemes** with guard-driven penetration, 32.7% team 3PT% (high volume from Johnson/Bedford), and frontcourt rebounding strength (opponents average 38.5 RPG allowed).[1][4][6] Rebounding is a cornerstone, led by Lawal and Amani Hansberry (7.4 RPG), enabling second-chance points in half-court sets.[4]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to the ACC Tournament, Virginia Tech went 7-3, including a four-game win streak capped by a quarterfinal upset win before a semifinal loss to a top seed, finishing 23-9 overall (16-6 ACC, 6th place).[3] They split their final regular-season series, winning at home but dropping a road game, showing resilience in close contests (6-2 in games decided by <10 points).[1][6]\n\n## Strengths\nVirginia Tech excels in **defensive efficiency** (81.4 AdjDEF), suffocating opponents inside with Lawal/Hansberry blocking shots (47 combined blocks) and forcing 12.0 turnovers per game.[1][4] They dominate the glass (+ margin via 195 OR/DR from top rebounders), favoring **slow-paced, physical matchups** against inefficient shooters or weak interior teams, where their 53.9% eFG% shines in the half court.[1][6]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerabilities include **perimeter defense** against hot-shooting guards (opponents hit 12.8 3PM/game) and limited depth with freshmen-heavy bench (e.g., Gurdak at 4.4 RPG).[1][4] **Up-tempo, athletic teams** with elite spacing trouble them, as seen in ACC losses where they allowed 410 opponent assists, exposing turnover issues (60 from Bedford alone).[1][6]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available including key freshmen like Christian Gurdak (136 rebounds).[3][4][5] Coach **Mike Young** brings strong tournament experience (7th season, prior Sweet 16 runs), though Virginia Tech's March Madness history is modest (2-4 since 2016, no Elite Eight).[3][5] As a #9 seed in Fort Worth, their defensive pedigree positions them for an upset in a region favoring grind-it-out games.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Tyler Johnson's three-point shooting** (49 makes on 134 attempts); if he heats up (36.6% clip), it stretches defenses and elevates their offense from good to tournament-winning, but cold streaks expose half-court stagnation.[1]",
