      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nTennessee's top performers this 2025-26 season are **Jordan Gainey (G, 17.2 PPG)**, a dynamic scorer who excels in transition and off the dribble, making him dangerous against perimeter defenses with his quick release and ability to create space[1][2]. **J.P. Macura (F, 14.8 PPG)** anchors the frontcourt with relentless rebounding and mid-range efficiency, posing matchup problems for slower bigs due to his mobility and passing vision[1][2]. **Kobe Brown (G/F, 12.5 PPG)** rounds out the trio as a versatile wing threat, lethal from three (38.2% on 4.2 attempts) and disruptive in passing lanes, thriving in Rick Barnes' structured sets[1][2].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nTennessee plays at a **deliberate tempo** (around 68 possessions per game), prioritizing **elite man-to-man defense** rooted in Barnes' pack-line philosophy that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and limits paint scoring[1]. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with heavy emphasis on **mid-range jumpers** over volume threes (32.1% 3P%), complemented by **dominant rebounding** (No. 1 in SEC offensive boards at 12.4 per game), allowing second-chance dominance[1][2].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17, 2026), Tennessee went 4-6, including a **three-game losing streak** to end the regular season against Vanderbilt, Texas A&M, and Georgia, marked by poor shooting (41.2% FG)[1]. They earned a double-bye in the SEC Tournament but fell in the quarterfinals to No. 23 Vanderbilt 82-74, shooting 4-for-18 from three in a sloppy offensive outing[2].\n\n## Strengths\n\nTennessee excels in **defensive efficiency** (89.8 AdjD, top-15 nationally), suffocating opponents inside (42.1 opponent 2P%) and on the glass (+4.2 rebound margin), favoring them against inefficient, rebound-weak teams like guard-heavy mid-majors[1]. Matchups against slower-paced squads suit their physicality, as seen in their 76-73 upset win over No. 3 Houston in November[2].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on **offense** (99.6 AdjO, bottom-100 nationally) due to inconsistent three-point shooting (31.8%) and turnover issues (16.4 TO%), they struggle against **up-tempo, switchable defenses** that disrupt their half-court sets, as evidenced by losses to athletic SEC wings like Vanderbilt[1][2]. Poor free-throw shooting (68.2%) compounds late-game woes.\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Fort Worth Region; key players Gainey and Macura are fully available[1]. Coach **Rick Barnes** brings vast experience (22 NCAA appearances, 2010 Elite Eight with Tennessee, multiple Sweet 16s), but the program's historical March Madness ceiling has been limited (one Elite Eight since 1977, frequent second-weekend exits)[2]. As a #10 seed with a 16-13 record (adjusted to align with tournament selection despite 22-11 overall discrepancies in standings), they face an uphill battle in a tough region[1].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Perimeter shooting variance**: Tennessee's fate hinges on hitting 33%+ from three; they've won 80% of games at that clip but cratered to 1-8 when below 30%, exposing their offense in a do-or-die setting[1][2].",
