      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nRhode Island's top scorers are **RJ Johnson** (G, 5.7 PPG, 171 total points over 30 games), **Tyler Cochran** (G, versatile guard with key scoring outbursts like 21 points vs. St. Bonaventure), and **Myles Corey** (G, consistent 10+ PPG contributor with 14 points in recent losses).[3][5][6] Johnson is dangerous as a rebounding guard (52 total rebounds) who crashes the glass and shoots 28.0% from three, creating second-chance chaos. Cochran thrives in transition with efficient scoring (e.g., 13 points vs. Saint Joseph's) and rebounding (15 in one game), punishing slower defenses. Corey excels at mid-range pull-ups and free throws, deadly in clutch spots with multi-game scoring reliability.[1][3][5]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nRhode Island plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing **elite defense** (adjusted efficiency 81.2) with aggressive steals (269 total) and blocks (115 team total), forcing 395 opponent turnovers while ranking high in A-10 rebounding margins (1053 total rebounds).[1][3][4] Offensively, they run a balanced scheme (adjusted efficiency 100.1) with .434 FG% and .311 from three (236 makes on 760 attempts), relying on guard-driven half-court sets and crash-the-boards rebounding (339 offensive boards).[3] Three-point volume is moderate but effective inside the arc, with physicality defining their gritty, low-possession style.[1][4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 2026), Rhode Island went 1-5, dropping five straight A-10 games including losses at La Salle (46-59 on 2/21), at St. Bonaventure (76-94 on 2/25), and home to Saint Joseph's (55-61 on 2/28).[5] They showed fight in a 59-46 win at La Salle earlier but struggled offensively in defeats, averaging under 60 points in four of six. No extended winning streak recently; they entered February 15-12 (6-8 A-10). A-10 Tournament result: early exit as a lower seed after regular-season slide.[3][5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nRhode Island dominates with **lockdown perimeter defense** and rebounding, holding foes under 70 points in wins (e.g., 80-65 at Vermont) via steals and physical boards.[3][5] They excel against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, using 484/671 FT% efficiency and transition buckets off turnovers to grind out games. Matchups favoring them include undersized fronts, where their 32.9 rebounds per game overwhelm.[1][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to hot-shooting offenses and athletic wings, as seen in blowouts like 76-94 at St. Bonaventure where they shot .420% and yielded 94.[5] Poor three-point defense (opponents .381% in losses) and late-game execution falter against teams with size and pace, exposing their 16-15 overall record despite efficiency metrics.[1][3][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the tournament; full roster availability with Johnson and Cochran healthy.[5][7] Coach **James Whitford** brings solid experience (prior NCAA appearances), backed by assistants Duane Woodward and Austin Carroll.[5] Historically, Rhode Island has limited March Madness success (last win 2017, deep runs rare), but their 27-4 adjusted record suggests upset potential as a #11 seed in Fort Worth.[1][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**RJ Johnson's rebounding aggression**—if the 5.7 PPG guard grabs 6+ boards per game to fuel transition, they spark runs; otherwise, stagnant half-court play dooms them.[3][5]",
