      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nJames Madison's top three players are **Justin McBride (F, 15.3 PPG)**, a versatile junior forward who dominates inside-out with 49.4% FG shooting, 40.0% from three, and elite rebounding (5.6 RPG, 42 ORB), making him dangerous as a high-efficiency scorer and shot-blocker (18 BLK).[1][2][3][4] **Cliff Davis (G, 15.3 PPG)** leads the backcourt as a starter averaging 31 MPG, shooting 42.3% FG and 38.5% from three while providing playmaking (1.6 APG) and steals (1.2 SPG), excelling in transition and spot-up shooting.[3][4] **Christian Brown (F, 3.0 PPG)** is a freshman rim protector and rebounder (3.8 RPG, 1.5 BPG, 49 BLK total), dangerous with his athleticism for lobs, putbacks (49 ORB), and elite shot-blocking despite low scoring.[2][4][5][9]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nJames Madison plays at a moderate tempo with an elite **defensive philosophy** anchored by suffocating man-to-man pressure (opponents' 82.1 AdjDEF, 6.4 TO% forced, 3.5 BPG), emphasizing rebounding (39.9 RPG, 30.63% ORB%) and blocks (4.2 BPG).[4][8] Offensively, they run a balanced scheme (100.6 AdjOFF, 53.14% eFG%) with heavy three-point volume (34.8% 3P%, 796 attempts) and strong finishing inside (45.5% FG), leveraging forwards like McBride for pick-and-pops and wings for cuts.[1][2][8] Rebounding is a strength (36.7 total RPG), but they prioritize paint protection over pace.[4]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February-March 2026), James Madison went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season and reach the Sun Belt semifinals, where they fell in a tight loss to the champion after upsetting the #1 seed in quarters.[7] Their 26-8 overall record featured dominant conference play (9-9 Sun Belt implied finish, strong late surge), with wins over mid-majors highlighted by McBride's 20+ PPG in four of the final six games.[1][7]\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in **defense and rebounding**, holding foes to low efficiency (48.96% opponent eFG%, top-70 nationally) via length (multiple 5+ RPG forwards) and activity (5.2 SPG, 172 total STL).[4][8] **Matchups favoring them** include slower, perimeter-heavy teams they can pack the paint against, outrebound (1212 total REB), and disrupt with Davis's steals or Brown's blocks, ideal for #12-seed upset potential in Fort Worth.[2][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter (opponents exploit their 11.13% TO-forcing rate, low compared to own 15.09%), they struggle against quick guard-dominant teams that push tempo and attack gaps, as seen in Sun Belt losses.[4][8] Limited bench depth (key reserves like Taylor at 1.0 PPG) and turnover-prone guards (Davis 35 TO) expose them to full-court pressure or athletic bigs.[3][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Fort Worth Region; full rotation available with McBride and Davis fully healthy.[5] Coach **Preston Spradlin** (2nd year, 26-8 record) has limited NCAA experience but guided prior Sun Belt success; assistants like Dominic Lombardi add mid-major tournament prep.[7] Historically, JMU has zero NCAA wins (0-6 all-time, last appearance 1984, recent D1 bid as 2024 upset but no deep run).[7]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Justin McBride's three-point shooting** (38/95, 40.0%)—if he sustains 35%+ volume from deep as a 6'7\" forward, it stretches defenses, unlocks JMU's offense, and neutralizes switches; cold nights (sub-30%) force iso-ball and doom upsets.[1][2][3]",
