      "perplexityContext": "# Miami (OH) RedHawks NCAA Tournament Scouting Report\n## #13 Seed, Fort Worth Region\n\n---\n\n## Key Players\n\n**Eian Elmer (#0, Guard, 6'6\")** leads the RedHawks with **12.6 PPG** on impressive **50.0% FG** and **43.4% 3P shooting**[6]. The junior's versatility as a wing defender and efficient scorer makes him the engine of Miami's offense. Elmer's **6.0 APG** and team-high **192 rebounds** demonstrate his all-around impact across 32 games played[6].\n\n**Evan Ipsaro (#2, Guard, 6'0\")** averages **13.9 PPG** and represents the team's scoring punch from the backcourt[6]. The junior guard has appeared in only 12 games but maintains elite efficiency at **.571 FG** and **.394 3P** shooting, suggesting he's either dealing with a late-season injury or has limited minutes distribution[6]. His **39 steals** across limited playing time indicates strong perimeter defense.\n\n**Jackson Kotecki (#24, Forward, 6'9\")** provides interior scoring and rebounding at **10.0+ PPG** with significant frontcourt presence[4]. The junior forward contributes to Miami's **265 offensive rebounds** (3rd in the nation efficiency metrics), making him crucial for second-chance opportunities and paint defense.\n\n---\n\n## Team Identity\n\nMiami (OH) operates as a **three-point heavy, high-efficiency offensive team** with a balanced defensive approach[4]. The RedHawks launched **853 three-pointers** (39.2% conversion rate) on the season, indicating a modern, spacing-oriented offensive scheme that stretches defenses[4]. \n\n**Defensive Philosophy**: Miami's **82.3 adjusted defensive efficiency** ranks well nationally. They employ **238 total blocks and 335 steals** across 32 games, showing they prioritize perimeter disruption while maintaining interior shot-blocking presence[4]. Their **265 offensive rebounds** suggest aggressive pursuit of misses, giving them multiple scoring opportunities per possession.\n\n**Pace & Rebounding**: The team scored **2,902 total points** (90.7 PPG) with balanced contributions across the roster, indicating they don't rely on isolation-heavy offense[4]. Their rebounding margin and 32-game sample size suggest sustainable, consistent performance.\n\n---\n\n## Recent Form\n\nThe search results do not provide specific information about Miami's performance in the final month of the regular season, winning/losing streaks, or MAC Conference Tournament results[1][2][3][4][6]. However, their **26-6 overall record** entering the tournament indicates sustained excellence throughout 2025-26. With an **adjusted offensive efficiency of 96.7** and **defensive efficiency of 82.3**, Miami has maintained elite performance metrics that likely extended through conference play[4].\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**Elite Three-Point Shooting**: Miami's **.392 three-point percentage** (334-of-853) represents one of the nation's most efficient long-range attacks[4]. Teams cannot pack the paint, forcing perimeter-oriented defensive schemes that play into the RedHawks' spacing advantage.\n\n**Efficient Scoring**: The **96.7 adjusted offensive efficiency** reflects excellent shot selection and execution. With multiple capable shooters like Elmer (43.4%), Ipsaro (39.4%), and role players contributing from deep, Miami creates spacing nightmares[4][6].\n\n**Defensive Versatility**: The combination of **39 steals from Elmer, 41 steals from Brady Ganley, and 43 steals from another defender** demonstrates aggressive perimeter pressure[4]. This creates turnovers (515 team steals) that lead to transition scoring opportunities.\n\n**Offensive Rebounding**: With **265 offensive boards**, Miami extends possessions and generates second-chance points—a critical advantage in tournament games where possessions are limited and efficiency matters most[4].\n\n**Favorable Matchups**: Mid-major tournament teams lacking three-point shooting or perimeter defenders will struggle containing Miami's spread offense and wing-heavy lineup.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**Guard Depth Concerns**: Miami's backcourt relies heavily on **Elmer and Ipsaro**, with Ipsaro playing only 12 games[6]. If either guard encounters foul trouble or injury in tournament play, the RedHawks lack proven secondary ballhandlers and scorers.\n\n**Size Limitations**: The roster features numerous guards (Perry, Ganley, Kirby, Stowers, Yofan) and relatively few true centers[2][3]. Teams with strong interior presences—particularly those capable of exploiting Miami's smaller frontcourt—can attack the paint and negate the three-point advantage.\n\n**Turnover Vulnerability**: With **335 turnovers across 32 games**, Miami loses approximately **10.5 turnovers per game**[4]. High-pressure defensive teams that force turnovers can disrupt the RedHawks' rhythm and create empty possessions.\n\n**Free Throw Shooting**: The team shot only **.749 from the line** (566-of-756), suggesting potential struggles in close tournament games where free throws determine outcomes[4].\n\n**Tournament Inexperience**: As a #13 seed, Miami likely lacks NCAA Tournament experience at the program level. Mid-major programs without consistent March Madness appearances may struggle with tournament intensity and atmosphere.\n\n---\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nThe search results do not provide information about current injuries, coaching tenure, or Miami's historical NCAA Tournament success[1][2][3][4][6]. However, the team's **26-6 record and elite efficiency metrics** suggest organizational health entering the tournament. The presence of a senior like **Peter Suder (#5)** alongside experienced juniors (**Elmer, Ipsaro, Kotecki, Atlason**) indicates roster maturity and chemistry developed over a full season[3].\n\n---\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Evan Ipsaro's availability and playing time** will determine Miami's tournament ceiling. A junior guard shooting **.571 FG** with 13.9 PPG in only 12 games suggests either late-season injury recovery or load management. If Ipsaro enters the tournament at full health with game rhythm, Miami's backcourt can outshoot most opponents and create separation. Conversely, if he's limited or rusty, the RedHawks lose their second primary scorer and must rely on role players to match offensive firepower in single-elimination play",
