      "perplexityContext": "## Vermont Catamounts Scouting Report: #14 Seed, Fort Worth 3 Region (2026 NCAA Tournament)\n\nVermont enters the 2026 NCAA Tournament with a 25-7 record, elite adjusted defensive efficiency of 82.3, and solid offensive efficiency of 99.0, positioning them as a gritty mid-major contender after dominating the America East.[1][2]\n\n## Key Players\n- **TJ Hurley (Sr G, 6-5)**: Averaging 17.2 PPG, 4.1 APG, and 1.8 SPG; dangerous as a versatile combo guard who attacks seams off the dribble, hits 38% of threes, and disrupts passing lanes with quick hands.[1][2]\n- **TJ Long (Sr G, 6-4)**: Leads at 18.9 PPG and 6.2 RPG; his pull-up shooting (42% from three on 5.3 attempts) and transition scoring make him a mismatch nightmare against slower defenders.[1][2]\n- **Gus Yalden (Soph F, 6-9, 258 lbs)**: 12.4 PPG and 8.1 RPG; dominates inside with physical post play, offensive rebounding (3.2 per game), and 55% FG efficiency, excelling in bully-ball scenarios.[1]\n\n## Team Identity\nVermont plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing **lockdown man-to-man defense** that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and smothers paint scoring. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack motion scheme** with heavy reliance on guard penetration and kick-outs, shooting 36.8% from three on high volume while ranking top-50 nationally in offensive rebounding (33.1%).[1][2]\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (Feb 17–March 17), Vermont went 9-2, including a five-game win streak capped by a **77-57 rout of Bryant in the America East quarterfinals** (March 7). They beat Albany 69-56 in the regular-season finale (March 3) but stumbled in the semis, losing narrowly after leading by double digits; still advanced as a top seed with 12-4 conference mark.[2][3]\n\n## Strengths\nVermont excels in **half-court defense** (allowing 82.3 adj eff), forcing 1.12 turnovers per possession and holding opponents to 42% eFG. They thrive against inefficient shooting teams or those lacking perimeter size, using Yalden's bulk and Hurley's length to control glass (top-30 defensive rebounding) and punish transition.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **up-tempo, three-point bombing offenses** that stretch their deliberate pace—opponents shooting over 36% from deep win 70% of the time. Perimeter depth thins late (top-7 rotation only), and they struggle vs athletic wings who deny Hurley/Long's drives, as seen in early losses to Liberty (73-79) and Rhode Island (65-80).[2]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full rotation healthy entering Fort Worth 3. Head coach **John Becker** (15th year) brings solid experience with six NCAA bids but 0-6 record, all first-round exits. Program history: 8 total appearances, best as 13-seed in 2005 (lost to Syracuse); consistent mid-major but no upsets.[2]\n\n## X-Factor\n**TJ Hurley's defensive versatility**—if he locks down the opponent's lead guard (limiting to under 12 PPG as in 8 of last 10 wins), Vermont can grind out an upset; failure here exposes their offense to pressure.[1][2]",
