      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nHoly Cross relies on a balanced scoring attack led by **Tyler Boston (G, 13.8 PPG, 3.2 APG)**, whose quickness and playmaking make him dangerous in transition and pick-and-roll situations, creating open looks for teammates.[3] **Aiden Disu (F, 5.3 RPG, 0.6 BPG)** anchors the frontcourt with rebounding prowess and shot-blocking, excelling in physical matchups where he can dominate the glass and finish lobs.[3] **Gabe Warren (G, 1.2 SPG)** provides perimeter defense and opportunistic scoring, thriving as a spot-up shooter and slasher who disrupts passing lanes.[3]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nHoly Cross plays at a deliberate tempo, emphasizing a pack-line **defensive philosophy** that prioritizes help rotations and limiting paint penetration, reflected in their elite adjusted defensive efficiency of 82.9. Offensively, they run a motion scheme with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (attempting high volume from beyond the arc) and cutting, though their adjusted offensive efficiency of 89.5 indicates struggles in half-court creation. Rebounding is a **strength** on the defensive end, led by forwards like Disu and Tim Finau (6-11 Fr. F).[1][2][3]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Holy Cross has surged with a 10-2 record, including a five-game winning streak to close the regular season, overcoming their 5-13 Patriot League mark.[5] In the conference tournament, they exceeded expectations by reaching the semifinals before falling to Lehigh, showcasing improved shooting and defensive intensity in knockout play.[5]\n\n## Strengths\n\nHoly Cross excels in **disruptive team defense**, forcing turnovers and excelling in transition off misses, which favors matchups against inefficient half-court offenses. Their size inside with Disu, Finau, and Declan Ryan (6-8 Jr. F) gives them an edge on the boards against smaller frontcourts, allowing second-chance points and rim protection.[1][2][3]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to athletic, perimeter-oriented teams that stretch their pack-line defense with off-ball movement and pull-up threes, exposing their mediocre adjusted offense. Perimeter scoring dries up against length, and they struggle rebounding against elite athletic bigs, leading to fast-break concessions.[3][5]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported entering the Fort Worth 3 region as a #15 seed; full roster availability including Boston and Disu. Coach lacks deep NCAA experience, with the program holding zero March Madness wins historically—last appearance was 2021 as a #15 seed, losing in the First Round. Their 23-9 overall record positions them as a gritty upset threat but with limited pedigree.[1][5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Tyler Boston's three-point volume**—if he heats up from deep (key to elevating their 89.5 offensive efficiency), Holy Cross can keep games low-scoring and pull off a Cinderella run; cold nights doom them against superior talent.[3]",
