      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Keith Palek III (F, 17.3 PPG)** leads the Bears with elite scoring volume, averaging 17.3 points on 192-460 FG and 163-230 FT, making him dangerous as a high-usage inside-out scorer who thrives in the paint and at the free-throw line despite a modest 25.5% from three (40-157).[3][1]  \n**Michael Osei-Bonsu (F, ~15.1 PPG estimated from team totals)** dominates the boards with 7.3 RPG (95 ORB, 132 DRB) and adds 1.2 SPG plus 0.8 BPG, posing matchup problems with his physicality, rebounding tenacity, and defensive versatility in transition.[4]  \n**Darrion Sutton (F, 4.6 PPG)** provides bench spark as a 6-9 sophomore with efficient 53.6% FG (67-125) and strong rebounding (4.3 RPG, 55 ORB), dangerous via putbacks, blocks (21 total), and rim protection against smaller fronts.[3][4]\n\n## Team Identity\nMissouri State plays at a deliberate tempo with a **defensive-first philosophy** anchored by elite adjusted defensive efficiency (83.0), emphasizing physical man-to-man pressure, rebounding dominance (team 1147 total rebounds), and forcing turnovers (373 team TO forced).[1]  \nOffensively, they run a **grind-it-out half-court scheme** reliant on paint scoring and free throws (16.2 FT/G at 67.7%), with solid three-point volume (7.6 3PT/G) but moderate accuracy; rebounding is a cornerstone strength, led by forwards crashing the glass.[3][5]\n\n## Recent Form\nIn the last month leading to the CUSA Tournament, Missouri State went 5-3, including a three-game win streak capped by a semifinal upset over WKU before falling in the championship to Liberty (CUSA regular-season champ at 17-3).[5]  \nTheir 22-12 overall mark featured clutch road wins in conference (8-12 CUSA), building momentum into the dance despite a middling finish.[5]\n\n## Strengths\nThe Bears excel in **defensive rebounding and physicality**, ranking high with 767 DRB and disruptive forwards like Osei-Bonsu (132 DRB), allowing them to control tempo and limit second-chance points.[1][4]  \nThey favor **matchups against inefficient, perimeter-heavy teams**, where their length (Sutton's 21 BLK, Palek's paint presence) clogs lanes and their 83.0 DE stifles scoring.[1]\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable on the perimeter with **low three-point efficiency** (team ~33%, Palek at 25.5%), they struggle against teams that spread the floor and attack closeouts.[3]  \n**High-possession offenses with elite guards** trouble them, as evidenced by 89.3 AdjOE and turnover issues (373 forced but 373 committed), exposing them to transition leaks.[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full roster availability including key freshmen like Melakih Cunningham (3.1 PPG, 2.0 RPG).[2][3]  \nHead coach Cuonzo Martin brings proven tournament experience (Sweet 16 with Missouri, multiple NCAA bids), though Missouri State's program history is modest—zero tournament wins since 1999, often first-round exits as a mid-major.[7]  \nAs a #16 seed in Fort Worth, upset potential exists via defense, but path is brutal.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Keith Palek III's free-throw volume**—if he draws 5+ attempts per game (as in 230 FTA season total) and converts at 70.9%, it fuels their grind; cold nights doom the low-efficiency offense.[3]",
