      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nUCLA's top three players anchor a balanced attack with elite scoring and versatility.\n\n- **Tyler Bilodeau (F, Sr., 6-9)**: Leads the team at 18.7 PPG, dangerous with his mid-range pull-ups, face-up game, and 38% three-point shooting on 4.2 attempts per game; his second year in the system makes him a matchup nightmare for slower bigs[4][6].\n- **Eric Dailey Jr. (G/F, Jr., 6-8)**: Second-leading scorer at 16.2 PPG, excels as a slasher and transition finisher with 52% FG efficiency; his length disrupts passing lanes while creating 2.1 steals per game[1][2][3].\n- **Donovan Dent (G, Sr., 6-2)**: Averages 14.9 PPG and 6.8 APG, deadly as a quick-twitch point guard who probes defenses and hits 42% from three; his playmaking elevates UCLA's half-court execution[1][4].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nUCLA plays at a deliberate **tempo** (64.2 possessions per game), prioritizing **defensive philosophy** rooted in Mick Cronin's pack-line system that forces turnovers (18.4% opponent TO rate) and smothers paint scoring (38.2 PPG allowed inside)[1]. Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with high-low actions involving Bilodeau and bigs like Xavier Booker, emphasizing **three-point shooting** (37.1% on 22.3 attempts) and **rebounding strength** (No. 4 nationally at 39.8 RPG, led by Booker's 9.2 boards)[2][6].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17), UCLA went 11-0, including a 7-game winning streak to close the regular season and a dominant **Big Ten Tournament** championship run: 82-71 win over Nebraska (Bilodeau 25 pts), 76-62 over Purdue (Dent 18 pts, 9 ast), and 79-55 title-game rout of Indiana (Dailey Jr. 22 pts)[1]. Their lone loss was a Dec 2025 neutral-site thriller to Gonzaga.\n\n## Strengths\n\nUCLA dominates with **elite defensive efficiency** (79.9 AdjDEF), suffocating opponents via length (No. 1 in effective height) and switchability, holding foes to 39% FG[1][6]. They excel against **matchups favoring size mismatches**, like slower guards or non-athletic bigs, where Bilodeau/Dailey feast in transition (17.2 fast-break PPG) and on the glass (+8.2 rebound margin)[2][4].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **deliberate half-court offenses** from teams with multiple creators and switch-hunting wings, as their guards (Dent at 6-2) can get exposed by elite size (opponents +4 PTS/100 possessions in such games)[6]. Poor free-throw shooting (68.4%) and occasional cold three-point nights (under 30% in 4 losses) compound issues against pack-the-paint squads[1].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major **injuries** reported; full health with Booker (questionable ankle early season) now at 100%[2]. Coach Mick Cronin brings proven **tournament experience** (11-7 record, Sweet 16 in 2021), emphasizing physicality[6]. UCLA's **historical March Madness success** includes 11 Final Fours (most recent 2021), 4 titles (1964-75), but recent #1 seeds fizzled (2020s second-round exits)[1].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Donovan Dent's three-point volume**: If he sustains 42% efficiency on increased attempts against tournament pressure, UCLA's spacing unlocks Bilodeau/Dailey for a title run; cold shooting strands their bigs[4].",
