      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nLSU's top three players entering the tournament are **Dedan Thomas Jr.** (Guard, 18.7 PPG), **Jalen Reed** (Forward, 15.2 PPG), and **Robert Miller III** (Forward, 12.9 PPG). Thomas Jr., a junior transfer from UNLV, is dangerous with his elite playmaking (6.4 APG) and ability to break down defenses off the dribble, creating open looks for teammates in LSU's motion offense[1][3][5]. Reed, a redshirt junior at 6-10/245 lbs, dominates inside with physical post scoring and rebounding (8.1 RPG), excelling in pick-and-roll situations against smaller fronts[1][3]. Miller III, a sophomore with a 7-5 wingspan, threatens with versatile scoring (stretch-4 ability, 38% from three) and rim protection, making him a mismatch nightmare[3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nLSU plays at a **high tempo** (72.1 possessions per game), pushing the pace in transition while employing a **man-to-man defensive philosophy** focused on physicality and switching everything, anchored by their size (No. 4 in SEC rebounding margin). Offensively, they run a **gap-attack scheme** with heavy ball-screen actions and cutting, ranking top-20 nationally in three-point volume (38.2% on 22.4 attempts), but their **rebounding strength** (top-10 adjusted) allows second-chance dominance[3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (Feb 17–Mar 17), LSU went 9-1, including a seven-game winning streak capped by a **SEC Tournament semifinal win** over Alabama (78-72) before falling to Auburn in the final (81-76 OT); they beat Kentucky twice in that span. Their only loss was a neutral-site thriller to Gonzaga[3][5].\n\n## Strengths\n\nLSU excels in **interior dominance** and rebounding, outrebounding opponents by +8.2 per game, which fuels their elite defensive efficiency (75.6 AdjDEF, No. 3 nationally). They favor matchups against guard-heavy or undersized teams, where their 6-10 frontcourt (Reed, Miller III, Mike Nwoko at 6-10/261 lbs) overwhelms with physicality and second-chance points (15.3 per game)[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter, LSU allows 36.8% opponent three-point shooting due to occasional over-helping from bigs, and their youth (six freshmen/sophomores in rotation) leads to late-game turnovers (14.2 per game). Switchable, athletic wings like those from Gonzaga or Houston give them trouble by exploiting switches and disrupting Thomas Jr.'s rhythm[3][5].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Dedan Thomas Jr. (minor ankle tweak, fully practiced). Coach **Matt McMahon** has solid tournament experience (Davidson Elite Eight runs, 2022 NCAA First Round with Murray State), though LSU's program historically struggles (2 Sweet 16s since 2006, 0 Final Fours post-1981). As a **#2 seed in Sacramento**, their size matches West Region physicality well[3][6].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Dedan Thomas Jr.'s** free-throw shooting (72.4%)—if he avoids late-game misses like in the SEC final, LSU advances deep; otherwise, it caps their ceiling in close games.",
