      "perplexityContext": "# Duke Blue Devils NCAA Tournament Scouting Report\n## #3 Seed, Sacramento 2 Region\n\n---\n\n## Key Players\n\n**Cameron Boozer (F, 6'9\", 250 lbs)** — The freshman forward is Duke's leading rebounder and most versatile defender, averaging **10.2 rebounds per game** with **1.50 steals per game**[4]. He provides elite rim protection and offensive rebounding (114 offensive boards), making him crucial in the paint against tournament competition. Boozer's size and athleticism give Duke a physical presence that translates to March.\n\n**Caleb Foster (G, 6'0\", 170 lbs)** — The point guard orchestrates Duke's offense with **143 assists** and tight ball security (79 turnovers across the season), maintaining strong floor management in high-pressure situations[1]. Foster's ability to run the offense and create for others makes him essential to Duke's spacing and half-court execution.\n\n**Isaiah Evans (G/F, 6'6\", 180 lbs)** — A secondary scoring option and perimeter defender, Evans has shown improvement in his sophomore year and provides versatility on the wing. His shooting ability (8-29 from three) and defensive versatility give Duke lineup flexibility against different tournament opponents.\n\n---\n\n## Team Identity\n\nDuke operates a **deliberate, defensively-anchored system** that prioritizes efficiency and ball control. Their adjusted **offensive efficiency of 100.6** is solid but not explosive, suggesting a methodical approach rather than high-tempo basketball. The team's adjusted **defensive efficiency of 79.9** represents elite-level defense—the backbone of this tournament contender.\n\nThe Blue Devils are a **strong rebounding team**, accumulating **1,468 total rebounds** while limiting opponents to just **1,102 rebounds**[4], a significant +366 advantage. This indicates Coach Jon Scheyer's system emphasizes boxing out and controlling the glass. **Three-point shooting is selective rather than volume-heavy**, with only **309 made threes on 881 attempts** (35.1%), reflecting a ball-movement system that prioritizes efficient looks over volume.\n\nTheir defensive philosophy centers on **forcing turnovers** (264 steals, 7.80 per game) while maintaining discipline (111 blocks, 3.30 per game)[4]. This creates a suffocating pace-and-space defense that disrupts offensive rhythm.\n\n---\n\n## Recent Form\n\nWhile full March data is limited by the current date, Duke finished the regular season **32-2 overall with a 17-1 ACC conference record**[3], indicating exceptional late-season consistency. The team's dominance in conference play—dropping only one ACC game—demonstrates championship-caliber execution and resilience. The **24-8 tournament-opening record** reflects any early postseason results, but Duke's elite regular season suggests they enter March in peak form.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n**Elite Perimeter Defense**: Duke's 264 steals and team steal rate of 7.80 per game[4] creates chaos for offenses. Tournament teams relying on ball movement and pace-and-space offense will struggle against this pressure.\n\n**Rebounding Dominance**: The +366 rebound margin over opponents is massive. Duke's ability to control the glass—particularly with Cameron Boozer's 114 offensive rebounds—provides second-chance opportunities and limits opponent possessions.\n\n**Defensive Efficiency (79.9 adjusted)**: This elite mark suggests Duke limits three-point shooting, controls the paint, and forces difficult looks. Tournament opponents will find scoring in bunches extremely difficult.\n\n**Paint Protection**: Patrick Ngongba II (6'11\", 250 lbs) provides 32 blocks in limited minutes (651 minutes), establishing shot-blocking deterrence. Maliq Brown adds versatility with 19 blocks as well[4].\n\n**ACC Pedigree**: A 17-1 conference record against tournament-caliber opponents provides invaluable tournament preparation and confidence.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n**Offensive Inconsistency**: The 100.6 adjusted offensive efficiency, while respectable, lacks explosive upside. Duke struggles to generate dominant offensive performances, relying on grinding possessions rather than overwhelming scoring.\n\n**Three-Point Shooting Limitations**: At 35.1% from three on 881 attempts (309 makes)[1], Duke's perimeter shooting is adequate but not dangerous. Teams with elite perimeter defense can neutralize Duke's spacing.\n\n**Limited Wing Scoring**: Beyond Cameron Boozer, Duke lacks a true star scorer on the wings. Isaiah Evans' 310 points and Dame Sarr's 262 points indicate role-player contributions rather than All-American production. Tournament teams with elite perimeter defenders can shut down Duke's secondary scoring.\n\n**Turnover Vulnerabilities**: Despite 576 team assists, Duke has 358 turnovers[1], indicating offensive stagnation in certain stretches. Aggressive defensive opponents can force chaos.\n\n**Freshman Depth**: Multiple freshmen (Cameron Boozer, Dame Sarr, Nikolas Khamenia, Sebastian Wilkins) lack tournament experience[2][3]. Pressure situations may expose youth.\n\n---\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\n**Coaching Experience**: Jon Scheyer is in his **4th season** as Duke's head coach[3]. While this represents growing tournament experience, it's not the decades of March success some programs possess. Scheyer's 2024-25 team likely provided tournament blueprints.\n\n**Injury Status**: The search results do not indicate significant injuries to key players, suggesting Duke enters the tournament at full health—a critical advantage.\n\n**Program History**: Duke basketball carries elite NCAA Tournament pedigree, though specific 2025-26 tournament results are unavailable in current data. The program's recruiting excellence and ACC dominance position them well for tournament runs.\n\n**Seeding Analysis**: As a **#3 seed**, Duke is positioned as a regional favorite but not a Final Four lock. The tournament seeding suggests other selection committee consensus that Duke is dangerous but not a national championship favorite.\n\n---\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Cameron Boozer's Foul Trouble** — The freshman's aggressive rebounding style and physical defense (50 steals, 18 blocks) makes him essential to Duke's identity, but tournament officiating and elite offensive players may target him defensively. If Boozer fouls out early in tournament games, Duke loses its most versatile big man and rim-running partner for Caleb Foster's pick-and-roll offense. Conversely, if Boozer maintains discipline while staying aggressive, Duke's paint control becomes nearly impenetrable. His",
