      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nOle Miss enters the tournament led by **Aj Storr** (G, 15.5 PPG), a dynamic scorer who averages 15.5 points per game on 44.8% field goal shooting and 35.0% from three, making him dangerous with his quick release and ability to create off the dribble in transition[3]. **Malik Dia** (F, 14.5 PPG) dominates inside-out with 14.5 points and 5.7 rebounds per game, shooting 45.2% from the field and providing elite rim protection with 29 blocks, excelling in pick-and-roll situations[3]. **James Scott** (F/C, 3.9 PPG but 4.6 RPG) is the defensive anchor, averaging 46 blocks on the season while grabbing 4.6 rebounds in just 18.1 minutes per game on 72.9% shooting, lethal as a shot-blocker who alters drives and cleans the glass[3].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nOle Miss plays at a deliberate **tempo** around 68 possessions per game, emphasizing a **defensive philosophy** of physical, switchable man-to-man with elite rim protection (150 team blocks)[3]. Their **offensive scheme** relies on high-efficiency inside scoring (44.6% FG) and balanced three-point shooting (33.2%, 249 makes), but they struggle with volume from deep (751 attempts)[3]. **Rebounding strength** is a hallmark, with 34.3 total rebounds per game led by Dia and Scott's interior presence[3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month leading to Selection Sunday, Ole Miss went 7-3, including a four-game winning streak snapped by a loss to a top-10 SEC foe, showcasing improved defensive clampdowns in wins over mid-tier conference teams[3]. They advanced to the **SEC Tournament semifinals**, upsetting a #3 seed before falling in a close contest, boosting confidence with back-to-back double-digit victories to close the regular season[3].\n\n## Strengths\n\nOle Miss excels in **defensive efficiency** (84.1 adjusted), suffocating opponents with length and shot-blocking (248 team steals and blocks combined), forcing turnovers and second-chance prevention[3]. **Matchups favoring them** include teams reliant on perimeter scoring or lacking size, where Dia and Scott feast on lobs and rebounds while Storr exploits switches[3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable on the perimeter against elite guard play, as their three-point defense allows 33.2% opponent shooting, and they rank outside the top 200 in effective FG% defense[3][7]. **Teams that give them trouble** are high-tempo, three-point bombing squads that stretch their switches and exploit Scott's limited offensive range (0% from three)[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major **injuries** reported, with full health for Storr, Dia, and Scott entering the Sacramento 2 Region[3]. Coach Chris Beard brings proven **tournament experience**, with multiple Sweet 16 runs and a Final Four at Texas[3]. Historically, Ole Miss has limited **March Madness success**, with just one Elite Eight appearance (2008) and frequent early exits as a mid-major SEC threat[3].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Aj Storr's three-point volume**—if he sustains 35.0% on high attempts against tournament defenses, Ole Miss can upset; cold shooting strands their interior duo[3].",
