      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nBaylor's top three players anchor a versatile lineup blending experience and athleticism. **Dan Skillings Jr. (G, 6'6\", Sr.)** leads the team at 18.2 points per game, dangerous with his explosive drives, rebounding prowess (7.1 RPG), and improved 36% three-point shooting on high volume[1][3][5]. **JJ White (G, 6'1\", Sr.)** averages 15.4 points and 4.2 assists, excelling as a quick guard who penetrates defenses and hits 42% from deep, making him lethal in pick-and-rolls[1][3][5]. **James Nnaji (C, 7'0\", Fr.)** contributes 12.8 points and 9.5 rebounds, a rim-protecting force with elite shot-blocking (2.3 BPG) and lob-finishing ability that dominates the paint[1][3][5][6].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nBaylor plays at a **controlled tempo** (68.5 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under Coach Scott Drew that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent TO rate) and elite paint protection. Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy ball movement, ranking top-20 in assists (15.8 APG), while relying on **above-average three-point shooting** (37.2%, 11.2 makes per game) and **dominant rebounding** (39.2 RPG, +5.1 margin) led by their bigs[1][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Baylor went 8-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **Big 12 Tournament semifinal win over Kansas (78-72)** before falling to Houston in the final (65-71). They averaged 82.1 points while holding foes to 64.3, showcasing defensive dominance in quad-1 wins over Arizona and Iowa State[1].\n\n## Strengths\n\nBaylor excels in **elite defense** (80.8 AdjDEF, No. 8 nationally) with length and switchability, smothering mid-majors and slowing high-tempo teams. **Rebounding and second-chance points** (14.2 per game) favor them against smaller lineups, while their **balanced scoring** (seven players in double figures) wears down defenses. Matchups against guard-heavy or undersized teams like midseeds in the Sacramento 2 region play to their size advantage[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **perimeter-oriented teams** that stretch their bigs, as they allow 35.1% opponent threes and struggle with off-dribble shooting (1.12 PPP allowed). **Turnover issues** (14.8% rate) and occasional **free-throw woes** (68.2%) get exploited by disciplined, physical squads like those with strong half-court execution, such as Big 12 rivals or methodical mid-majors[1].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full health with Nnaji and Skillings Jr. at 100%. **Coach Scott Drew** brings proven tournament experience, including the 2021 national title and 8 Sweet 16s in 20 years. Baylor's March Madness history shines with a **.682 winning percentage** (47-22), three Final Fours, and consistent deep runs as a Big 12 power[1][6].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Freshman center James Nnaji's foul trouble**—averaging 3.2 fouls but dominating when on the floor—could swing paint control against region foes with skilled bigs, determining if Baylor advances past the second round.",
