      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Christian Coleman (G, 6-8, Sr)** averages 17.2 points per game, making him dangerous with his size advantage as a wing scorer who exploits mismatches through mid-range pull-ups and transition finishes.[1][4]  \n**Kanye Clary (G, 6-0, Jr)** leads the backcourt at 15.8 points and 4.2 assists per game, thriving as a quick first-step creator who pressures defenses full-court and hits 38% from three.[1][4]  \n**Parsa Fallah (F, 6-10, Sr)** contributes 12.4 points and 8.1 rebounds per game, dangerous inside with his soft touch around the rim and ability to stretch the floor at 35% from deep despite his size.[1][4]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nOklahoma State plays at a deliberate tempo (68.2 possessions per game), prioritizing a pack-line defensive philosophy that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and limits paint scoring.[1] Offensively, they run a gap-drive scheme emphasizing ball movement and cutting, with moderate three-point volume (34.2 attempts per game at 36.1%) but elite rebounding strength (39.8 rebounds per game, top-20 nationally).[1]\n\n## Recent Form\n\nIn the last month (February 17-March 17), Oklahoma State went 7-3, including a four-game winning streak to close the regular season before a semifinal loss in the Big 12 Tournament (lost 72-68 to #4 Kansas).[1][5] They beat #12 TCU and #9 Texas Tech in the conference quarters and semis but faltered against elite perimeter shooting.[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nThey excel in defensive efficiency (87.4 adjusted) by clogging driving lanes and crashing the glass offensively, winning rebounding battles by +4.2 per game.[1] Matchups favor them against fast-paced, guard-heavy teams lacking size, where Fallah and Coleman dominate the boards and Clary disrupts in transition.[1][4]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to teams with superior perimeter shooting and ball-handling (shot 31% from three in losses to top-25 foes), as their smaller guards like Clary (6-0) struggle against length.[1] Physical, half-court offenses with strong bigs expose their thin frontcourt depth beyond Fallah.[1][4]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported, though Parsa Fallah (knee) and Robert Jennings II (hip) are questionable but played limited minutes recently.[1] Coach Steve Lutz (2nd season) has solid tournament experience, reaching the NIT second round last year; assistants like Keiton Page add Big 12 pedigree.[5] Historically, Oklahoma State has 6 Sweet 16s and a 1988 Elite Eight but no Final Four, with a 22-20 record since 2010.[5]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Christian Coleman's three-point shooting**: If he sustains 37% from deep (on 5.2 attempts), it spaces the floor for Clary's drives; cold nights (under 30%) have doomed their losses against balanced defenses.[1][4]",
