      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Jackson Hicke (G)**: Leading scorer at 13.6 PPG and team assists leader at 2.5 APG, Hicke is dangerous as a versatile guard who creates off the dribble, hits pull-up jumpers, and exploits defensive closeouts with his vision and speed[2].\n\n**Malik Abdullahi (F)**: Rebounding machine at 5.9 RPG, Abdullahi threatens with his 6'7\" frame, finishing through contact on cuts and second-chance opportunities while stretching the floor with mid-range pull-ups[1][2].\n\n**CJ Happy (F)**: Block leader at 0.5 BPG with 6'9\" length, Happy is a mismatch nightmare, using his size for rim protection, offensive boards, and face-up drives that punish slower bigs[1][2].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nPrinceton plays at a deliberate tempo, prioritizing **elite defensive discipline** with a pack-line philosophy that funnels drivers into help rotations, holding opponents to low-efficiency shots (AdjDE 87.2)[query]. Offensively, they run a motion scheme emphasizing ball movement, **high-volume three-point shooting** (key to their 105.4 AdjOE), and cutting, while ranking mid-tier in rebounding by crashing from multiple positions[1][2][3].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month, Princeton has surged with a 10-game winning streak, including a dominant Ivy Tournament run where they upset top-seeded Yale in the semis (78-65) and crushed Harvard in the final (82-70) to claim the auto-bid despite a middling 5-9 regular-season conference mark[3][4]. This hot streak has propelled their overall 26-3 record, outscoring foes by 15+ PPG in tournament play[query].\n\n## Strengths\n\nPrinceton excels in **defensive efficiency** (87.2 AdjDE), suffocating opponents with length and physicality to force turnovers and limit second chances, ideal against inefficient or isolation-heavy teams[query][2]. They thrive in **matchups favoring deliberate pace**, like grinding down fast-break reliant mid-majors or teams weak on perimeter D, where their three-point volume (led by Hicke) creates explosive leads[1][3].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **athletic, switchable lineups** that disrupt their motion offense with length and quickness, exposing occasional half-court stagnation if threes go cold[2]. Rebounding depth falters against dominant interior teams, as their forwards get pushed around on the glass, leading to transition leaks[1][query].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster healthy entering the #9 seed spot in Sacramento 2 region[1]. Coach Mitch Henderson (14th season) brings solid tournament experience, with Princeton reaching the Sweet 16 as an 8-seed in 2023; program history includes four Ivy titles since 2022 but just one NCAA win in the last decade prior to recent bids[4]. As a 26-3 squad, they match well against typical #8 seeds in controlled environments.\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Jackson Hicke's three-point volume**: If Hicke sustains 40%+ from deep on 6+ attempts (his season norm), Princeton's spacing overwhelms, fueling upsets; cold nights cripple their half-court creation[2].",
