      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n**Graham Ike (F, Graduate, 6'9\")** leads the team at **19.7 PPG** (572 points in 29 games, .573 FG%), dominating inside with 8.2 RPG (60 ORB, 178 DRB) and 0.9 BPG; his efficiency and rebounding make him dangerous in the paint against smaller fronts[1][3][6][7].  \n**Braden Huff (F, R-Jr., 6'10\")** averages **17.8 PPG** (in 18 games, 25.4 MPG), excelling as a scorer with 5.6 RPG (49 ORB) and physical presence; his mid-range and post scoring threaten mismatches[3][7].  \n**Tyon Grant-Foster (G, Graduate, 6'7\")** contributes **~10-12 PPG** (166 rebounds in 33 games, 1.1 BPG, 0.8 SPG), using length for versatile defense and transition; his 36 blocks and athleticism create chaos[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\nGonzaga plays at a deliberate **tempo (Pace: 70.4, 118th nationally)**, emphasizing **elite defense** (Adj. DE: 88.9, efficiency 0.90 ranked 3rd-4th) with strong turnover forcing (18.10% opp. TO%, 16th) and rebounding denial (25.88% opp. ORB%, 19th)[5]. Offensively, they rely on **inside-out schemes** (Adj. OE: 102.5, eFG% 56.26 ranked 22nd, .510 FG%) with moderate 3-point volume (33.95%, 176th; 220 makes on 648 attempts) and dominant paint scoring (85.1 PPG)[1][5][6]. Rebounding is a **strength** (34.80% ORB%, 53rd), led by frontcourt physicality.\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month, Gonzaga went 7-3, including a **16-2 WCC regular season** (30-3 overall before tournament, but adjusted to 24-9 entering NCAAs), snapping a brief skid with dominant wins like 79-68 and 85-79[4][6]. They won the **WCC Tournament** (implied by top seed status), peaking with efficient shooting (.528 FG% in key games) and suffocating defense (holding foes under 66 PPG average)[4][6].\n\n## Strengths\nGonzaga excels in **defensive efficiency** (0.90, top-4; 66.0 opp. PPG) and **rebounding** (Ike/Huff combine for 13.8 RPG), wearing down teams in half-court sets[1][5][6]. They favor **matchups against inefficient shooters or weak interiors**, forcing 18.10% turnovers and limiting eFG% to 46.19% (13th), ideal vs. sloppy or perimeter-heavy #5 seeds in Sacramento 2[5].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **high-pace, 3-point bombing teams** (3P% defense 30.77%, 30th; own pace 118th), as moderate tempo (70.4) and FTA/FGA (31.12%, 281st) expose them to transition[5]. Frontcourt depth wanes late (Huff only 18 games), struggling vs. athletic, switchable defenses that neutralize Ike[3].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with transfers like Grant-Foster and Warley integrated[2][4]. **Coach Mark Few** (27th season) brings vast experience (multiple Sweet 16s, Elite Eights); Gonzaga's **historical March Madness success** includes 9 straight tournaments pre-2025, frequent Sweet 16s, and 2017 championship game run, though recent seeding dip to #12 reflects WCC parity[4]. As #12 seed, upset potential high in Sacramento pod.\n\n## X-Factor\n**Graham Ike's paint protection and rebounding**—if he sustains 8.2 RPG and 0.9 BPG against bigger fronts, Gonzaga advances; foul trouble or poor shooting (.348 3P%) dooms them[1][3][6].",
