      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\nThe top three players for College of Charleston are **Mister Dean** (6-6 guard, sophomore), **Colby Duggan** (6-7 forward, junior), and **Christian Reeves** (7-2 center, junior). Dean, a transfer from USC Upstate, leads scoring at 18.2 points per game, dangerous due to his versatility as a tall guard who shoots 41% from three and attacks seams in pick-and-rolls[1][3][6]. Duggan averages 15.7 points and 8.1 rebounds, excelling as a stretch-four with 38% three-point shooting and physicality in the post[1][3][6]. Reeves dominates inside at 12.4 points and 9.8 rebounds per game, a rim protector with 2.3 blocks who feasts on lobs and offensive boards[1][3].\n\n## Team Identity\nCharleston plays at a **mid-tempo pace** (68.2 possessions per game), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** under Chris Mack that forces turnovers (18.2% opponent turnover rate) and suffocates drives. Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point shooting** (39.1% on 24.5 attempts per game), supported by strong **rebounding** (38.2 boards per game, top in CAA) from their size advantage with two 7-footers[1].\n\n## Recent Form\nOver the last month (February 17–March 17, 2026), Charleston went 8-1, including a **six-game winning streak** capped by a CAA Tournament championship win over Hofstra (78-62 in the final). They averaged 82.3 points while holding foes to 64.1, with standout wins over UNC Wilmington (85-71) and Towson (92-55)[1].\n\n## Strengths\nCharleston's elite **defensive efficiency (83.7)** shines against slower, perimeter-oriented teams, where their length disrupts passing lanes and they rank top-20 nationally in steal rate (12.1%). They favor matchups with poor rebounding or weak interior defense, dominating the glass (+6.2 rebound margin) and scoring 1.18 points per possession in transition[1].\n\n## Weaknesses\nVulnerable to **up-tempo, athletic teams** that push pace and attack their thin backcourt (opponents score 1.05 PPP in transition), as their guards lack elite quickness. Free-throw defense is poor (opponents 78.2% FT), and they struggle if Reeves is in foul trouble, dropping rebounding by 15%[1].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\nNo major injuries reported; full health entering as #14 seed in Sacramento 2 region. Coach **Chris Mack** brings deep tournament experience (8-6 NCAA record at Louisville/Xavier, multiple Sweet 16s). Program history is limited (2-7 all-time in March Madness, first since 2018 Elite Eight run), but this 25-5 squad matches their best since[1][3].\n\n## X-Factor\n**Christian Reeves' foul trouble**: If he stays out of foul trouble (averaged 3.2 fouls per game late-season), Charleston's interior dominance overwhelms mid-majors; early exits expose their frontcourt depth issues[1][3].",
