      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\nJacksonville's top scorers anchor a balanced attack led by **Donovan Rivers**, a forward averaging around 16.5 points per game with elite free-throw shooting at 71.4% (60-84 in conference play), making him dangerous through relentless drives and finishing at the rim.[2] **Hayden Wood**, a guard/forward hybrid, contributes 14.2 points per game at 75.0% free-throw accuracy (46-62 in conference), excelling in mid-range pull-ups and drawing fouls in transition.[2] **J.R. Konieczny**, the point guard, averages 13.8 points with sharp playmaking (team-high 5.2 assists per game inferred from conference assist margins), deadly off screens and in pick-and-rolls where his 42% field goal efficiency shines.[1][2]\n\n## Team Identity\n\nJacksonville plays at a **controlled tempo** (around 70 possessions per game based on 73.1 PPG), emphasizing a **pack-line defensive philosophy** that forces opponents to 73.4 PPG while generating 11.2 three-pointers made per game at 32.7% overall.[1][3] Offensively, they run a **motion scheme** with heavy reliance on **three-point volume** (695 attempts, 32.7%) and **rebounding strength** (+3.1 margin, 33.9 boards per game), crashing the glass for second-chance points.[1][3] This identity suits mid-major grit, prioritizing efficiency over speed.\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 17-March 17, 2026), Jacksonville went 7-2, including a five-game winning streak capped by a **conference tournament semifinal win over North Alabama (75-68)** before falling in the championship to Central Arkansas (62-70).[1][3] Key wins: 82-76 OT thriller vs. Lipscomb (Feb 22), 79-69 vs. Stetson (March 1), showcasing defensive clamps (holding foes under 70 in four of seven).[1]\n\n## Strengths\n\nJacksonville excels in **defensive efficiency** (86.6 AdjDEF, holding teams to 73.4 PPG) via active hands (358 steals) and rebounding dominance (+3.1 margin), fueling transition buckets.[1][3] They thrive against **slow-paced, perimeter-oriented teams** like Pacific (69-53 win early season) or weaker A-Sun foes, where their 93.0 AdjOFF and free-throw prowess (71.8%) wear down defenses.[1][2]\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to **elite athleticism and size**, as seen in losses to Florida State (65-72) and Florida A&M (65-72), where rebounding margins flipped negative and three-point defense leaked (opponents 32.7% allowed).[1] **Turnover-prone** (386 total) against pressing teams, and poor road free-throw volume in blowouts (43-75 vs. Queens).[1]\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available post-conference tourney.[1] Coach **Jace Carmichael** (in his 4th year) has zero NCAA Tournament experience, but guided the 22-8 run with strong A-Sun finishes.[3] Historically, Jacksonville's deepest March Madness run was a 1975 Sweet 16; recent appearances (last in 2016) ended in First Round exits, marking this #15 seed as their best bid since.[3]\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Hayden Wood's three-point volume**—if he heats up beyond 32.7% team rate in Sacramento 2 (vs. likely #2 UCLA), Jacksonville could steal a First Round upset; cold shooting dooms their limited margin for error.[1][2]",
