      "perplexityContext": "## Key Players\n\n**Dominique Daniels Jr. (G, Sr., 5-10)** leads the team with 20.5 points per game (PPG), excelling as a quick, shifty point guard who penetrates defenses and draws fouls, averaging 5.2 assists while shooting 82% from the free-throw line. His danger lies in explosive first-step drives and clutch scoring, as seen in his 25-point outing at Colorado and 22 points in the WAC tournament win over Abilene Christian[1][2][5].\n\n**Miles Daniels (G, Sr., 6-2)** contributes 14.8 PPG as a combo guard, dangerous for his perimeter shooting (38% from three) and ability to create off the dribble, often stretching defenses with pull-up jumpers in transition[1][2].\n\n**Bradey Henige (F, Soph., 7-0, 240 lbs)** anchors the frontcourt with 11.2 PPG and 8.5 rebounds per game (RPG), using his size for dominant rebounding (leading with 8 boards vs. Oregon State) and rim protection, making him a mismatch nightmare against smaller bigs[1][2][5].\n\n## Team Identity\n\nCal Baptist plays at a controlled tempo (around 68 possessions per game), emphasizing a pack-line defensive philosophy that forces turnovers and limits paint scoring, as evidenced by their elite adjusted defensive efficiency of 86.1. Offensively, they run a motion scheme focused on inside-out ball movement, ranking high in WAC rebounding strength with bigs like Henige and Nate Garcia (7-0 Fr.) cleaning the glass, while attempting 22 threes per game at 35% clip to keep defenses honest[5].\n\n## Recent Form\n\nOver the last month (February 7 to March 5), Cal Baptist went 4-2, including a crucial 65-63 win at Abilene Christian (Feb 7) and a dominant 87-48 rout of the same foe in the WAC tournament quarterfinals (March 5, 22 points from Daniels Jr.). They snapped a brief skid with a 46-65 loss at Utah Valley (Feb 19) but enter the NCAA Tournament on a two-game winning streak after conference play, boosting their 23-10 record[5].\n\n## Strengths\n\nCal Baptist excels in defensive rebounding (No. 1 in WAC) and rim protection from their 7-footers (Henige, Garcia), allowing them to control tempo and convert stops into transition buckets. They thrive in matchups against guard-heavy or slow-paced teams, where Daniels Jr.'s penetration and their 94.5 adjusted offensive efficiency shine, as in their 75-69 upset at Oregon State[5].\n\n## Weaknesses\n\nVulnerable to athletic wings who exploit their smaller guards (Daniels Jr. at 5-10), leading to poor perimeter defense (opponents shoot 36% from three lately), and they struggle against high-tempo, three-point bombing teams that stretch their bigs. Poor free-throw defense (ranked bottom-third in WAC) and occasional offensive droughts (e.g., 46 points at Utah Valley) expose them[5].\n\n## Tournament Outlook\n\nNo major injuries reported; full roster available with freshmen like Nate Garcia stepping up. Head coach has limited NCAA experience (first tournament appearance), but the program has zero historical March Madness success as a Division I member since 2018, marking this #16 seed in Sacramento 2 as uncharted territory—expect a classic First Four or play-in battle[1][2][5].\n\n## X-Factor\n\n**Dominique Daniels Jr.'s ability to dictate tempo**: If he avoids turnovers (averages 3.1) and exploits mismatches for 20+ points, Cal Baptist can upset; if smothered by longer guards, their offense stalls fatally.",
